INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(2854,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10788761','Impact of COVID-19 on cataract surgical competency of ophthalmology residents.','Ghiasian L, Aghili S, Abdolalizadeh P.','2023','phaco capsulorrhexis phaco uncooperative tenon epibulbar capsulorhexis cystotome infiniti infiniti alcon stellaris bausch lomb phaco locs subcapsular symptomatically phaco viscoelastic capsulorhexis capsulorhexis hydrodissection sculpting cracking phaco phaco infiniti stellaris cracking capsulorrhexis viscoelastic viscoelastic capsulorrhexis capsulorrhexis hydrodissection sculpting cracking chopping fluidity alahmadi amparore verge capsulorhexis phaco sculpting chopping capsulorhexis capsulorhexis steeper capsulorhexis capsulorhexis phaco capsulorhexis capsulorhexis gervasio e000525 bmjophth alahmadi alhatlan helayel khandekar habash shahwan opth s283073 tzoumas boote dhillon cackett iovino ehc ophthatraining konopinska obuchowska lisowski dub dmuchowska rekas e0257876 gogate bhushan ijo_1067_20 laiginhas meireles breda remulla sher genyk nally doleman giwa zrab051 bjsopen zrab051 golnik gauba mayorga palis chylack leske bullimore szigiato palakkamanil aubin ziai e42 jcjo saied hma salah eddin abdelhakim cairo chernew linetsky commonwealthfund budden rannard mennie bulstrode amparore checcucci serni minervini gacci esperto esru dooley refract jcrs yulan yaohua jinhua oetting 0b013e328319914f randleman cherwek jhanji titiyal vajpayee phacoemulsifiation i09 hollick refract jcrs youngson s35','Abstract Context: Coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) pandemic led to a decrease in elective surgeries. Purpose: To compare the performance of postgraduate year 4 prepandemic (2018–2019) and pandemic (2021–2022) ophthalmology residents. Settings and Design: This was an observational comparative study conducted at a university-based hospital. Methods: Ophthalmology residents of postgraduate year 4 during prepandemic (2018–2019) and pandemic (2021–2022) time periods were included. The performances of residents in phacoemulsification were scored by a single faculty assessor using the International Council of Ophthalmology''s Ophthalmology Surgical Competency Assessment Rubric for phacoemulsification (ICO-OSCAR: phaco). The scores were compared between the two groups. Statistical Analysis Used: The comparisons between the pre- and post-pandemic groups were performed using the Chi-square and Mann–Whitney U test. Results: Seventy-one surgeries were rated in each group. All task-specific and global scores of the pandemic group were lower than those of the prepandemic group (all P values < 0.001). The maximum intergroup difference was in capsulorrhexis circular completion (difference of 1.21 score, P < 0.001) and tissue handling (difference of 1.21 score, P < 0.001) among task-specific and global scores, respectively. The residents of the two groups had the highest similarity in irrigation and aspiration (difference of 0.61 score, P < 0.001) from task-specific scores and central eye position from global scores (difference of 0.93 score, P < 0.001). Conclusion: The COVID-19 pandemic has negatively affected the surgical competency of ophthalmology residents in phacoemulsification. Keywords: Cataract, COVID-19 pandemic, ophthalmic surgical training, phacoemulsification, residency According to the Center for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC), hospitals postponed routine services such as outpatient clinics and elective surgical procedures to reduce coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) transmission risk and conserve health-care resources. Similarly, international ophthalmic societies also recommended to keep running only urgent and emergency services during the pandemic.[ 1 ] The pandemic-induced limitations of routine practice have disrupted residents'' training, which is more evident in surgical subspecialties such as ophthalmology.[ 2 ] Although greater availability of online educational options provides sufficient platform for the theoretical training of residents and fellows, there was a significant decrease in the number of hours spent in surgical training during the pandemic.[ 3 ] There has been limited research on the effects of the COVID-19 pandemic on ophthalmology residency training thus far. A majority of studies used questionnaires to assess the residents'' attitude about the training programs, while others compared the number of surgeries before and after the pandemic.[ 2 , 4 , 5 , 6 , 7 , 8 , 9 , 10 ] Cataract surgery might be the most restricted form of elective ophthalmology surgery during the pandemic. Previous questionnaire-based studies reported that more than half of the residents have significant concerns about their training in cataract surgery.[ 4 , 7 ] The volume-based studies also reported a significant drop in the number of cataract surgeries performed by residents during the pandemic.[ 6 , 8 ] However, no study has considered the competency-based evaluation of ophthalmology trainees. Therefore, the aim of this study was to compare the ophthalmology residents'' performance in phacoemulsification before and during COVID-19 pandemic. Methods This is a retrospective cross-sectional study. Institutional review board approved the study. This study adhered to the tenets of the Declaration of Helsinki, and informed consent was obtained from the subjects after they were explained the nature and possible consequences of the study. Six ophthalmology residents between April 2018 and January 2019 (prepandemic group) and 11 residents between April 2021 and January 2022 (pandemic group) participated in the study. All residents were in postgraduate year 4 at a university-based hospital and they had performed at least 15 phacoemulsification surgeries before enrollment into the study. The resident performance was rated by a faculty member (LG, an anterior segment specialist) utilizing the International Council of Ophthalmology''s Ophthalmology Surgical Competency Assessment Rubric for phacoemulsification (ICO-OSCAR: phaco).[ 11 ] The rater directly visualized the surgery through the side glasses of the microscope. The residents were blinded about the study, rating process, and their scores. All eyes had uncomplicated phacoemulsification with 2.8-mm clear corneal incision under topical or general anesthesia. General anesthesia was preferred during the pandemic period and lockdown condition because the number of elective cataract surgeries was significantly reduced. By general anesthesia, uncooperative patients were also used for residential education with longer time of surgery. No sub-tenon or epibulbar augmentation was performed. The capsulorhexis was performed by cystotome using an insulin needle. The nucleus was removed by phacoemulsification using Infiniti (Infiniti Vision System; Alcon, Fort Worth, TX, USA) or Stellaris (Bausch and Lomb, Rochester, NY, USA) phaco machines. All cases were simple, moderate to severe cataracts without any other ocular disease. Preoperatively, one faculty member (LG, an anterior segment specialist) determined the severity of nuclear cataracts ranging from grade 2 to 4 based on lens opacities classification system (LOCS) III[ 12 ] with/without posterior subcapsular or cortical cataract. The best corrected visual acuity worse than 20/40 to 20/50 was a prerequisite for cataract surgery, along with symptomatically decreased vision. The ICO-OSCAR: phaco assessment tool consists of a six-item global rating system (wound neutrality, eye positioned centrally, tissue handling, intraocular special awareness, iris protection, and overall speed) and a 14-item task-specific checklist (draping, incision, viscoelastic, capsulorhexis flap formation, capsulorhexis circular completion, hydrodissection, instrument insertion into the eye, the stability of the instrument, sculpting, nucleus rotation, nucleus cracking, irrigation, lens insertion, wound closure) consisting of breakdown of cataract surgery. Each global and task-specific component is rated as a novice (score 2), beginner (score 3), advanced beginner (score 4), and competent (score 5). Statistical analysis Data were entered using Statistical Package for the Social Sciences (SPSS) for Windows software version 22.0 (SPSS Inc., Chicago IL, USA). The Shapiro–Wilk test was used to test the normality. The comparisons between the pre- and post-pandemic groups were performed using Chi-Square and Mann–Whitney U test. P value of less than 0.05 was considered statistically significant. Results Totally, 142 cataract surgeries were rated, including 71 surgeries in the prepandemic and 71 surgeries in the pandemic group. More than 80% of surgeries were performed under general anesthesia in the pandemic group, while topical anesthesia was the most preferred type of anesthesia in the prepandemic group ( P < 0.001) [ Table 1 ]. The two groups were similar in terms of phaco machine ( P = 0.60) and surgery time ( P = 0.29) [ Table 1 ]. Table 1. Basic characteristics of phacoemulsification surgeries at the prepandemic period and during COVID-19 pandemic Prepandemic Pandemic P a Surgery time Early morning (8–11 am) 43.7% (31/71) 31.0% (22/71) 0.29 Mid-day (11–15) 40.8% (29/71) 50.7% (36/71) Afternoon (15–19) 15.5% (11/71) 18.3% (13/71) Phaco machine Infiniti 64.8% (46/71) 60.6% (43/71) 0.60 Stellaris 35.2% (25/71) 39.4% (28/71) Anesthesia Topical 90.1% (64/71) 16.9% (12/71) <0.001 General 9.9% (7/71) 83.1% (59/71) Open in a new tab a Chi-square test. COVID-19=coronavirus disease 2019 The pandemic and prepandemic groups had a mean total task-specific score of 49.69 ± 6.52 and 62.58 ± 4.01, respectively ( P < 0.001). Senior residents achieved the lowest score for nucleus cracking in both the pandemic and prepandemic groups [ Table 2 ]. All task-specific scores of the pandemic group were lower than those of the prepandemic group (all P values < 0.001) [ Table 2 ]. The two groups had the maximum difference in capsulorrhexis circular completion (1.21 score, P < 0.001), followed by probe and second instrument insertion (1.20 score, P < 0.001), effective use and stability of instrument (1.12 score, P < 0.001), and viscoelastic (1.11 score, P < 0.001). The least intergroup differences were in irrigation and aspiration (0.61 score, P < 0.001) and draping (0.73 score, P < 0.001). Table 2. Task-specific scores of senior residents in phacoemulsification at the prepandemic period and during COVID-19 pandemic Prepandemic Mean±SD Median (IQR) (min, max) Pandemic Mean±SD Median (IQR) (min, max) P a Draping 4.41±0.71 3.68±0.58 <0.001 5 (4, 5) (3, 5) 4 (3, 4) (3, 5) Incision and paracentesis 4.61±0.60 3.62±0.66 <0.001 5 (4, 5) (3, 5) 4 (3, 4) (3, 5) Viscoelastic 4.70±0.52 3.59±0.69 <0.001 5 (4, 5) (3, 5) 4 (3, 4) (2, 5) Capsulorrhexis commencement of flap 4.49±0.56 3.51±0.81 <0.001 5 (4, 5) (3, 5) 3 (3, 4) (2, 5) Capsulorrhexis circular completion 4.57±0.65 3.36±0.68 <0.001 5 (4, 5) (3, 5) 3 (3, 4) (2, 5) Hydrodissection 4.58±0.63 3.54±0.70 <0.001 5 (4, 5) (3, 5) 4 (3, 4) (2, 5) Phacoemulsification/probe and second instrument insertion into eye 4.89±0.36 3.70±0.71 <0.001 5 (5, 5) (3, 5) 4 (3, 4) (2, 5) Phacoemulsification/effective use and stability of instrument 4.48±0.59 3.36±0.62 <0.001 5 (4, 5) (3, 5) 3 (3, 4) (2, 5) Sculpting or primary chop of nucleus 4.34±0.71 3.28±0.74 <0.001 4 (4, 5) (3, 5) 3 (3, 4) (2, 5) Nucleus rotation and manipulation 4.14±0.52 3.36±0.79 <0.001 4 (4, 4) (3, 5) 3 (3, 4) (2, 5) Nucleus cracking or chopping with safe phacoemulsification of segments 3.97±0.60 3.22±0.69 <0.001 4 (4, 4) (3, 5) 3 (3, 4) (2, 5) Irrigation and aspiration 4.22±0.55 3.61±0.63 <0.001 4 (4, 5) (3, 5) 4 (3, 4) (2, 5) Lens insertion 4.48±0.70 3.54±0.61 <0.001 5 (4, 5) (3, 5) 3 (3, 4) (3, 5) Wound closure 4.50±0.67 3.69±0.71 <0.001 5 (4, 5) (3, 5) 4 (3, 4) (2, 5) Total task-specific score 62.58±4.01 49.69±6.52 <0.001 63 (60, 65) (51, 70) 51 (46.5, 54) (32, 64) Open in a new tab a Mann–Whitney U test. COVID-19=coronavirus disease 2019, IQR=interquartile range, SD=standard deviation The mean total global score was 26.71 ± 2.40 in the prepandemic group and 20.46 ± 3.21 in the pandemic group ( P < 0.001). The lowest and highest global scores were in central eye position and iris protection, respectively, in both groups [ Table 3 ]. All global scores of residents in the pandemic group were lower than those of the prepandemic group (all P values < 0.001) [ Table 3 ]. The pandemic and prepandemic groups had the maximum difference in tissue handling (1.21 score, P < 0.001), followed by iris protection (1.15 score, P < 0.001), intraocular spatial awareness (1.04 score, P < 0.001), overall speed (0.95 score, P < 0.001), and wound neutrality (0.94 score, P < 0.001). The least intergroup difference was in central eye position (0.93 score, P < 0.001). Table 3. Global scores of senior residents in phacoemulsification at the prepandemic period and during COVID-19 pandemic Prepandemic Mean±SD Median (IQR) (min, max) Pandemic Mean±SD Median (IQR) (min, max) P a Wound neutrality and minimizing eye rolling and corneal distortion 4.25±0.60 3.31±0.75 <0.001 5 (4, 5) (3, 5) 3 (3, 4) (2, 4) Eye positioned centrally within microscope view 4.14±0.69 3.21±0.71 <0.001 5 (4, 5) (3, 5) 3 (3, 4) (2, 4) Conjunctival and corneal tissue handling 4.59±0.58 3.38±0.59 <0.001 5 (4, 5) (3, 5) 3 (3, 4) (2, 5) Intraocular spatial awareness 4.58±0.46 3.54±0.73 <0.001 5 (4, 5) (3, 5) 4 (3, 4) (2, 5) Iris protection 4.75±0.53 3.59±0.67 <0.001 5 (5, 5) (3, 5) 4 (3, 4) (2, 5) Overall speed and fluidity of procedure 4.39±0.58 3.44±0.60 <0.001 5 (4, 5) (3, 5) 3 (3, 4) (2, 4) Total global scores 26.71±2.40 20.46±3.21 <0.001 27 (26, 28) (21, 30) 21 (18, 23) (12, 27) Open in a new tab a Mann–Whitney U test. COVID-19=coronavirus disease 2019, IQR=interquartile range, SD=standard deviation Discussion The impact of COVID-19 is more evident in the training of surgical subspecialties such as urology, general surgery, orthopedics, etc., While virtual educational platforms may be sufficient for the theoretical training of residents and fellows, the practical and surgical training had been halted during the pandemic due to the suspension of elective surgeries. Current study compared the residents'' competency in performing phacoemulsification, before and during the pandemic. Residents had poorer performance in all task-specific and global scores during the pandemic. Ophthalmology residency provides extensive training to fulfill comprehensive core ophthalmology competency over a period varying from 4 to 7 years according to the country of training. A prolonged delay in practical and surgical training might not be recovered without an extension of training and can result in failure to acquire the necessary surgical and clinical skills for both residents. There has been limited research on the effects of the COVID-19 pandemic on ophthalmology residency training thus far. Almost all previous reports indicate a significant and usually negative impact of the pandemic on the process of training in ophthalmology.[ 5 , 6 , 7 , 8 , 13 , 14 ] Our results were in agreement with previous studies and showed that the competency of residents dropped significantly during the pandemic compared to the prepandemic period. However, because some reports were published very early in the pandemic, they do not necessarily reflect its long-term impact on the education of ophthalmologists in training. Moreover, previous studies provided no objective data about the competency of residents.[ 2 , 5 , 6 , 7 , 8 , 13 , 14 ] Reduction in residents'' in-person hands-on practical surgical training is the most important reason for their poor surgical performance during the pandemic. During the pandemic, the highest drop in surgical procedures and outpatient consultations was recorded in ophthalmology (nearly 80%) among all fields of medicine.[ 15 ] Nearly all elective ophthalmic surgeries, which constitute the vast majority of ophthalmic procedures, were almost totally suspended.[ 5 ] It can be attributed to the higher risk of COVID-19 infection for ophthalmologists due to close contact with patients. As a result, ophthalmology residents have experienced remarkable limitations in their clinical training and surgical exposure. Alahmadi et al .[ 2 ] evaluated the impact of the current pandemic on ophthalmology residency training by obtaining the residents'' perspective. They reported a significant reduction in routine outpatient care and a dramatic decline in exposure to surgical training and minor procedures such as injections and lasers.[ 2 ] In Ferrara et al .''s survey, clinical activity was reportedly reduced by at least 50% in more than three-quarters of trainees.[ 4 ] However, no changes were noted in residents'' emergency care exposure as emergency departments continued to run normally during the lockdown.[ 2 ] Therefore, senior trainees seem to be affected to a greater extent compared to junior trainees during the COVID-19 pandemic.[ 16 ] Amparore et al .[ 17 ] also reported that the most impactful reduction in surgical training activities was shown by final-year residents. It is important as these are the residents who are on the verge of employment shortly and therefore need to gain independence in these procedures soon. One of the solutions is external rotation of the residents. Besides reduction in surgical exposure, pandemic-induced depressive disorders might affect the residents'' performance. More than half of the ophthalmology residents had mild to severe depressive symptoms during the pandemic.[ 2 ] The burnout rate was also higher than the traditional rates during the COVID-19 pandemic.[ 9 ] Depression and burnout might not be just associated with long duty hours. Stress about contracting the virus and bringing it home to friends and family appears to be an important contributor to this during the pandemic.[ 9 ] Phacoemulsification is one of the most common ophthalmic surgical procedures and is, therefore, an important component of residency training in ophthalmology. All residents should be reasonably proficient in this surgery at the end of their training. However, cataract surgery was the most restricted form of surgery in ophthalmology during the pandemic, even in university-based hospitals. In an international survey, more than half of the ophthalmology residents (53%) declared that they were unable to perform routine cataract surgery during the pandemic.[ 4 ] Similarly, Silva et al .[ 8 ] reported that up to 99% of Portuguese residents did not participate in any cataract surgery at that time. More than 60% of Indian residents also experienced deteriorated cataract surgery training during the pandemic.[ 7 ] In the UK, 80% of ophthalmology residents did not reach the expected cut-off value of 350 procedures of phacoemulsification by the end of training.[ 6 ] The learning curve for phacoemulsification cataract surgery is generally accepted to be quite steep. Previous studies showed that among various stages of phacoemulsification, nucleus dealing and capsulorhexis are always the most difficult parts in phaco learning curve.[ 18 , 19 , 20 , 21 , 22 ] In line with previous studies, our residents also had the lowest scores in nucleus sculpting, nucleus rotation, and chopping before and during the pandemic. The residents of the pandemic group also attained the lowest scores in capsulorhexis among all task-specific measures, while the capsulorhexis scores of the prepandemic group were relatively higher compared to other task-specific measures. It might be explained by steeper learning curve for capsulorhexis than for phacoemulsification part.[ 18 ] The senior trainees have less difficulty performing capsulorhexis, while they do not reach proficiency in phaco until their experience exceeds 100 cases.[ 18 , 19 , 20 , 21 ] In other words, the senior residents of the pandemic group did not perform enough surgeries even to gain competency in capsulorhexis. Current study also showed that the residents of the pandemic group had worse performances in global measures concerning fine, well-controlled intraocular maneuvers such as iris protection or intraocular spatial awareness. Live surgeries, edited video-recordings of surgeries and surgical simulation, such as wet-labs and cataract or vitreoretinal surgical simulators, are powerful tools for supporting some educational needs during the pandemic.[ 4 , 23 , 24 ] Specifically, surgical video-recordings are recognized as a safe and effective teaching tool that can be implemented with post hoc interactive discussion aiming to acquire crucial surgical and nontechnical skills, such as decision-making, surgical planning, situational awareness, and understanding of surgical steps.[ 24 , 25 ] In this regard, Ferrara et al .[ 4 ] asked ophthalmology trainees to evaluate if simulator training and web-based teaching sessions, including live discussion of edited surgical videos, could be helpful for surgical training during the pandemic. They observed an affirmative response of the trainees.[ 4 ] Although the residents'' satisfaction was remarkable with the virtual methods, they had negative perspectives on the quality of training during the pandemic.[ 2 , 3 ] It is probably rooted in the lack of practical training and indicates that virtual methods cannot replace in-person training completely.[ 2 , 3 ] Simulation-based tools do not provide enough abilities for surgery and cannot substitute real-life surgical scenarios.[ 4 , 23 ] High “observational” surgical volume and time spent on direct patients are the major barriers to their widespread adoption.[ 6 ] Current study has certain limitations. First, we did not consider the impact of environmental factors of the theater, such as probable guidance of the senior surgeons. Second, the number of previous surgeries for each resident was not considered in the analysis, although we set the cut-off point of 15 previous surgeries as the inclusion criterion. Moreover, other outcome measures such as surgery time were not assessed. The small sample size might be another limitation. Finally, the emotional impacts of COVID-19 may also affect the performance of residents and it is advisable to evaluate them using appropriate questionnaires. Conclusion The current study showed that the phacoemulsification competency of senior ophthalmology residents significantly decreased during the pandemic compared to the prepandemic period. It was more evident in nucleus handling, capsulorhexis, iris protection, and intraocular awareness. Financial support and sponsorship Nil. Conflicts of interest There are no conflicts of interest. References 1. Nguyen AX, Gervasio KA, Wu AY. Differences in SARS-CoV-2 recommendations from major ophthalmology societies worldwide. BMJ Open Ophthalmol. 2020;5:e000525. doi: 10.1136/bmjophth-2020-000525. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Alahmadi AS, Alhatlan HM, Bin Helayel H, Khandekar R, Al Habash A, Al-Shahwan S. Residents'' perceived impact of COVID-19 on Saudi ophthalmology training programs-A survey. Clin Ophthalmol. 2020;14:3755–61. doi: 10.2147/OPTH.S283073. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Tzoumas N, Boote T, Higgs J, Ellis H, Dhillon B, Cackett P. Transforming ophthalmic education into virtual learning during COVID-19 pandemic: A global perspective. Eye (Lond) 2021;35:2648–50. doi: 10.1038/s41433-020-01182-6. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Ferrara M, Romano V, Steel DH, Gupta R, Iovino C, van Dijk EHC, OphthaTraining Group; Romano MR. Reshaping ophthalmology training after COVID-19 pandemic. Eye (Lond) 2020;34:2089–97. doi: 10.1038/s41433-020-1061-3. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Konopińska J, Obuchowska I, Lisowski Ł, Dub N, Dmuchowska DA, Rękas M. Impact of the COVID-19 pandemic on ophthalmic specialist training in Poland. PLoS. One. 2021;16:e0257876. doi: 10.1371/journal.pone.0257876. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Hussain R, Singh B, Shah N, Jain S. Impact of COVID-19 on ophthalmic specialist training in the United Kingdom-the trainees'' perspective. Eye (Lond) 2020;34:2157–60. doi: 10.1038/s41433-020-1034-6. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Mishra D, Nair AG, Gandhi RA, Gogate PJ, Mathur S, Bhushan P, et al. The impact of COVID-19 related lockdown on ophthalmology training programs in India—Outcomes of a survey. Indian J Ophthalmol. 2020;68:999–1004. doi: 10.4103/ijo.IJO_1067_20. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Silva N, Laiginhas R, Meireles A, Breda JB. Impact of the COVID-19 pandemic on ophthalmology residency training in Portugal. Acta Med Port. 2020;33:640–8. doi: 10.20344/amp.14341. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Aziz H, James T, Remulla D, Sher L, Genyk Y, Sullivan ME, et al. Effect of COVID-19 on Surgical Training Across the United States: A national survey of general surgery residents. J Surg Educ. 2021;78:431–9. doi: 10.1016/j.jsurg.2020.07.037. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Clements JM, Burke JR, Hope C, Nally DM, Doleman B, Giwa L, et al. The quantitative impact of COVID-19 on surgical training in the United Kingdom. BJS Open. 2021;5:zrab051. doi: 10.1093/bjsopen/zrab051. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Golnik KC, Beaver H, Gauba V, Lee AG, Mayorga E, Palis G, et al. Development of a new valid, reliable, and internationally applicable assessment tool of residents'' competence in ophthalmic surgery (An American Ophthalmological Society Thesis) Trans Am Ophthalmol. 2013;111:24–33. [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. Chylack LT, Jr, Wolfe JK, Singer DM, Leske MC, Bullimore MA, Bailey IL, et al. The longitudinal study of Cataract Study Group. The lens opacities classification system III. Arch Ophthalmol. 1993;111:831–6. doi: 10.1001/archopht.1993.01090060119035. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. Szigiato AA, Palakkamanil M, Aubin MJ, Ziai S. Canadian ophthalmology resident experience during the COVID-19 pandemic. Can J Ophthalmol. 2021;56:e42–4. doi: 10.1016/j.jcjo.2020.10.015. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. El-Saied HMA, Salah Eddin Abdelhakim MA. Impact of COVID-19 pandemic on young ophthalmologists in Cairo university hospitals. Semin Ophthalmol. 2020;35:296–306. doi: 10.1080/08820538.2020.1826046. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 15. Mehrotra A, Chernew M, Linetsky D, Hatch H, Cutler D. New York: Commonwealth Fund; 2020. [Last accessed on 2023 Apr 10]. The Impact of the COVID-19 Pandemic on Outpatient Visits: A Rebound Emerges. Available from: https://www.commonwealthfund.org/publications/2020/apr/impact-covid-19-outpatient-visits . [ Google Scholar ] 16. Budden CR, Rannard F, Mennie J, Bulstrode N. The Impact of COVID-19 on Plastic Surgery Training in the United Kingdom, Canada and Australia-A cross-sectional study. Indian J Plast Surg. 2021;54:327–33. doi: 10.1055/s-0041-1734569. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 17. Amparore D, Checcucci E, Serni S, Minervini A, Gacci M, Esperto F, et al. European Society of Residents in Urology (ESRU). Urology Residency Training at the Time of COVID-19 in Italy: 1 Year After the Beginning. Eur Urol Open Sci. 2021;31:37–40. doi: 10.1016/j.euros.2021.07.002. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 18. Dooley IJ, O''Brien PD. Subjective difficulty of each stage of phacoemulsification cataract surgery performed by basic surgical trainees. J Cataract Refract Surg. 2006;32:604–8. doi: 10.1016/j.jcrs.2006.01.045. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 19. Yulan W, Yaohua S, Jinhua T, Min W. Step-by-step phacoemulsification training program for ophthalmology residents. Indian J Ophthalmol. 2013;61:659–62. doi: 10.4103/0301-4738.120224. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 20. Oetting TA. Surgical competency in residents. Curr Opin Ophthalmol. 2009;20:56–60. doi: 10.1097/icu.0b013e328319914f. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 21. Randleman JB, Wolfe JD, Woodward M, Lynn MJ, Cherwek DH, Srivastava SK. The resident surgeon phacoemulsification learning curve. Arch Ophthalmol. 2007;125:12159. doi: 10.1001/archopht.125.9.1215. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 22. Prakash G, Jhanji V, Sharma N, Gupta K, Titiyal JS, Vajpayee RB. Assessment of perceived difficulties by residents in performing routine steps in phacoemulsifiation surgery and in managing complications. Can J Ophthalmol. 2009;44:284–7. doi: 10.3129/i09-051. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 23. Lee R, Raison N, Lau WY, Aydin A, Dasgupta P, Ahmed K, et al. A systematic review of simulation-based training tools for technical and non-technical skills in ophthalmology. Eye (Lond) 2020;34:1737–59. doi: 10.1038/s41433-020-0832-1. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 24. Hollick EJ, Allan BD. Live surgery: National survey of United Kingdom ophthalmologists. J Cataract Refract Surg. 2008;34:1029–32. doi: 10.1016/j.jcrs.2008.02.023. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 25. Youngson GG. Teaching and assessing non-technical skills. Surgeon. 2011;9(Suppl 1):S35–7. doi: 10.1016/j.surge.2010.11.004. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',16,'2025-10-14 10:15:11','2025-10-14 10:15:11');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(2855,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10903720','The Ophthalmology Workload during the Lebanese Crisis and Pandemic.','Wehbi Z, Halawy M, Noureddin B, Al-Haddad C.','2024','neuroophthalmology pursuant unaffordable nonnuclear explosions tragic corneoscleral lacerations topographies precrisis nonlebanese daou paralyzed preimmigration premium unaffordable pound apprehensive pounds rhegmatogenous detachments compensating postlockdown moorfields ppediatric nonnuclear apartments pound redirecting neuroophthalmology sinking inaction devi daou helou helou hachem helou shti220218 helou aciksari salio schreeb dmp hajjar atallah faysal atiyeh bakhach cherfan jalkh bitar fares makdessi maalouf ghoul blanch mazzoli kheir awwad bou ghannam rachid nonnuclear explosions guell knorz packard piovella tellier besieged defeated escrs austerity tsaousis austerity ijabmr ijabmr_674_20 dhiman mjafi mjafi jcma alhilali swailem albdaya mousa khandekar rmhp s384456 baudin benzenine mariet ghezala daien gabrielle din phylactou fajardo moorfields opth s302576 sachdev ijo_1116_21 sukkarieh lahoud ghorayeb karam succarieh zahran geha sakr bachir s00068','Abstract: PURPOSE: The purpose is to to explore the effects of the Lebanese economic crisis on the ophthalmology workload in Lebanon, and the concomitant influence of the COVID-19-related lockdown and Beirut port explosion. METHODS: Data pertaining to all the clinic visits to the Ophthalmology Department at the American University of Beirut Medical Center between January 01, 2019, and December 31, 2021, were extracted. Completed visits to different subspecialties were used to analyze the different parameters. All parameters were compared across the 3 years. RESULTS: We included 102,810 completed visits in this study. A total decrease of 15.3% occurred in visits between 2019 and 2020. The mean number of patients per month decreased significantly between 2019 and 2020 in retina/uveitis and glaucoma/anterior segment clinics. The same trend was observed in pediatric/neuro-ophthalmology and cornea/refractive surgery, although it did not reach statistical significance. For oculoplastics, the workload actually tended to increase from 2019 to 2021. During the pandemic-related lockdown (March, April, and May 2020), the workload decreased by 37.8% in oculoplastics, 63.4% in pediatric/neuro-ophthalmology, 52.8% in retina/uveitis, 60.2% in cornea/refractive surgery, and 50.7% in glaucoma/anterior segment. After the Beirut port explosion in August 2020, an overall decrease of 30.2% was found in the number of patient visits in all sub-specialties, while the number of patients in oculoplastics remained nearly unchanged. CONCLUSION: The ophthalmology workload was strongly affected by the economic crisis in all subspecialties especially with the pandemic in 2020, except for oculoplastics. During the pandemic, the most affected subspecialty was the pediatric/neuroophthalmology, while oculoplastics was the least affected. Keywords: Beirut blast, COVID-19 pandemic, economic crisis, ophthalmology workload Introduction According to the latest World Bank Economic Monitor, Lebanon witnessed one of the top three most severe economic and financial crises globally since the mid-nineteenth century.[ 1 ] Add to that the many challenges posed by the COVID-19 pandemic and the huge inflation pursuant to the unprecedented Beirut port explosion.[ 1 ] A world report stated that the Lebanese health-care system was strongly and adversely affected by the crisis.[ 2 ] Hospitals were restricting operations and suffering from shortage in supplies and medications, while patients were opting for outpatient clinics instead of private ones,[ 2 ] postponing their appointments, using generic or alternative drugs due to the shortage or the high prices, and preferring to see their doctors in primary health-care centers to avoid the consultation fee, made unaffordable by the inflation rate.[ 3 ] Furthermore, after the Beirut port explosion, people’s priorities were to repair houses and recover from illnesses before pursuing elective follow-up clinical examinations.[ 3 ] Adding to the Lebanese crisis and the pandemic, one of the most powerful nonnuclear explosions occurred in Beirut in August 2020, with <50% of hospitals still capable of delivering full health services.[ 4 ] This tragic event added to the severe economic collapse: import and export were halted because of the large destruction estimated to be around 15 billion USD.[ 5 ] Various ocular injuries (77 patients) were admitted to eye center emergency rooms with more than half being open globe injuries;[ 6 ] suturing corneoscleral lacerations was needed in 14% of all surgeries done that night.[ 7 , 8 , 9 ] We aim to study the effect of these Lebanese overlapping crises on the clinical workload in ophthalmology in a Tertiary Care Center and to compare it across subspecialties in 2019, 2020, and 2021. Methods We conducted a retrospective review of electronic medical visits (EMR; EPIC Systems Corp, WI) pertaining to patients who presented to the Ophthalmology Department at the American University of Beirut Medical Center between January 1, 2019 and December 31, 2021. The data of the clinical visits were extracted. This study was approved by the Institutional Review Board of the American University of Beirut and performed in line with the principles of the Declaration of Helsinki. All the following subspecialties were included: retina, glaucoma, cornea and refractive surgery, pediatric ophthalmology, neuro-ophthalmology, and uveitis. The total number of patients per year in 2019, 2020, and 2021 as well as the number of patients seen in each subspecialty every month were extracted. Patients’ age, gender, and nationality were noted. Extracted data included whether the patient was insured or not (with the type of insurance) and the number of procedures (refractive surgeries and intravitreal injections) or imaging tests (namely optical coherence tomography, topographies) done each year. The percentage of all missed appointments in each year was noted. The surgical workload of each provider per month during the years 2019, 2020, and 2021 was also extracted. Duplicate entries or clinical visits with missing data were excluded from the study. Statistical analysis The extracted data were entered into SPSS version 25 (IBM Corp, Armonk, NY, USA). Means and standard deviation were reported for all continuous variables and number of visits per month. Chi-square test was used to compare categorical parameters between years. ANOVA was used to compare the means per month for each subspecialty across the 3 years, while independent t -tests were used to compare means per month between each 2 years. For all statistical analyses, a P < 0.05 was considered statistically significant. Results A total of 117,777 patient encounters were extracted. After filtering the data, 102,810 completed visits were analyzed. The number of patients examined in the ophthalmology department decreased 15.3% from 36,660 in 2019 to 31,056 in 2020, then increased by 13% to 35,094 completed visits in 2021. A total of 14,958 appointments were missed: 5585 in 2019, 3549 in 2020, and 5824 in 2021 [ Figure 1 ]. Figure 1. Open in a new tab Encounter filtering process Patient demographics are shown in Table 1 . The mean age was lower in 2020 and 2021 as compared to 2019 ( P < 0.001), with an equal division between both genders in all years. 88.4% of all patients were Lebanese, while the rest were citizens of more than 100 countries, 9.6% being from Arab countries. The majority of patients (83.5%) were adults ( P < 0.001). The mean age for the pediatric population was 8.1 ± 4.8 years while it was 55.1 ± 19.6 years for adults. During 2020, the number of insured people decreased by 4.5% compared to precrisis (2019). Similarly, the number of self-payers decreased by 24.4% from 19,875 in 2019 to 15,024 in 2020. These numbers increased in 2021 as well as the proportion of employees and patients with governmental coverage. Table 1. Demographic characteristics of all patients with completed visits 2019 2020 2021 P Age (years), mean±SD 48.1±25.5 47.3±24.6 46.7±25.1 <0.001 Gender, n (%) Male 16,622 (45) 14,285 (46) 16,036 (46) 0.32 Nationality, n (%) Lebanese 32,392 (88.4) 27,761 (89.4) 30,786 (87.7) 0.14 Nonlebanese 4268 (11.6) 3295 (10.6) 4308 (12.3) 0.048 Age group Pediatric 6179 4733 5962 0.077 Adults 30,481 26,323 29,132 0.14 Open in a new tab SD: Standard deviation The decrease in the workload between 2019 and 2020 was statistically significant for retina/uveitis ( P = 0.01) and glaucoma/anterior segment ( P = 0.026). However, it was not significant for cornea/refractive surgery and pediatric/neuro-ophthalmology. The same trend was observed with the increase in the number of patients between 2020 and 2021, where it was statistically significant for retina/uveitis ( P = 0.049) and glaucoma/anterior segment ( P = 0.035). In oculoplastics, the mean number of patients tended to increase from 198.2 ± 40.6 patients/month in 2019 to 206.2 ± 58.8 patients/month in 2020 ( P = 0.25). In addition, the number of refractive surgeries done in 2020 was higher by 46% when compared to 2019. On the other hand, the changes in the workload between 2019 and 2021 were not statistically significant in any ophthalmic subspecialty [ Table 2 ]. Table 2. Mean number of patients per month and difference across subspecialties between 2019, 2020, and 2021 Mean number of patients per month P (ANOVA) 2019 2020 2021 Oculoplastics 198.2±40.6 206.2±58.8 232.2±46.3 0.25 Pediatric/neuro 401.6±75 302.9±110.9 399.7±120.3 0.052 Retina/uveitis 778.1±68.3 611.8±154.3 740.3±145.8 0.012* Cornea/refractive 731.2±107.3 616.4±190.4 642.2±103.2 0.15 Glaucoma/anterior segment 375.7±33.3 304.9±73.3 367.3±62 0.015 † Open in a new tab *The difference in workload was statistically significant while comparing 2019–2020 ( P =0.01), and 2020–2021 ( P =0.049), † The difference in workload was statistically significant while comparing 2019–2020 ( P =0.026), and 2020–2021 ( P =0.035) During the pandemic-related lockdown in Lebanon (from March 2020 to end of May 2020), the workload across subspecialties decreased clearly: a decrease of 37.8% between the lockdown period in 2019 and the same period in 2020 was noted in oculoplastics, 63.4% in pediatric/neuro-ophthalmology, 52.8% in retina/uveitis, 60.2% in cornea/refractive surgery, and 50.7% for glaucoma/anterior segment. This decrease was observed especially in April 2020 where the number of patients (as compared to April 2019), decreased 76.8% in pediatric/neuro-ophthalmology, 67.7% in cornea/refractive surgery, 63.2% in oculoplastics, 60.7% in retina/uveitis, and 57.4% in glaucoma/anterior segment. In addition, after the Beirut Port explosion in August 2020, the workload decreased in all subspecialties except for Oculoplastics where the number of patients remained nearly unchanged [ Figure 2 ]. Figure 2. Open in a new tab Comparison of the workload in months showing the pandemic (March–May 2020) and the Beirut explosion period (August 2020) compared to the same periods in 2019 and 2021 The total surgical workload decreased in all subspecialties between 2019 and 2020: 27% in glaucoma/anterior segment, 27% in pediatric/neuro-ophthalmology, 25.5% in cornea/refractive surgery, 3.3% in oculoplastics, and 2.6% in retina/uveitis. The number of surgeries decreased during the lockdown (2020) 75.8% in cornea/refractive surgery, 56.8% in glaucoma/anterior segment, 45.2% in pediatric/neuro-ophthalmology, 37.5% in retina/uveitis, and 33.3% in oculoplastics, compared to the same period in 2019. In August 2020, the number of surgeries decreased 39% in oculoplastics, 20% in cornea/refractive surgery, 16.1% in retina/uveitis, 10.3% in glaucoma/anterior segment, and only 3.8% in pediatric/neuro-ophthalmology [ Figure 3 ]. Figure 3. Open in a new tab Comparison of the surgical workload changes during 2019, 2020, and 2021 Discussion Our study compared the workload in the ophthalmology department at a leading hospital in Lebanon before, during, and after the Lebanese economic crisis. The main economic crisis started at the end of 2019 and worsened in 2020, in addition to the COVID-19-related lockdown imposed by the government during March–May 2020, and the unprecedented port explosion in August 2020 – all make 2020 the “year of the overlapping crises.” This is the first report about the impact of these crises on the ophthalmology practice in Lebanon and the differential effect across the various subspecialties. This study showed that workload decreased by 15.3% between 2019 and 2020 and then increased by 13% in 2021. Workload decreased significantly between 2019 and 2020 for retina/uveitis and glaucoma/anterior segment; the decrease was not significant for the cornea/refractive clinic, pediatric/neuro-ophthalmology, and oculoplastics clinics. Moreover, the workload decreased >50% for all sub-specialties during the pandemic lockdown (March, April, and May 2020) when compared to the same period in 2019, except for oculoplastics where it decreased by 37.8%. The Beirut port explosion had a similar effect on all subspecialties except for oculoplastics where the clinical workload was nearly unchanged. The current study showed that the total number of surgeries done decreased in all ophthalmic sub-specialties between 2019 and 2020, mostly in glaucoma/anterior segment and pediatric/neuro-ophthalmology (27%). During the COVID-19 lockdown, the highest decrease in surgical workload was noted in cornea/refractive surgery (75.8%), while in August 2020, and after the port explosion, the number of surgeries done decreased the most in oculoplastics (33%). A recent study by Daou et al . found that the health-care system in Lebanon was paralyzed by the crises: after conducting interviews with six Lebanese physicians from different specialties, many of them stated that the workload was affected by the purchasing power decrease, and an increase in demand for preimmigration evaluations/medical reports from Lebanon was noted.[ 3 ] Lebanese people were not able to access their bank savings, due to withdrawals’ restrictions and capital control; the currency lost more than 90% of its value between 2019 and 2021. Moreover, the number of people with extreme poverty in Lebanon was rising and their priority was feeding their children, which affected continuity of care and induced delays in disease management.[ 3 ] Lebanese patients started seeking primary health care centers with no consultation fee and generic drug use was prevalent.[ 3 ] Among our studied population, in 2020, a decrease of 4.5% in people with private health insurance was noted, with a further decrease of 24.4% in self-payers. This could justify the notable flow to the primary health-care centers and avoidance of expensive drugs. After the global recession of 2008, a decrease in currency value occurred, health-care budget restrictions were adopted in Europe and medication prices increased.[ 10 ] Ophthalmologists in the United Kingdom were rationing the number of procedures including surgeries, while no premium lenses were used during the crisis in Italy.[ 10 ] In Greece, where the economic crisis lasted for years, patients shifted to public hospitals for their ophthalmic treatment because private ones became unaffordable.[ 11 ] Significant dysfunctions hit the Greek public system during the financial crisis in 2009. A decrease of 50% affected the funding of public hospitals, as well as the quality of provided services. This effect was worse in ophthalmology as it needed sophisticated technology and materials of high costs.[ 12 ] In our study, the mean number of patients visiting the ophthalmology department decreased after the crisis (2020) in all subspecialties except in Oculoplastics, where it tended to increase between 2019 and 2020. Adding to that, the number of refractive surgeries done in 2020 was higher by 46% when compared to 2019. This could be explained by the fact that the Lebanese pound started to decrease in early 2020 and people were apprehensive that the elective procedure prices would increase later to match the real expected rate of USD, as the fees were still the same in Lebanese pounds. There was also rising fear of a shortage of surgical supplies and closure of services. The COVID-19 pandemic further burdened the already failing economic status. In March 2020, and because of the pandemic, the Lebanese Government decided to impose a complete lockdown in the country that lasted till May. Our study showed a decrease of more than 35% between the lockdown period in 2020 compared to the same period in 2019 in all sub-specialties: this decrease was the highest in pediatric/neuro-ophthalmology (63.4%) and the lowest in oculoplastics (37.8%). A multicenter study in India showed similarly a decrease in the number of elective surgical cases in ophthalmology during the pandemic.[ 13 ] However, this number did not change in other specialties such as obstetrics, oncology, orthopedics, and neurosurgery.[ 13 ] Another study in Taiwan stated that the ophthalmology department was one of the most affected departments with workload decline after the start of the pandemic.[ 14 ] The load of cataract surgeries per month decreased during the pandemic and did not recover to prepandemic levels in Saudi Arabia, and a 41.6% decrease in the number of rhegmatogenous retinal detachments was noted during the 2 months lockdown period in 2020 in France, without compensating postlockdown activity.[ 15 , 16 ] At Moorfields Eye Hospital in London, a 92% reduction in corneal elective work was observed during the lockdown period.[ 17 ] In the current study, and during the lockdown, a overall decrease in the surgical workload was noted in all subspecialties: cornea/refractive surgery (75.8%), glaucoma/anterior segment (56.8%), pPediatric/neuro-ophthalmology (45.2%), retina/uveitis (37.5%), and oculoplastics (33.3%). In India, a 25% or more reduction in outpatient workload and surgeries occurred for more than 90% of ophthalmologists during the COVID-19. The financial sustainability of ophthalmologists was significantly affected.[ 18 ] The Beirut Port explosion on August 4, 2020, had a detrimental effect on what remained of the economic structure and stability in Lebanon; after that date, the number of clinic visits decreased across subspecialties when compared to August 2019 except for Oculoplastics where it nearly remained the same. Globe injury combined with oculoplastic or neuro-ophthalmic injury was seen in more than half of the injured patients, and this marked the complexity of management.[ 7 , 8 ] A study done on 23 eyes of 19 patients showed that glass material caused open-globe injuries in all of them.[ 19 ] This could be the reason why our oculoplastics clinical workload did not change during August 2020. The number of oculoplastic surgeries decreased 39% compared to August 2019, probably due to the fact that only urgent surgeries caused by the explosion were performed during this month. The nonnuclear devastating explosion destroyed the capital, with more than 200 deaths, and nearly 300,000 Lebanese left homeless.[ 20 ] This made displaced people and those suffering multiple injuries give priority to restore their apartments or seek medical care.[ 3 ] Routine eye check-ups or elective ocular surgeries were mostly postponed. Overall, the increase in the oculoplastic surgeries was attributed to the ocular injuries caused by the Beirut port blast, in addition to the increased number of patients opting to undergo elective surgeries which became more affordable as the Lebanese Pound started to lose its value at the beginning of the economic crisis. The decrease in the number of other ocular surgeries is attributed to the decrease and scarcity of health-care resources and redirecting the remaining resources toward the most critical and urgent cases, owing to the global pandemic and the national crisis. Our study has some limitations. First, this was a retrospective review of the encounters in a single institution, albeit the largest Tertiary Care Center in Beirut and Lebanon. Second, there were some duplicate visits and a high number of missed appointments that were eliminated. In addition, and to assess subspecialties workload, the analyzed data pertained only to private clinic visits and did not include the visits to outpatient departments (resident clinics). The main strength of this study is the very large number of visits to the largest hospital in Beirut, in addition to being the first study to reflect the effects of the overlapping Lebanese crises on ophthalmic care in the country. Conclusion The economic crisis significantly influenced the ophthalmology workload, with all subspecialties impacted except for oculoplastics. During the pandemic, pediatric/neuroophthalmology was the most affected, while oculoplastics, was the least influenced. Financial support and sponsorship Nil. Conflicts of interest There are no conflicts of interest. References 1. The World Bank. Lebanon Sinking into One of the Most Severe Global Crises Episodes, Amidst Deliberate Inaction. 2021 [ Google Scholar ] 2. Devi S. Economic crisis hits Lebanese health care. Lancet. 2020;395:548. doi: 10.1016/S0140-6736(20)30407-4. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Daou M, Helou S, El Helou J, El Hachem C, El Helou E. Ensuring care continuity in extreme crises: A participatory action research approach. Stud Health Technol Inform. 2022;290:937–41. doi: 10.3233/SHTI220218. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Helou M, El-Hussein M, Aciksari K, Salio F, Della Corte F, von Schreeb J, et al. Beirut explosion: The largest non-nuclear blast in history. Disaster Med Public Health Prep. 2022;16:2200–1. doi: 10.1017/dmp.2021.328. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Hajjar MS, Atallah GM, Faysal H, Atiyeh B, Bakhach J, Ibrahim AE. The 2020 beirut explosion: A healthcare perspective. Ann Burns Fire Disasters. 2021;34:293–300. [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Mansour AM, Cherfan DG, Jalkh A, Kuhn F. Analysis of ocular injury 1-year outcome in survivors of Beirut port ammonium nitrate blast. Graefes Arch Clin Exp Ophthalmol. 2022;260:2353–9. doi: 10.1007/s00417-022-05580-2. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Mansour HA, Bitar E, Fares Y, Makdessi AA, Maalouf A, Ghoul ME, et al. The Beirut Port explosion: Injury trends from a mass survey of emergency admissions. Lancet. 2021;398:21–2. doi: 10.1016/S0140-6736(21)01246-0. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Blanch RJ, Mazzoli RA, Porter K. Ophthalmic injuries in the port explosion in Beirut, Lebanon: Lessons for provision of ophthalmic trauma care and major incident management. JAMA Ophthalmol. 2021;139:943–5. doi: 10.1001/jamaophthalmol.2021.2707. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Kheir WJ, Awwad ST, Bou Ghannam A, Khalil AA, Ibrahim P, Rachid E, et al. Ophthalmic injuries after the port of beirut blast-one of largest nonnuclear explosions in history. JAMA Ophthalmol. 2021;139:937–43. doi: 10.1001/jamaophthalmol.2021.2742. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Güell J, Knorz M, Packard R, Piovella M, Tellier T. European Ophthalmology Besieged but not Defeated by Global Recession. Ocular Surgery News. 2008 [ Google Scholar ] 11. ESCRS. Ophthalmology in Greece Affected by Strict Austerity Measures. Euro Times. 2016 [ Google Scholar ] 12. Tsaousis KT. Cataract services in Greek public hospitals through and after the austerity period. Int J Appl Basic Med Res. 2021;11:192–4. doi: 10.4103/ijabmr.IJABMR_674_20. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. Paul SK, Joshi A, Goel A, Singh G, Paul D, Dhiman M, et al. Non-COVID surgical load of operation theatre during COVID-19 pandemic in armed forces tertiary care centres:“Snapshot of two timelines”. Med J Armed Forces India. 2022 doi: 10.1016/j.mjafi.2022.04.016. [doi:10.1016/j.mjafi. 2022 04.016] [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. Chang YT, Chiang SC, Lee WC, Chen TJ, Hwang SJ. Varied impacts on outpatient services among departments and divisions in the early phase of the COVID-19 pandemic: Implications for personnel mobilization and preparatory training. J Chin Med Assoc. 2021;84:951–5. doi: 10.1097/JCMA.0000000000000569. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 15. AlHilali S, Al-Swailem SA, Albdaya N, Mousa A, Khandekar R. Impact and determinants of COVID-19 pandemic on the cataract surgery rate at a tertiary referral center. Risk Manag Healthc Policy. 2022;15:2335–42. doi: 10.2147/RMHP.S384456. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 16. Baudin F, Benzenine E, Mariet AS, Ben Ghezala I, Daien V, Gabrielle PH, et al. Impact of COVID-19 lockdown on surgical procedures for retinal detachment in France: A national database study. Br J Ophthalmol. 2023;107:565–9. doi: 10.1136/bjophthalmol-2021-319531. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 17. Din N, Phylactou M, Fajardo-Sanchez J, Watson M, Ahmad S. The impact of COVID-19 on acute and elective corneal surgery at Moorfields eye hospital London. Clin Ophthalmol. 2021;15:1639–45. doi: 10.2147/OPTH.S302576. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 18. Agarwal D, Saxena R, Sinha R, Mishra D, Sachdev MS, Sharma N. All India ophthalmological society financial impact survey on ophthalmology practice in India during COVID-19. Indian J Ophthalmol. 2021;69:2196–201. doi: 10.4103/ijo.IJO_1116_21. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 19. Sukkarieh G, Lahoud C, Ghorayeb R, Abi Karam M, Succarieh Y, Saleh M, et al. Characteristics of open eye injuries in the Beirut Port explosion. Injury. 2021;52:2601–5. doi: 10.1016/j.injury.2021.07.031. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 20. El Zahran T, Geha M, Sakr F, Bachir R, El Sayed M. The Beirut port blast: Spectrum of injuries and clinical outcomes at a large tertiary care center in Beirut, Lebanon. Eur J Trauma Emerg Surg. 2022;48:4919–26. doi: 10.1007/s00068-022-02023-9. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',26,'2025-10-14 10:15:22','2025-10-14 10:15:22');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(2856,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6611303','Robotics and ophthalmology: Are we there yet?','Pandey SK, Sharma V.','2019','telerobotic femtosecond zeus zrss femtosecond prototypes mechatronics devloped probot transurethral zeus literal unintelligible areused telesurgical transcontinental instigated improvised minuscule mechatronics improvise magnificently maneuverability unobstructed curtailed slightest tsirbas telemanipulation viewfinder stereoscopic wrists processor processor yaw manipulates stereoscopic endowrist scissors spreaders stereoscopic stereoscopic stereoscopic stereo showcases icons telemanipulation consoles stereoscopic unobstructed processor simplifies telemanipulator plana capsulorhexis keratoplasty vigilantly motions wrists capsulorhexis telemanipulators comanipulation underneath tsirbas lacerations videoscope manipulator trackball trackball manipulator dogangil miniature microrobots barrel submarine microneedles microrobot vitreo microrobots telerobotic elaborates telerobotic motions curtail mishaps telerobotic nudging bounds stereotaxical telemanipulator interoperator manipulator skillful capsulorhexis plana hexapod electromechanical caliper mechatronics telesurgery stereoscopic joystick femtosecond capsulorhexis tsirbas mango dutson bimanual delicately smet naus gjl faridpooya mura semiautonomous manipulator preceyes hubschman tsirbas capsulorhexis realignment miniaturization manipulator roizenblatt gehlbach micron bourla hubschman culjat plana capsulorhexis maneuverability realignment presume atleast startup meticulousness femtosecond capsulorhexis motions preprogrammed unwatchful endured innately perceptive deviates reciprocate bourla hemal challacombe 410x simaan i10 ruurda broeders iamj simmermacher borel rinkes vroonhoven laparosc percutan igs lanfranco castellanos s0889 tsirbas mango dutson rahimy hubschman iriss liverneaux hendriks selber parekattil robotically bourcier nardin gaucher speeg mutter nonliving tvst bourges hubschman hexapod bourges hubschman culjat iordachita kazanzides handa manipulator inrobotics menciassi fichtinger fiorini dario smet naus gjl faridpooya mura attariwala colgate glucksberg bf01880668 dogangil ergeneman pane muntwyler iros rsj microrobots rams jpl nefs grafton binocular marescaux rubino vix transcontinental telesurgery hubschman tsirbas spitznas motorized teleguided micromanipulator toyama roggia tamaki e54116 roizenblatt gehlbach rsrr s122301 nuzzi brusasco s148644 pitcher hubschman jcsb grozdanovic grozdanovic asimov three_laws_of_robotics takacs rudas haidegger polytech bourla hubschman culjat tsirbas iae 0b013e318068de46','Abstract Ophthalmology is a field that is now seeing the integration of robotics in its surgical procedures and interventions. Assistance facilitated by robots offers substantial improvements in terms of movement control, tremor cancellation, enhanced visualization, and distance sensing. Robotic technology has only recently been integrated into ophthalmology; hence, the progression is only in its initial stages. Robotic technologies such as da Vinci Surgical System are integrated into the field of ophthalmology and are assisting surgeons in complex eye surgeries. Ophthalmic surgeries require high accuracy and precision to execute tissue manipulation, and some complex ocular surgery may take few hours to complete the procedures that may predispose high-volume ophthalmic surgeons to work-related musculoskeletal disorders. A complete paradigm shift has been achieved in this particular field through the integration of advanced robotic technology, resulting in easier and more efficient procedures. Where robotic technology assists the surgeons and improves the overall quality of care, it also projects several challenges including limited availability, training, and the high cost of the robotic system. Although considerable studies and trials have been conducted for various robotic systems, only a few of them have made it to the commercial stage and ophthalmology, on its own, has a long way to go in robotics technology. Keywords: da Vinci robotic system, robotics in ophthalmology, intraocular robotic interventional surgical system, telerobotic technology, femtosecond laser cataract devices, robot assisted vitreoretinal surgery, ZEUS Robotic Surgical System (ZRSS), multi-arm hybrid robotic system Recent advertisement of femtosecond laser system as “robotic cataract surgery” generated interest among ophthalmologists and patients. Robotics have indeed revolutionized various surgical specialties, including orthopedics, gynecology, urology, and laparoscopic surgery.[ 1 ] Robot-assisted surgery provides plenty of improvement over unassisted human hands such as high precision, improved dexterity in small spaces, more range of motion, and tremor filtration.[ 1 , 2 ] Innovations have also been made in the field of ophthalmology and the robotic surgery integration has been the most major recent advancement. Numerous prototypes and designs have been introduced in recent years to utilize in ophthalmic surgery as well.[ 3 ] Surgical robots in ophthalmology have the ability to advance the treatments, decrease the rates of complications, and also offer the potential of treating conditions that remain incurable even today. Majority of the robotic surgical systems in this field in the past have mostly focused on the singular tasks, typically the ones that were delicate. Although there is still ongoing research in this area, there has been an increasing interest in developing devices which can perform complete surgical procedures. The main goal for these surgeries would be to include more efficiency and speed without losing the ability of increased precision.[ 4 ] Another requirement to meet this goal is to add the ability to switch instruments mid-surgery, manipulation of the surgical instrument simultaneously, and an adequate range of motion. Robotics in Medicine-A Historical Account Robotics is not a new concept in the medical field. Medical professionals have been using robot-based applications in medicine to assist them in various ways. As the meaning denotes, robots are essentially meant to provide labor to make the work easier for humans. Over the past 20 years, robotics has made a place in various subspecialties of the medical world. First developed by the Mechatronics in Medicine Laboratory, Imperial College, London, United Kingdom, it has been almost 30 years since robots were devloped for of physician''s assistance in the surgical rooms. The first robot, Probot , was designed primarily to aid the medical team in the transurethral resection of the prostate in 1991.[ 5 ] With technological progression, the robotics in the surgical field advanced as well and the world witnessed integration of robotics such as Zeus and da Vinci systems in the medical industry. These systems have been incorporated in the surgical procedures at a remarkable speed. Within the span of 10 years, the medical industry transformed as healthcare organizations started to utilize these systems in the clinical practice. When taken in literal terms, the robot means a machine that is designed to execute unintelligible and repetitive tasks. However, today they areused for performing tasks that require extreme precision, specification, and entail certain risks and danger. The robotic technology presently is able to perform research and tasks which cannot be possibly done using human workforce.[ 6 ] Despite the effectiveness of robotics in the field of surgery and medicine, a slow progress has been witnessed in the overall integration of technology in the health industry. They have taken a slow route entering into the field but are gaining steady advancement with the passage of time. The telesurgical machines are used for the transcontinental robot-assisted cholecystectomy for quite some time now. The newer technology like voice-activated robotic arms has joined the industry recently. Introduction of robotic technology in the medical field has instigated a complete paradigm shift. It has made the procedures easier and more efficient and has improvised the overall quality of care.[ 6 , 7 , 8 ] The subsequent section of the review article sheds light on how the ophthalmologists can benefit from this particular advancement. Robotics in Ophthalmology Figs. 1 – 3 illustrate some of the robotics model used in ophthalmic surgery. Although it has become vital for all levels and all kinds of surgeries to be highly accurate and precise when it comes to tissue manipulation, ophthalmologic surgeries are unique for their limited surgical area and minuscule tissue structure. The delicate ocular surgical procedures are subjected to several limitations. Factors such as hand tremors and the degree of control over the sensitive tissue can make a huge difference. Figure 1. Open in a new tab A Steady-hand Eye Robot Developed in Johns Hopkins University for Retinal Microsurgery. (Figure courtesy- Johns Hopkins University) Figure 3. Open in a new tab Intraocular Robotic Interventional Surgical System. (Figure courtesy- Mechatronics) Figure 2. Open in a new tab Da Vinci Surgical System. (Figure courtesy- Intuitive Surgical, USA) Robotic surgery in the medical field is generally implemented in the context where the surgeon is able to operate using smart instruments that have a superior level of functions. Mainstream clinical care has been able to improvise magnificently because of the assistance offered through surgical robots. This technology has sufficiently offered the professionals abilities to have three-dimensional views, superior instrument maneuverability, increased magnification, and diminished error possibility. As a matter of fact, ophthalmology is a medical specialty that requires stabilized hand movement, adequate lighting, and a clear and unobstructed view. Robotic surgery is clearly suitable for these requirements. It has curtailed the possibilities of collateral tissue damage and threats directed towards visualization. Technically, surgeries in ophthalmology are complicated and unlike other surgical procedures, ophthalmologists are able to work on the surgical site and ocular structures directly. This provides them greater visibility but also enhances the risks involved as the sensitive and delicate ocular tissue (e.g. retina) is prone to get damaged even on the slightest error from the surgeon''s end. This challenge has been significantly addressed through the robotics technology. According to the study of Tsirbas et al ., the robotic instrumentation can offer the performing surgeons ability to execute a short learning curve and improve their overall surgical speed.[ 8 ] As a matter of fact, the robotic technology is able to provide the precision of 1 mm currently. This is only going to develop further as the technology is advancing in the field of ophthalmology. Robotic surgery in the ophthalmologic industry, although currently in very early phase, possesses significant scope. It can sufficiently fulfill the gap where there is a lack of professional assistance. Theoretically, developing countries, for instance, can benefit from this precise technological opportunity if these devices are produced by local manufacturers and are made accessible to the practitioners. These robotic devices can facilitate the surgeries that are otherwise unavailable in the territory. India can be taken as an example. There are substantial implications in the implementation of the robotics technology in ophthalmology such as direct robotic costs, availability and maintenance of the devices, and staff training. However, if the technology is successfully implemented, it can bring productive outcomes in the field. There are several robotic technologies that have been successfully integrated into the ophthalmic surgical procedures. The following sections offer an overview of the da Vinci System that is the most commonly used robot in the field of ophthalmology. The da Vinci Surgical System The da Vinci system is the current standard robotic surgical system used in the field of ophthalmology. Developed by the Intuitive Surgical, USA, it is a telemanipulation robot that has been utilized for performing pterygium surgery in human eyes and has been successful in ex vivo corneal surgery. This system, essentially, comprises of two primary components: a control console and the robotic apparatus. The control console is for the surgeon to manipulate the robotic arms using the remote. The robotic apparatus comprises of three, sometimes four, arms that serve the purpose of holding a dual channel endoscope. The console offers an ocular viewfinder that offers a stereoscopic view of the operative field from the endoscope. The da Vinci system allows the surgeon to manipulate the controls of the console using their fingers, wrists, hands, and arms. These movements are then transferred to the robotic arms once they are filtered and scaled by the computer processor. No significant delay is incurred between the movements of the surgeon and that of the robotic arms and system.[ 9 ] The movements of the surgeon are almost mirrored by the system, except that the tremors and minor movements are filtered by the processor. The system is designed in a manner that it allows the surgeon to insert, extract, roll, yaw, and grip the tools of the robot. Three arms of system carry surgical instruments, and the fourth arm, which is only recently added, manipulates the digital stereoscopic camera to visualize the field or area under surgery. Each of the arms has several joints, allowing the system to have a three-dimensional movement of the surgical instruments and optics. The tools have technology that offers movement up to 360°. This is called EndoWrist technology. Three arms designated to operate surgical tools can handle dissecting forceps, scissors, scalpel, spreaders, and other similar tools. The stereoscopic camera in the fourth arm comprises of a lens and dual stereoscopic cameras. The lens comprises of a video imaging column and two light sources to illuminate the field of surgery. The dual stereoscopic cameras are for the three-dimensional vision having progressive magnification, offering a magnified image up to 12-15 times. The console in the da Vinci system has an optical viewing system, known as the stereo viewer which offers a three-dimensional view of the operating field and showcases the messages and icons from the system, indicating the status of the robot in real time. There are two telemanipulation handles that allow remote manipulation of the four articulated robotic arms. The da Vinci SI, the updated version of the system, offers two consoles, enabling two surgeons to operate simultaneously. However, out of the two operators, one is the primary robotic surgeon and the other a surgical assistant. Contrary to the other newer versions, da Vinci SI has three robotic arms instead of four and they can be utilized by two operators at the same time.[ 10 ] da Vinci System in Ophthalmic Surgery The da Vinci system can offer tremendous benefits to the ocular surgical procedures, making them more effective and convenient to proceed. The most significant property of the robotic system is its optical magnification. The stereoscopic camera in the system offers optical and digital magnification of the field of surgery, enabling the surgeon to have a clear and unobstructed view of the specific area.[ 11 , 12 ] Secondly, the ability of the system processor to control and filter the tremors in the surgeon''s movements results in limiting the human error and improving the quality of surgical movements. The joints in the robotic arms enable a 360° movement which ultimately offers optimal positioning and accuracy during the surgery. The da Vinci robot surgical system is designed in a manner that it simplifies the motion. The console for the surgeons ensures better comfort during microsurgery. To operate with minimal incisions and for conducting minimally invasive surgeries, the da Vinci system offers great support through offering a magnified vision, adequate illumination, and fine surgical movements.[ 12 ] The da Vinci system is the only surgical telemanipulator that is available in the market and is currently being introduced in the field of ophthalmology for anterior and posterior segment procedures. It can be effectively used in the procedures of corneal laceration repair, pars plana vitrectomy, intraocular foreign body removal, anterior capsulorhexis, penetrating keratoplasty, and pterygium surgery.[ 13 , 14 , 15 ] Limitations of the da Vinci Robotic System Design The design of the da Vinci surgical system in the field of ophthalmology is not completely sans challenges. There are some limitations in the design that obscure its optimal functioning and application in the practical world. The first limitation is the presence of high remote center of motion (RCM) in the robot, placed right above the wrist and at a long distance from the tip of the instrument. This location of the RCM makes the intraocular movements less controllable and promotes unnecessary tension of the surface of the external eye. This demands the surgeon to utilize another RCM in the procedure during the ocular penetration site.[ 14 ] This system requires the surgeon to move the robotic arms rather vigilantly to ensure the right positioning and movement of the RCM. These motions, as a matter of fact, are not as sensitive as the human wrists and cannot mirror the exact movements of the surgeon''s arms. As a result, the range of motion gets limited. This, subsequently, results in creating challenges in the complex ocular procedures such as creating round, curvilinear capsulorhexis that is mandatory for cataract surgeries. The second limitation in the process of using the da Vinci surgical robots in ophthalmologic procedure is the achievement of intraoperative visualization. The robot entails a video capture system that is designed essentially for the endoscopic use and cannot offer an optimal and detailed image like that of an operating microscope. Considering that technology is still in its initial stages, it is likely to progress and address these limitations with advancement.[ 15 ] Application of Robotics in the Ophthalmic Surgery The robots created for operating on the eyes must meet specific basic requirements. There are three designs that are being developed currently for telemanipulators utilizing virtual or fixed remote center of motion, comanipulation devices, and smart surgical tools such as a steady hand.[ 16 ] There are several potential applications of robotic technology in the field of ophthalmology. Some of them are discussed underneath. Corneal laceration repair According to the study of Tsirbas et al .,[ 8 ] the da Vinci surgical system has been successfully used in the repair of the corneal lacerations in harvested porcine eyes. The research study compared the time taken to complete the surgery through human performance and the robotic arm performance. In the system-performed surgery, a videoscope and two 360° rotating, 8-mm instruments with three robotic arms were positioned over the eye. The surgery was performed using a robotic system console and was successfully executed with the advanced visualization offered to facilitate the process and control the delicate placement of the corneal sutures. Retinal vascular microsurgery The study of Jensen et al . presents the application of the da Vinci surgical system to perform retinal microsurgery.[ 17 ] In their research, they used a six degree of freedom manipulator that required a hand-held trackball to operate. A computer controller was used to interpret the input of the trackball and the manipulator was moved according to a pre-programmed algorithm. The researchers claim that the device can be used in microsurgery and limit the aspects of tremor and fatigue. The device, however, has its limitations of size and operational features. Vitreoretinal surgery The study conducted by Dogangil et al . refers to the usage of miniature autonomous robots or microrobots.[ 18 ] These devices are so tiny that they can fit into the barrel of a syringe and can execute movements like that of a submarine. Alongside, the researchers are working on developing a three-dimensional visual serving approach that has the capability to carry microneedles and chemical sensors which can help in the process of surgery and diagnosis. The objective of the research is to develop controlled magnetic fields that can steer the microrobot, whereas an active microscope can offer a real-time, three-dimensional feedback. This can allow drug delivery in the field of vitreo-retinal surgery using wireless magnetic microrobots.[ 18 ] Telerobotic Technology The article of Volpe elaborates the commercial use of the robot-assisted microsurgery system.[ 19 ] The system utilizes the telerobotic technology of NASA to carry out a precision cable-driven system that offers efficient functioning. It scales down the human input motions, filters tremors to improve the accuracy and precision, and offers constrained motion of the instrument in the eye to curtail any surgical mishaps and detrimental impacts.[ 19 ] Utilizing telerobotic systems and programming them to utilize motion scaling, stepwise nudging, and z bounds can enhance both safety and speed when moving towards the target point.[ 20 ] Progressively increased autonomous and automated surgeries are most likely the path forward and with the rapid research and advancements underway currently, this can be possible in near future and can result in further revolutionizing the field of ophthalmology. Application of robotics in ophthalmology is summarized in Table 1 . Table 2 summarizes the studies published on robotics and ophthalmology. Table 1. Application of Robotics in Ophthalmology Robotics in Ophthalmology Place and Year of Development Functions 1. Stereotaxical Micro-telemanipulator for Ocular Surgery France, 1980s Allows 4 degrees of rotation with translation to facilitate precise movements. 2. Prototype robotic system based on the S.M.O.S. platform Japan, 1989 Assists in vitreoretinal surgery and increased accuracy. The prototype eliminates the interoperator variability to enhance precision. 3. Steady hand manipulator for retinal microsurgery John Hopkins University, Baltimore, Maryland, USA, 1999 Reduces tremors, and minimizes undesirable eye wall tension. 4. Multi-arm hybrid robotic system Columbia University, USA, 2009 Offers skillful manipulation of eye. 5. Da Vinci Surgical System The United States, approved in 2000 by FDA Used in performing suture repair of a corneal laceration, executing complete continuous capsulorhexis on the anterior lens capsule, and to perform 3-port 25-gauge pars plana vitrectomy. 6. Hexapod Surgical System France, 2011 Mounted to the da Vinci Macro Robot, this system offers a remote center of motion at the site of ocular penetration. 7. Micro Hand using the micro electromechanical systems technology, (MEMS) USA, 2010 Offers manipulation of fresh retinal tissue and can maneuver caliper weights. It is designed in the same manner as a human hand. 8. Intraocular Robotic Interventional Surgical System Mechatronics and Control Laboratory and Stein Eye Institute, USA 2011 It is designed to facilitate telesurgery using a stereoscopic visualization system, joystick controls, and custom designed arms. This system is equipped with tremor filtration and performs scaled motion. 9. Femtosecond Laser Devices The United States, approved by FDA in 2001 Facilitates cataract surgery and can be used in wound construction, capsulorhexis creation, and nucleus breakdown during the cataract surgery. Open in a new tab Table 2. Studies Published on Robotics and Ophthalmology Name of Author/Year Type of Surgery Robotics & Ophthalmology Comments 1. Tsirbas, A., Mango, C. and Dutson, E., 2007 Bimanual, three-dimensional robotic surgery. Ocular microsurgery was performed using da Vinci Surgical Robot. Ocular microsurgery was performed using a da Vinci surgical robot. Repair of corneal laceration done in a porcine model. Excellent visualization was obtained during the surgery and the sutures were placed delicately at the corneal level with controlled movements. Robotic ocular microsurgery has proven to be successful. Moreover, it can make the procedure more time-efficient. 2. Smet, MD., Naus, GJL., Faridpooya, K., and Mura, M., 2018 Presents overview of intraocular surgery, ex vivo corneal surgery, and semiautonomous surgery Reviewed three robotics used in ophthalmic procedures: steady hand manipulator for retinal microsurgery, Preceyes surgical system for intraocular surgery, and da Vinci Surgical Robot for ex-vivo corneal surgery. Several eye-specific systems have proven to be successful in different ocular surgeries, but only a few of these robotic systems have been able to make it to the commercial platform. Despite the development, robotic systems will take time to get incorporated in the surgical procedures. 3. Hubschman, JP., Tsirbas, A., and Schwartz, SD., 2008 Robotic external ocular surgery was performed using robotic arms. Robotic arms were used to carry out a 25 gauge robotic vitrectomy. Robotic forceps were used to grasp and subsequently remove the foreign body from the anterior chamber. A 360 degree capsulorhexis was achieved through the movement of the robotic arms. Robotic arms are not much feasible to use in ocular surgery. The visualization gets hindered and continuously requires realignment. Conversely, robotic forceps offer more ease in manipulation and control of the instruments. However, the procedure can be improved substantially through miniaturization. 4. Noda Y, Ida Y, and Tanaka S., et al ., 2013 Application of robotic instruments and system for vitreoretinal surgery The capability of robotics application to target the fundus was analyzed in comparison to the manual procedure and performance. Robotic assistance in approaching the target on the fundus, and to stabilize the manipulator tip above the fundus ensures more accuracy and improves the contact with the target. This has proven to be beneficial for both experienced and inexperienced hands. 5. Roizenblatt M, Edwards TL, and Gehlbach PL., 2018 Robot-assisted vitreoretinal surgery Robotic systems considerably suppress tremor during the vitreoretinal surgery and improves the coordination between the surgeon and the robot. Vitrectomy is executed with more precision by robotics when it is combined with intravenous thrombolysis. Improved control, dexterity, diminished tremors, and micron-scale distance sensing has been achieved in vitreoretinal surgery through robotics. However, there are substantial challenges in implementation including costs, clinical risks and complications. 6. Bourla DH, Hubschman JP, Culjat M, et al ., 2008 Intraocular Robot surgery using da Vinci Surgical System. The da Vinci Surgical System was utilized in performing different surgical procedures including 25-gauge pars plana vitrectomy, intraocular foreign body removal, and anterior capsulorhexis. The aim of the study was to assess the robot''s ability to offer control to the surgeon, improve dexterity and maneuverability, and enhance visualization. The da Vinci Surgical System successfully offered complete range of movement. Steady motion with optimum dexterity was achieved. To retain excellent visualization, realignment was required. This highlights the shortcomings of da Vinci Surgical System in ocular surgeries. Open in a new tab The Scope of Robotics in Ophthalmology In ophthalmology, robotics is in its initial stage but with increasing development and research in this area, it is expected to reach a stage where it can be introduced in the normal ophthalmic practices. It can be rational to presume that there are plenty of opportunities for robotics ophthalmic surgeries, especially in intervention performance that is only rendered possible with robotic systems, or might atleast greatly simplify the existing approaches.[ 21 ] Procedures such as intravascular drug delivery and retinal vessel cannulation may become feasible since robotic microsurgical manipulations would be safer with reduced iatrogenic complications. Additionally, advanced imaging integration with robotic systems might also allow motion guidance or total surgical procedures automation. There are numerous more potential pathways of bringing robotics in the field of ophthalmology. Up until now, all the surgical systems enhance the time of surgery. However, there is a need to emphasize cost and efficiency as well. The setup time can be drastically reduced by utilizing a hybrid system implemented on current surgical tables.[ 22 ] Although robotic technology in ophthalmology is still in its startup stages, the trend is expected to follow through consistently and tremendous advancement is anticipated in the field in the coming times. To gain a better understanding of how well the robotics technology is developing in the field, the following section offers a brief overview of the potential benefits and challenges. Potential Benefits of Robotics in Ophthalmology One of the reasons why robotics technology in ophthalmology is highly anticipated is the meticulousness achieved through it. The challenges formerly experienced in form of movement stability are now addressed through the robotics. The surgeons are able to scale both movements and speed at the same time. This has promoted their efficiency in performing delicate tasks.[ 23 , 24 ] Robotic techniques and high-precision instruments have made it achievable for the ophthalmologists to address more complex and accuracy-sensitive conditions. The robotic systems are effective for the purposes that require repetitive tasks, making the work easier for the ophthalmologist.[ 25 ] Micro-incision cataract surgeries that were once deemed difficult to execute, they are now substantially aided with robotic technology such as Femtosecond lasers. These devices are effective in performing precise corneal incision, well-sized perfect capsulorhexis, and nuclear fragmentation and help to improve the outcome of refractive cataract surgery. Remaining steps of phacoemulsification procedures can be performed by the ophthalmic surgeons. In the future, the robotic system can take over the tasks that involve repetitive motions, providing the depth and strength that is ideal for the task to be carried out.[ 26 ] To achieve optimum results, it is important that the surgeon knows well how to operate the robotic system. With this challenge, there are several opportunities rising as well. According to the latest trend, the surgeons are now trained on simulators. The information and steps that are preprogrammed into the simulators and the robot make it easier for the surgeons to execute the transition from one step to another. This makes the work easier for surgeons to perform and in less time.[ 27 ] While performing invasive surgeries, the surgeons have to be careful about their posture and movements as any unwatchful movement can cause substantial damage to the tissue. This has limited the freedom in the movement for the surgeons and has led them to suffer from occupational musculoskeletal disorders. Therefore, it is not just the patient''s safety and treatment that demands advancement in the surgical procedures, but the well-being of the surgeons as well.[ 28 ] Robotic techniques cater to both the demands sufficiently. Where they have improved the quality of treatment for the patients with marginalized risks, they have also granted considerable relaxation and comfort to surgeons. These techniques are able to reduce the physical stress endured during the surgery. It is difficult to predict the exact scope of robotics in ophthalmology, but it can be effectively deduced by comparing the results of robot-performed and human-performed surgeries. There are ongoing research trials to determine how surgical robots can be used in improving the ophthalmological productivity and which procedures can be accelerated without causing any negative impact. Challenges: Robotics in Ophthalmology Robotic-assisted advancement is not sans challenges. There are issues which need to be carefully considered so that they are effectively resolved. Ophthalmological procedures need logical processing for effective execution aside from accuracy. For an ophthalmic surgeon, who is taking the assistance of a robotic system, he/she must be able to carry out the procedure in a sequence of steps that are logical. The devices utilized must be innately perceptive. For an optimally functioning robot, it is important that it functions as a highly skilled and proficient human being.[ 29 ] If the device deviates from the logical progression, it will not achieve the purpose it is designed for. If the robotic surgical device, da Vinci, is taken into consideration, it does allow the surgeon to achieve a magnified view of the eye and perform corneal incision but its frame rigidity does not reciprocate the flexible movements like that of a surgeon.[ 30 ] We can regard da Vinci as one of the indirect devices used in ophthalmology and, ophthalmology, on its own, has a long way to go in robotics technology. The study of Bourla, et al ., however, advocates the suitability of the da Vinci robotic system for ocular surgeries.[ 31 ] There are several issues highlighted in terms of levels of stress at the entry points and in relation to the visualization of the surgical field. These issues denote that the da Vinci robotic system is not completely appropriate as yet for ocular surgeries. With the current and existing progression of surgical robot systems in ophthalmology, it has become difficult to predict how robotic technology is going to proceed. The future is mostly dependent on the clinical trial data that is to be achieved through ongoing pilot studies. Once the relevant data is achieved, more in-depth analysis can be conducted for the scope of robotics in ophthalmology. Several obstacles remain before robotic surgery will become popular in ophthalmology. A high cost, steep learning curve, and patient trust present other challenges in robotics in ophthalmology. At present, considerable studies and trials have been conducted for various robotic systems, only a few of them have made it to the commercial stage and ophthalmology, on its own, has a long way to go in robotics technology. Financial support and sponsorship Nil. Conflicts of interest There are no conflicts of interest. References 1. Kumar R, Hemal AK. Emerging role of robotics in urology. J Min Access Surg. 2005;1:202–10. doi: 10.4103/0972-9941.19268. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Dasgupta P, Challacombe B, Murphy D, Khan MS. Coming full circle in robotic urology. BJU Int. 2006;98:4–5. doi: 10.1111/j.1464-410X.2006.06242.x. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Fine HF, Wei W, Roger Goldman E, Simaan N. Robot-assisted ophthalmic surgery. Can J Ophthalmol. 2010;45:581–4. doi: 10.1139/i10-106. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Ruurda JP, Broeders IAMJ, Simmermacher RPM, Borel Rinkes IH, Van Vroonhoven TJ. Feasibility of robot-assisted laparoscopic surgery: An evaluation of 35 robot-assisted laparoscopic cholecystectomies. Surg Laparosc Endosc Percutan Tech. 2002;12:41–5. doi: 10.1097/00129689-200202000-00007. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Cleary K, Nguyen C. State of the art in surgical robotics: Clinical applications and technology challenges. Comput Aided Surg. 2001;6:312–28. doi: 10.1002/igs.10019. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Lanfranco A R, Castellanos AE, Desai JP, Meyers WC. Robotic surgery: A current perspective. Ann Surg. 2004;239:14–21. doi: 10.1097/01.sla.0000103020.19595.7d. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Jones SB, Jones DB. Surgical aspects and future developments of laparoscopy. Anesthesiol Clin North America. 2001;19:107–24. doi: 10.1016/s0889-8537(05)70214-5. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Tsirbas A, Mango C, Dutson E. Robotic ocular surgery. Br J Ophthalmol. 2007;91:18–21. doi: 10.1136/bjo.2006.096040. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Rahimy E, Wilson J, Tsao TC, Schwartz S, Hubschman JP. Robot-assisted intraocular surgery: Development of the IRISS and feasibility studies in an animal model. Eye. 2013;27:972–8. doi: 10.1038/eye.2013.105. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Liverneaux PA, Hendriks S, Selber JC, Parekattil SJ. Robotically assisted microsurgery: Development of basic skills course. Arch Plast Surg. 2013;40:320–6. doi: 10.5999/aps.2013.40.4.320. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Bourcier T, Nardin M, Sauer A, Gaucher D, Speeg C, Mutter D, et al. Robot-assisted pterygium surgery: Feasibility study in a nonliving porcine model. Transl Vis Sci Technol. 2015;4:9. doi: 10.1167/tvst.4.1.9. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. Bourges JL, Hubschman JP, Wilson J, Prince S, Tsao TC, Schwartz S. Assessment of a hexapod surgical system for robotic micro-macro manipulations in ocular surgery. Ophthalmic Res. 2011;46:25–30. doi: 10.1159/000314719. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. Bourges JL, Hubschman JP, Burt B, Culjat M, Schwartz SD. Robotic microsurgery: Corneal transplantation. Br J Ophthalmol. 2009;93:1672–5. doi: 10.1136/bjo.2009.157594. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. Mitchell B, Koo J, Iordachita I, Kazanzides P, Kapoor A, Handa J, et al. IEEE; Development and application of a new steady-hand manipulator for retinal surgery. InRobotics and Automation, 2007 IEEE International Conference on 2007 Apr 10; pp. 623–9. [ Google Scholar ] 15. Taylor RH, Menciassi A, Fichtinger G, Fiorini P, Dario P. Springer Handbook of Robotics. Cham: Springer; 2016. Medical robotics and computer-integrated surgery; pp. 1657–84. [ Google Scholar ] 16. Smet MD, Naus GJL, Faridpooya K, Mura M. Robotic-assisted surgery in ophthalmology. Curr Opin Ophthalmol. 2018;29:248–53. doi: 10.1097/ICU.0000000000000476. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 17. Jensen PS, Grace KW, Attariwala R, Colgate JE, Glucksberg MR. Toward robot-assisted vascular microsurgery in the retina. Graefes Arch Clin Exp Ophthalmol. 1997;235:696–701. doi: 10.1007/BF01880668. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 18. Dogangil G, Ergeneman O, Abbott JJ, Pané S, Hall H, Muntwyler S, et al. Intelligent Robots and Systems, 2008. IROS 2008. IEEE/RSJ International Conference on 2008 Sep 22. IEEE; Toward targeted retinal drug delivery with wireless magnetic microrobots; pp. 1921–6. [ Google Scholar ] 19. Volpe R. The RAMS arms. Retrieved on 2018 Oct 22. Available from: http://www.robotics.jpl.nasa.gov/systems/ 20. Harris JM, Nefs HT, Grafton CE. Binocular vision and motion-in-depth. Spatial Vis. 2008;21:531–47. doi: 10.1163/156856808786451462. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 21. Marescaux J, Leroy J, Rubino F, Smith M, Vix M, Simone M, et al. Transcontinental robot-assisted remote telesurgery: Feasibility and potential applications. Ann Surg. 2002;235:487–92. doi: 10.1097/00000658-200204000-00005. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 22. Hubschman JP, Tsirbas A, Schwartz SD. Robotic surgery in ophthalmology. Retina Today. 2008:81–4. [ Google Scholar ] 23. Spitznas M. Motorized teleguided stereotactic micromanipulator for vitreous microsurgery. Arch Ophthalmol. 1983;101:623–30. doi: 10.1001/archopht.1983.01040010623020. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 24. Noda Y, Ida Y, Tanaka S, Toyama T, Roggia MF, Tamaki Y, et al. Impact of robotic assistance on precision of vitreoretinal surgical procedures. PloS One. 2013;8:e54116. doi: 10.1371/journal.pone.0054116. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 25. Roizenblatt M, Edwards TL, Gehlbach PL. Robot-assisted vitreoretinal surgery: Current perspectives. Robot Surg. 2018;5:1–11. doi: 10.2147/RSRR.S122301. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 26. Nuzzi R, Brusasco L. State of the art of robotic surgery related to vision: Brain and eye applications of newly available devices. Eye Brain. 2018;10:13–24. doi: 10.2147/EB.S148644. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 27. Pitcher JD, Wilson JT, Tsao TC, Schwartz SD, Hubschman JP. Robotic eye surgery: Past, present, and future. J Comput Sci Syst Biol. 2012;S3:001. doi: 10.4172/jcsb.S3-001. [ Google Scholar ] 28. Grozdanovic D, Grozdanovic M. Research on key indicators in modern surgical practice assessment: Ergonomic approach. Surg Innov. 2018;25:323–32. doi: 10.1177/1553350618772720. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 29. Asimov I, Robot I. Robot series. Available from: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Three_Laws_of_Robotics . 30. Takács A, Nagy DÁ, Rudas I, Haidegger T. Origins of surgical robotics: From space to the operating room. Acta Polytech Hung. 2016;13:13–30. [ Google Scholar ] 31. Bourla DH, Hubschman JP, Culjat M, Tsirbas A, Gupta A, Schwartz SD. Feasibility study of intraocular robotic surgery with the da Vinci surgical system. Retina. 2008;28:154–8. doi: 10.1097/IAE.0b013e318068de46. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',13,'2025-10-14 10:15:34','2025-10-14 10:15:34');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(2857,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12201925','The Impact of the COVID-19 Pandemic on Ophthalmology Conferences.','Schmuter G, Kolomeyer NN, Nijm LM, Tao J, Subramanian PS, Al-Mohtaseb Z, Kim T, Rachitskaya AV, Law JC, Tooley AA.','2025','underexplored acceptances oculofacial cumulatively acceptances acceptances acceptances cumulatively acceptances aleksandra rachitskaya alcon apellis iveric apellis agtc apellis gabriella schmuter tooley natasha kolomeyer nijm jeremiah zaina mohtaseb aleksandra rachitskaya janice uxo9002 nyp gurnani chn singap sridhar wykoff yonekawa widefield swept uxo9002 nyp','Abstract Introduction The COVID-19 pandemic prompted ophthalmology conferences to transition to virtual platforms. The impact of these changes on attendance and research productivity remains underexplored. The objective of this study was to evaluate the effects of virtual platforms on attendance and research inclusion at ophthalmology conferences during the COVID-19 pandemic. Methods This cross-sectional study analyzed data from 5 ophthalmology conference organizations conducted from 2019 to 2021. Conference meetings were categorized as in-person, virtual, or hybrid. Data included total attendance, trainee participation, and research submissions, obtained directly from each respective organization. Results Conference meetings integrating a virtual component experienced a cumulative 7% increase in total attendance and a 28% rise in trainee participation. Research submissions rose by 31%, while research acceptances showed variability, with increases observed in some conferences during the virtual years. These trends highlight the potential for virtual platforms to enhance accessibility and engagement, particularly for trainees. Conclusion Virtual platforms had an overall positive impact on ophthalmology conference attendance and research engagement during the COVID-19 pandemic. Continued integration of virtual components is recommended to enhance accessibility and inclusivity. Keywords: Ophthalmology, Conferences, COVID-19, Virtual conferences, Medical education Introduction The COVID-19 pandemic uniquely disrupted all aspects of daily life, particularly within healthcare, necessitating novel adaptations such as virtual and telehealth platforms [ 1 ]. In this context, online platforms became increasingly important to maintain educational and professional continuity [ 2 ]. Ophthalmology conferences, integral to education and collaboration, were either canceled or transitioned to virtual formats during the pandemic. These conferences play a vital role in fostering collaboration, research dissemination, and professional development for trainees and practitioners alike [ 3 ]. Recent studies have also highlighted the use of teleophthalmology and ancillary diagnostic technologies such as optical coherence tomography during the pandemic to maintain care standards [ 4 ]. However, the impact of these transitions on conference attendance and educational value remains uncertain. This study aimed to characterize the changes in attendance and research productivity associated with the integration of virtual platforms into conference meetings before, during, and after the peak of the COVID-19 pandemic. Methods This cross-sectional study analyzed data from five US-based ophthalmology conference organizations, encompassing a total of 15 conference meetings held between 2019 and 2021. To maintain confidentiality, the conferences have been anonymized in this study and have each been assigned a letter (A–E). Conferences represented diverse subspecialties: glaucoma, cataract/anterior segment, oculofacial plastics, neuro-ophthalmology, and women’s ophthalmology. Conferences were included if they provided data on attendance and research submissions for three consecutive years (2019–2021) and successfully transitioned to a virtual or hybrid format at some point during or after the peak of the COVID-19 pandemic. For this study, the peak of the COVID-19 pandemic is defined as the period of time around March of 2020, the period during which national lockdowns were implemented across the USA [ 5 ]. Conferences canceled during the study period or unable to provide complete data were excluded. Data were obtained directly from conference organizers and included total attendance, trainee participation, and research submissions (submission and acceptance rates). The data were aggregated records provided by each organization. There was no independent assessor or randomization performed, and interrater reliability statistics were not applicable. To encompass broader forms of research dissemination, the term research submissions was used instead of abstracts. Institutional Review Board exemption was obtained for this study. The data used in this study were obtained directly from the individual conference organizations included in the analysis. Access to the data may be subject to restrictions by the respective organizations, and each respective conference organization must be contacted separately to obtain their data. Institutional Review Board exemption was obtained. For privacy purposes, the names of the conference organizations have been anonymized. These data are not publicly available online. Results Fifteen conference meetings were analyzed: seven in-person, five virtual, and three hybrid events ( Table 1 ). Overall, conferences integrating a virtual component experienced a 7% increase in total attendance. Trainee attendance rose by 28%. A graphical representation of total conference attendees throughout 2019 to 2021 is provided in Figure 1 . Research submissions cumulatively increased by 31%, while research acceptances varied: an increase of 6.3% in 2020 followed by a decline in 2021. Table 1. Ophthalmology conference data from 2019–2021 Conference Outcome Year 2019 percent change 2020 percent change 2021 A In-person % In-person % Virtual Total attendees 1,192 1 1,205 15 1,383 Total trainees 195 7 208 118 454 Submitted abstracts 230 3 224 −22 174 Accepted abstracts 169 4 176 −42 102 B In-person Percent change Virtual Percent change Hybrid Total attendees 3,821 −35 2,485 −13 2,143 Total trainees 402 21 488 −22 381 Submitted abstracts 1,686 5 1,770 −32 1,201 Accepted abstracts 1,364 9 1,481 −23 1,144 C In-person Percent change Virtual Percent change Hybrid Total attendees 959 −14 829 −1 823 Total trainees 150 −50 75 65 124 Submitted abstracts 364 −21 287 1 290 Accepted abstracts 217 −18 177 46 258 D In-person Percent change In-person Percent change Virtual Total attendees 663 −27 486 108 1,009 Total trainees 161 −20 129 130 297 Submitted abstracts 340 14 387 −32 265 Accepted abstracts 290 14 332 −26 247 E In-person Percent change Virtual Percent change Hybrid Total attendees 497 51 751 13 846 Total trainees 106 57 166 −16 140 Submitted abstracts 158 23 195 39 272 Accepted abstracts 144 8 156 60 250 Open in a new tab Events listed in 2020 took place prior to government stay-at-home orders and lockdown restrictions around March 15, 2020. Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Total conference attendance for conferences A – E from 2019 to 2021. Specific trends among conferences were observed: Conference A experienced a 15% increase in total attendance and a 118% rise in trainee participation when transitioning to a fully virtual format in 2021, despite decreases in research submissions and acceptances. Conference B demonstrated a 35% decline in total attendance during its fully virtual year in 2020, yet showed a 21% increase in trainee participation. Conference C observed declines in attendance during its virtual year, followed by partial recovery with a hybrid format. Conference D recorded a dramatic 108% increase in total attendance and a 130% rise in trainee participation with a fully virtual format in 2021. Conference E demonstrated consistent growth, including a 60% increase in research acceptances and a notable rise in attendance when transitioning to a hybrid model in 2021. Discussion This study highlights how virtual platforms influenced accessibility and participation at ophthalmology conferences during the COVID-19 pandemic. Total attendance increased by 7%, and trainee participation rose by 28%. Research submissions increased cumulatively by 31%. These findings suggest that virtual formats improved accessibility for trainees and may facilitate broader research dissemination. However, variability in research acceptance and attendance trends underscores that virtual models may not benefit all conferences equally. Our findings support the integration of virtual components into conference planning to promote accessibility, inclusivity, and continuous medical education, especially for trainees and international participants. Limitations include limited parameters assessed, variability in baseline conference sizes, and lack of comparison to conferences in other medical fields. Future studies should explore specific barriers to attendance and the efficacy of hybrid versus in-person models. Future virtual conference models may benefit from similar principles: accessibility, quality, and inclusivity. Conclusion The inclusion of virtual components in ophthalmology conferences during the COVID-19 pandemic was associated with increased attendance and research submissions. While research acceptances showed variability, specific conferences demonstrated notable gains. Further research is needed to fully understand the factors driving these trends, especially the rise in trainee participation, and may include international conferences for study. Adopting hybrid models moving forward may maximize educational opportunities while preserving the unique benefits of in-person interactions, ultimately supporting ophthalmologists’ continuous education and professional development worldwide. Statement of Ethics This study protocol was reviewed and approved by the Institutional Review Board at the City University School of Medicine, approval no Protocol #2021-0038. This study did not involve direct interaction with human participants, as the data analyzed were aggregated and anonymized conference attendance and research submission records provided by the respective conference organizations. Written informed consent was not required as determined by the Institutional Review Board at the City University School of Medicine, which granted an exemption for this study (Protocol #2021-0038). This research was conducted ethically in accordance with the principles outlined in the World Medical Association Declaration of Helsinki. Conflict of Interest Statement Aleksandra V. Rachitskaya, MD – consulting: Alcon, Genentech, Apellis, Iveric Bio, Zeiss; speaking: Apellis, Genentech, and Regeneron; research: AGTC, Apellis, Genentech, and NGM Bio. The other authors report there are no competing interests to declare. Funding Sources This research was conducted without the support of any external funding. No sponsors were involved in the study design, execution, data analysis, manuscript conception, planning, writing, or the decision to publish the findings. Author Contributions All authors listed meet all conditions to qualify for authorship as listed by the ICMJE. Gabriella Schmuter, MD, and Andrea A. Tooley, MD: conceptualization, data curation, formal analysis, investigation, methodology, validation, and writing. Natasha Nayak Kolomeyer, MD: data curation, formal analysis, investigation, methodology, validation, and writing. Lisa M. Nijm, MD, JD; Jeremiah Tao, MD; Prem S. Subramanian, MD, PhD; Zaina Al-Mohtaseb, MD; Terry Kim, MD; Aleksandra V. Rachitskaya, MD; and Janice C. Law, MD: data curation, investigation, validation, and writing – review and editing. Funding Statement This research was conducted without the support of any external funding. No sponsors were involved in the study design, execution, data analysis, manuscript conception, planning, writing, or the decision to publish the findings. Data Availability Statement The data used in this study were obtained directly from the individual conference organizations included in the analysis. Access to the data may be subject to restrictions by the respective organizations, and each respective conference organization must be contacted separately to obtain their data. For privacy purposes, the names of the conference organizations have been anonymized. These data are not publicly available online to maintain confidentiality and privacy of each organization. However, the data may be available upon reasonable request. Requests can be directed to the corresponding author at uxo9002@nyp.org . References 1. Gurnani B, Kaur K. Impact of the COVID-19 pandemic on clinical ophthalmology. Indian J Med Res. 2021;153(1 & 2):199–200. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Chong JC, Tan CHN, Chen DZ. Teleophthalmology and its evolving role in a COVID-19 pandemic: a scoping review. Ann Acad Med Singap. 2021;50(1):61–76. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Gupta MP, Sridhar J, Wykoff CC, Yonekawa Y. Ophthalmology conferences in the coronavirus disease 2019 era. Curr Opin Ophthalmol. 2020;31(5):396–402. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Garg I, Katz R, Lu Y, Miller JB. Remote imaging capture with widefield swept-source OCT angiography during the COVID-19 pandemic. Clin Ophthalmol. 2022;16:477–86. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention . CDC museum COVID-19 timeline. Published March 2022: https://www.cdc.gov/museum/timeline/covid19.html (accessed April 27, 2025). Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Data Availability Statement The data used in this study were obtained directly from the individual conference organizations included in the analysis. Access to the data may be subject to restrictions by the respective organizations, and each respective conference organization must be contacted separately to obtain their data. For privacy purposes, the names of the conference organizations have been anonymized. These data are not publicly available online to maintain confidentiality and privacy of each organization. However, the data may be available upon reasonable request. Requests can be directed to the corresponding author at uxo9002@nyp.org .',7,'2025-10-14 10:15:43','2025-10-14 10:15:43');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(2858,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11653905','Career choice of Saudi medical students: opting for ophthalmology residency programs - perceptions and sociodemographic influencing factors - a cross-sectional study in Northern Saudi Arabia.','Almulhim A, Thirunavukkarasu A, Alanazi B, Alfaleh AS, Alreshidi SO, Almusib RBA, Alfaleh LAZ.','2024','fourths madani aldebasi nationals jouf raosoft raosoft lcbe jouf permissions briefed scopes scopes scopes covariables fif approximately11 onyekwe nwosu compel gameiro scopes parija gameiro parija madinah gpas deanship jouf dgssr jouf khathlan aab eyenet alasiri leenen vision2030 vision2030 vrp alharbi alhazmi e11991 alrabghi abudungor alsulaiman najjar manjoumi e40170 ghamdi bryar michaelov waterman sharan ariff faiz dareshani dow bahria aljuhani abdulaziz alharbi madinah e44936 madani alasiri howldar zagzoog alhibshi borie qattan revitalize provis mohiuddin nasr nadhmi miladi shahwar noorulhasan naveed potentialities aldebasi alhejji bukhari alawad alghaihab alakel raosoft raosoft raosoft raosoft samplesize abdulsalim farooqui alshammari alotaibi alhazmi alqasomi qassim shomrani hamouda baha e56871 sudershan panjaliya dhar algethami alsaedi alahmadi bardisi khoshhal e20844 tavakol dennick morera keto tilahun mamo metu jabbar noor zahra irum manzoor alanazi thirunavukkarasu alrowily alaqel alaqel alruwaili alfaisal alfaisal dau whens gameiro darcie alf hazaki gameiro carricondo e1101 bongomin olum kajjimu kanyike atulinda ninsiima onyekwe nwosu snn nnamdi azikiwe nnewi wingert e129 e37 ahmady namazi zarei mirmoghtadaei hamidi bashir mctaggart taibah leman claramita rahayu multidiscip premji punnett e56 bechara lindor parija poddar alaqeel alhammad basuhail alderaan alhawamdeh alquhayz gibney cantwell wray boysen wiechmann saadat e33592','Abstract Background and aim The present survey aimed to explore northern Saudi medical students’ perceptions and sociodemographic influencing factors in choosing ophthalmology as a career specialty to determine if they are in alignment with the health sector transformation program of the Vision 2030 initiative. Methods This study was conducted among 384 medical students from two universities in northern Saudi Arabia (KSA). We used a standard and validated questionnaire to collect the required data. The perception aspect consisted of 20 questions divided into five subdomains: personal and lifestyle factors, residency program aspects, patients’ care, work culture, and financial factors. We used binomial logistic regression to find sociodemographic predictors for choosing ophthalmology as the first choice. Results The present study demonstrated that northern Saudi medical students preferred ophthalmology as the second most preferred specialty (11.2%). Among the motivational factors, the most frequently reported was the influence of faculty members from their college (74.2%), followed by financial aspects (74%) and family and friends (73.7%). The preference to choose ophthalmology was significantly higher among female gender (ref: male, AOR = 2.63, 95% CI = 1.39–4.88, p = 0.001) and students with a GPA of 4.6 and above (ref: GPA less than 3.5, AOR = 3.77, 95% CI = 2.99–5.36, p = 0.023). Conclusion Northern Saudi medical students’ preference for ophthalmology aligns with Vision 2030 goals. Therefore, it is essential to tailor strategies by policymakers to the medical students according to the identified sociodemographic influencing factors. Furthermore, we recommend prospective studies across all the regions of KSA to identify regional variations. Keywords: Ophthalmology, Residency program, Vision 2030, Career, Specialty, Medical student Introduction Ophthalmologists play an integral role in the healthcare system, and they offer essential care for visual health, which greatly impacts the quality of life and productivity. Their contribution is invaluable for the management of both common and rare ocular diseases [ 1 , 2 ]. Furthermore, due to the nature and widespread frequency of eye disorders, the patient care required from an ophthalmologist lasts for many years, as stated by the American Academy of Ophthalmology [ 3 ]. The recently implemented health sector transformation program under Vision 2030 by the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia (KSA) necessitates the assessment of medical education programs and necessary changes that help the students to choose a specialty of their choice and need for the country [ 4 – 6 ]. Vision 2030 was launched in the KSA as a part of the national developmental plan. One of the important pillars of this initiative is the national health sector transformation program, which aims to improve the quality and accessibility of medical services in the country, in turn, improving public health and preventing diseases, including ophthalmology-related diseases [ 6 ]. In the KSA, vision-related problems such as diabetic retinopathy, cataracts, glaucoma, dry eye disorders, and age-related macular degeneration are widely prevalent. Moreover, the aging population, increasing screen time among children, and rising prevalence of diabetes mellitus in KSA, several ophthalmologic problems are expected to increase in the future among adults and children [ 7 – 9 ]. These statements indicate the necessity of having more ophthalmologists in the future for the nation’s health transformation program and change in healthcare needs, as ophthalmologists play vital role in delivering care for these problems [ 10 , 11 ]. Most of the time, undergraduate medical students select their future career specialty during their study period in medical school. Hence, the medical school curriculum and experience play a vital role in encouraging the students to choose the specialty, such as ophthalmology, that the KSA requires to meet the change in healthcare needs. According to some of the authors, the process of selecting ophthalmology as the residence program for medical students is multifaceted and influenced by numerous factors [ 12 – 14 ]. These factors are generally divided into personal factors and work-related factors. A recent study conducted among Canadian medical students in 2022 by Li et al. reported that more than three-fourths of questions in their survey that is related to ophthalmology had positive responses, and the positive opinion was significantly associated with the student’s interest and exposure to ophthalmology during medical school [ 12 ]. According to Madani et al., the workload during the residency training program was one of the critical determinants of selecting an ophthalmology residence program [ 15 ]. Considering the ever-changing desire of medical students to select the residency program, continuous assessment of these factors is essential. The literature search about Saudi Vision 2030 and the Saudi health workforce in Google Scholar and other major databases revealed that numerous authors explored various aspects of Vision 2030, including policies and demand for other specialties [ 16 – 18 ]. However, limited research focuses on medical students’ career preferences in ophthalmology within the context of this health sector transformation program. In fact, a recent study in 2024 by Aldebasi et al. stated that only 38% of ophthalmologists are Saudi nationals, and the study highlights disparities in the geographic distribution of ophthalmologists [ 19 ]. Furthermore, given the regional variations, in the context of the Vision 2030 Program of Saudi Arabia, which emphasizes the importance of matching the development of healthcare workers with the nation’s health needs, a comprehensive analysis of medical students’ perceptions and associated sociodemographic factors to take ophthalmology as a first choice need to be evaluated. Also, there is a need for region-specific data for policymakers and educational institutions. Hence, the present survey aimed to explore northern Saudi medical students’ perceptions and sociodemographic influencing factors in choosing ophthalmology as a career specialty to determine if they are in alignment with the health sector transformation program of the Vision 2030 initiative. Materials and methods Study description The present study is a cross-sectional study conducted from 15 September 2023 to 15 February 2024. It was done among medical students from two universities in the northern KSA regions. The selected universities were Jouf University and Northern Border University. The present study included all medical students from years 2 to 5 and interns, excluding those unwilling to participate and first-year students. Sampling strategies The present study calculated the sample size using Raosoft online sample size calculator [ 20 ]. This calculator has been used by numerous authors in the research [ 21 – 23 ]. Raosoft uses the principles of Cochran’s sample size estimator formula ( n = z 2 pq/d 2 ). In this formula, n = minimum required medical students for this study, p = expected proportion, q = 1 – p, and d = 5% margin of error. We have taken the expected proportion as 50% to get the maximum number of required participants. Considering these values, the expected number of participants for the study was estimated as 384. Furthermore, the number of students selected from each university was based on the total number of medical students enrolled at that institution. In the following stages, the required number of students from each academic year was selected based on the total number of students in that academic year using systematic random sampling (according to their registration number order). Data collection procedures We started the data collection process after obtaining the clearance letter from the College of Medicine Scientific Committee and ethical approval from LCBE, Jouf University (approval no: 2-04-45). The concerned authorities of the selected college of medicine from the chosen university were approached, and necessary permissions were obtained. The required number of students was selected after obtaining permission from the concerned authorities (as described in the sampling strategies). Initially, our team contacted the student leader of each class to find out the availability of the students. To ensure availability and increase the response rate, the data collectors approached medical students during the self-directed learning period. Moreover, efforts have been made to avoid data collection one week before the exam and during exam time. The data collectors started to collect the data using a standard and validated questionnaire from the students after briefing the study. The authors used the details of the institute-approved informed consent form. The data collectors briefed the participants on the study’s aim and steps to be followed during participation (including the right to withdraw at any time) and the duration of the data collection form. Furthermore, the participants were informed about the anonymity and privacy of data and the overall benefits of the study results for the healthcare sectors. After briefing these details, informed consent was obtained from all participants who were willing to participate. We made the Google form, and the medical students were requested to complete the Google form on the data collectors’ electronic devices. The medical education and ophthalmology faculties prepared this questionnaire through a focus group discussion based on existing pieces of literature [ 12 , 15 , 24 ]. The questionnaire consists of three parts. In the first part, we inquired about sociodemographic characteristics such as age, gender, year of education, grade point average (GPA), taking ophthalmology as an elective during the internship, and their participation in research related to ophthalmology. The second part inquired about the medical student’s first choice of residency training program and future career options. Those who wished to choose ophthalmology responded to the questions related to motivational factors to choose ophthalmology. The third part consisted of twenty questions about participants’ perception of choosing ophthalmology as their career choice. These twenty questions were divided into five subdomains, such as personal and lifestyle factors (3 items), residency program aspects (4 items), patients’ care (5 items), work culture (4 items), and financial factors (4 items). The selected medical students responded on a 5-point Likert’s scale ranging from strongly agree (5 points) to strongly disagree (1 point). We tested the prepared questionnaire among 30 students, and their feedback was that the data collection form was easy and that a student could complete it within 10 min. The Cronbach alpha value of the questionnaire obtained through the pretest analysis was 0.83, indicating a high level of internal consistency of the tool used in the study [ 25 , 26 ]. Data analysis The research team completed all descriptive and inferential statistical analysis using Statistical Package for Social Sciences (SPSS), IBM Version 21.0, a version widely used by researchers in similar studies [ 27 – 29 ]. The analysis involves descriptive statistics (frequency, proportion, mean, standard deviation) and inferential statistics. The descriptive data, including students’ background characteristics and Likert’s scale responses in perception items, is shown as number and frequency (n, %). Next, a comparison of the responses among each item of the students’ perceptions between those who wished to choose ophthalmology as their first preference and those who did not select it was completed using an independent t-test. Finally, we used binomial logistic regression to find the sociodemographic predictors for choosing ophthalmology as the first choice. All applied statistical tests were two-tailed, and a p -value less than 0.05 was considered a statistically significant value. Results During the data collection period, we contacted 465 medical students to participate in the survey to get a minimum sample size of 384 (response rate: 82.6%). Among the participants, the majority of the respondents belonged to the age group of 21 to 23 years, with a mean age of 22.28. Regarding exposure to ophthalmology, 41.7% completed ophthalmology courses in their undergraduate, 9.6% took ophthalmology elective training during an internship, and 20.9% participated in ophthalmology research (Table 1 ). Table 1. Sociodemographic characteristics of the study population ( n = 384) Variable Frequency Proportion Age (years) (mean ± SD) 22.28 ± 1.99 Less than 20 109 28.4 21 to 23 145 37.8 24 and above 130 33.8 Gender Male 191 49.7 Female 193 50.3 Marital status: Single 377 98.2 Currently married 7 1.8 Education status: Second years 68 17.7 Third year 96 25 Fourth year 95 24.7 Fifth year 57 14.8 Medical intern 68 17.7 Grade Point Average (GPA) 3.5 or less 72 18.8 3.6 to 4 104 27.1 4.1 to 4.5 109 28.4 4.6 and above 99 25.7 Completed an ophthalmology course in your medical school? No 224 58.3 Yes 160 41.7 Ophthalmology elective training status Intern - No 32 8.3 Intern - Yes 36 9.4 I am still a medical student (Not applicable) 316 82.3 Participated in any ophthalmology research project. No 304 79.2 Yes, and published 31 8.1 Yes, and not published 49 12.8 Any doctor in your immediate family member No 262 68.2 Yes 122 31.8 Any ophthalmologist in your immediate family member No 366 95.3 Yes 18 4.7 Open in a new tab Regarding the students’ first preference for a residency program, 13.3% preferred internal Medicine. Ophthalmology was the next (11.2%), followed by family medicine (9.9%), general surgery (9.4%), and pediatrics (7.3%). The total proportion in the figure may not be 100% as we did not depict 16.7% of the students’ responses who did not decide their specialty residency program (Fig. 1 ). Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Medical students’ first preference for their residency program and future career ( n = 384) Of the 384 participants, the highest proportion of the motivational factor was a faculty member (s) of their college, followed by financial aspects, family, and friends, and taking electives during the internship. Interestingly, more than two-thirds of the students responded yes to the requirement for the “Saudi Vision 2030 program” (Fig. 2 ). Fig. 2. Open in a new tab Motivational factors to select ophthalmology as the primary choice for residency program ( n = 43) Regarding medical students’ perceptions of ophthalmology as a career choice, we observed the agreement as follows: 71.9% agreed on research and academic activities available in a residency program, 71.6% of them responded ophthalmologists get adequate personal time, and 69.8% agreed on the ratio of in and outpatients’ care. Regarding financial and future scope domain agreement, 69.1% responded that sufficient job opportunities are available (Table 2 ). Table 2. Medical students’ responses to perception questions (five domains) ( n = 384) Items Strongly agree n (%) Agree n (%) Neutral n (%) Disagree n (%) Strongly disagree n (%) Personal and Lifestyle Factors Ophthalmologists get adequate personal time 136 (35.4) 139 (36.2) 95 (24.7) 7 (1.8) 7 (1.8) It is my personal choice (without any external influence). 108 (28.1) 165 (43.0) 88 (22.9) 17 (4.4) 6 (1.6) Good lifestyle 123 (32.0) 122 (31.8) 127 (33.1) 7 (1.8) 5 (1.3) Residency program-related aspects Duration of residency program 154 (40.1) 122 (31.8) 87 (22.7) 15 (3.9) 6 (1.6) Easy course concepts 127 (33.1) 115 (29.9) 128 (33.3) 9 (2.3) 5 (1.3) Competitiveness 135 (35.2) 107 (27.9) 107 (27.9) 24 (6.3) 11 (2.9) Research and academic activities are very high 169 (44.0) 107 (27.9) 94 (24.5) 8 (2.1) 6 (1.6) Patients’ care Long-term relationship with the patients for their care. 121 (31.5) 128 (33.3) 120 (31.3) 9 (2.3) 6 (1.6) Diverse patient population 213 (55.5) 69 (18.0) 88 (22.9) 5 (1.3) 9 (2.3) Good patients’ prognosis 118 (30.7) 118 (30.7) 115 (29.9) 16 (4.2) 17 (4.4) Diversity of patient care 130 (33.9) 91 (23.7) 114 (29.7) 40 (10.4) 9 (2.3) Good ratio of inpatient to outpatient care. 144 (37.5) 124 (32.3) 100 (26.0) 10 (2.6) 6 (1.6) Work culture Perfect work hours 105 (27.3) 126 (32.8) 131 (34.1) 11 (2.9) 11 (2.9) Excellent balance between clinic and operating room time. 161 (41.9) 80 (20.8) 119 (31.0) 12 (3.1) 12 (3.1) Multidisciplinary care 86 (22.4) 105 (27.3) 161 (41.9) 21 (5.5) 11 (2.9) Prestigious career 110 (28.6) 124 (32.3) 117 (30.5) 22 (5.7) 11 (2.9) Financial and Future Scope Earning potentials are very high 98 (25.5) 132 (34.4) 123 (32.0) 23 (6.0) 8 (2.1) There are adequate job opportunities for graduating Ophthalmologists. 168 (43.8) 97 (25.3) 107 (27.9) 10 (2.6) 2 (0.5) Various scopes are available to practice 153 (39.8) 82 (21.4) 134 (34.9) 13 (3.4) 2 (0.5) Vision 2030 requirements 105 (27.3) 92 (24.0) 165 (43.0) 16 (4.2) 6 (1.6) Open in a new tab Of the students who wished ophthalmology as their first preference had significantly higher mean scores in personal choice ( p = 0.001), duration of residence training ( p = 0.0.007), competitiveness ( p = 0.001), diverse patient population ( p = 0.005), and care ( p = 0.0013), and available practice scopes ( p = 0.001) than those who did not wish to select ophthalmology as first choice (Table 3 ). Table 3. Comparison of perception scores between students who preferred ophthalmology as a first choice and those who did not. The data (score) presented here is mean (standard deviation [SD]) and p -value Items Overall Mean (SD) First choice Mean (SD) Not a first choice Mean (SD) p -value* Personal and Lifestyle Factors Ophthalmologists get adequate personal time 3.91 (0.91) 3.93 (0.88) 3.91 (0.92) 0.902 It is my personal choice (without any external influence). 3.95 (1.07) 4.58 (0.93) 3.87 (1.06) 0.001 Good lifestyle 3.91 (0.93) 3.93 (0.83) 3.91 (0.95) 0.889 Residency program-related aspects. Duration of residency program 4.05 (0.96) 4.42 (0.76) 4.00 (0.98) 0.007 Easy course concepts 4.23 (1.00) 4.47 (0.91) 4.20 (1.01) 0.100 Competitiveness 3.71 (0.97) 4.25 (0.82) 3.64 (0.96) 0.001 Research and academic activities are very high 3.97 (0.97) 4.42 (0.91) 3.91 (0.96) 0.001 Patients’ care Long-term relationship with patients for their care. 3.86 (1.06) 4.26 (1.07) 3.81 (1.05) 0.009 Diverse patient population 4.11 (0.95) 4.49 (0.74) 4.06 (0.96) 0.005 Good patients’ prognosis 3.91 (0.92) 4.26 (0.69) 3.87 (0.94) 0.009 Diversity of patient care 4.02 (0.94) 4.35 (0.81) 3.97 (0.95) 0.013 Good ratio of inpatient to outpatient care. 3.79 (0.97) 3.93 (0.91) 3.77 (0.98) 0.313 Work culture Perfect work hours 4.02 (0.92) 4.19 (0.76) 3.99 (0.93) 0.196 Excellent balance between clinic and operating room time. 4.09 (0.93) 4.49 (0.74) 4.04 (0.94) 0.003 Multidisciplinary care 3.61 (0.99) 3.74 (0.93) 3.59 (0.99) 0.341 Prestigious career 3.79 (1.06) 3.44 (1.18) 3.84 (1.04) 0.22 Financial and Future Scope Earning potentials are very high 3.76 (1.10) 3.49 (1.22) 3.80 (1.08) 0.082 There are adequate job opportunities for graduating Ophthalmologists. 3.78 (1.02) 4.00 (1.05) 3.75 (1.01) 0.134 Various scopes are available to practice 3.92 (0.91) 4.47 (0.63) 3.85 (0.91) 0.001 Vision 2030 requirements 3.75 (0.97) 4.35 (0.75) 3.68 (0.97) 0.001 Open in a new tab * Test applied - independent t-test Binomial logistic regression revealed that preference to choose ophthalmology was significantly higher among female gender (ref: male, AOR = 2.63, 95% CI = 1.39–4.88, p = 0.001), students with GPA of 4.6 and above (ref: GPA less 3.5, AOR = 3.77, 95% CI = 2.99–5.36, p = 0.023), those who opted elective training in ophthalmology (ref: no, AOR = 1.54, 95% CI = 1.09–2.14, p = 0.016), and the students who involved in ophthalmology research publication (ref: no, AOR = 2.69, 95% CI = 1.33–4.07, p = 0.009) (Table 4 ). Table 4. Binary logistic regression analysis of sociodemographic factors for choosing ophthalmology as the first specialty preference ( n = 384) Parameter Total n = 384 Ophthalmology first choice Adjusted odds ratio (AOR)* (95% Confidence interval) p -value Yes n = 43 No n = 341 Age group Less than 20 109 6 103 Ref 21 to 23 145 22 123 1.35 (0.40–3.71) 0.622 24 and above 130 15 115 1.14 (0.81–3.18) 0.845 Gender Male 191 13 178 Ref Female 193 30 163 2.63 (1.39–4.88) 0.001 Education status: Basic science (year 2 and year 3) 164 13 151 Ref Fourth year 95 13 82 1.14 (0.37–3.53) 0.824 Fifth year 57 9 48 0.30 (0.11–1.86) 0.196 Medical intern 68 8 60 0.93 (0.61–2.87) 0.713 GPA 3.5 or less 72 5 67 Ref 3.6 to 4 104 8 96 1.95 (0.87–3.68) 0.165 4.1 to 4.5 109 11 98 3.12 (1.51–5.07) 0.042 4.6 and above 99 19 80 3.77 (2.99–5.36) 0.023 Completed an ophthalmology course in your medical school? No 224 18 206 Ref Yes 160 25 135 1.81 (0.78–3.62) 0.353 Ophthalmology elective training status No 32 5 27 Ref Yes 36 10 26 1.54 (1.09–2.14) 0.016 I am still medical student 316 28 288 0.87 (0.51–3.28) 0.763 Participated in any ophthalmology research project. No 304 17 287 Ref Yes, and published 31 11 20 2.69 (1.33–4.07) 0.009 Yes, and not published 49 15 34 3.87 (1.74–5.38) 0.004 Any doctor in your immediate family member No 262 27 235 Ref Yes 122 16 106 0.55 (0.21–1.48) 0.24 Any ophthalmologist in your immediate family member No 366 37 329 Ref Yes 18 6 12 4.21 (2.81–5.64) 0.007 Open in a new tab * Test applied – Binomial logistic regression analysis (enter method); Adjusted with the other covariables of the study. Discussion Choosing the residency program that is their future career among medical students is a challenging task involving several factors. The number of available specialists in medical and surgical specializations, including the ophthalmology department, is crucial in the KSA, as it needs to be in accordance with the ambitious Vision 2030 national health sector transformation program [ 6 ]. In the KSA, medical school studies consisted of 6 years (excluding the preparatory year), in which the first three years are basic sciences, the next two years (four th and fif th ) are clinical sciences, and the last year will be internship training in clinical settings [ 30 , 31 ]. It is worth mentioning that medical students’ right time to choose a specialty is during medical school, particularly the end of the third year, as stated by the American Medical Association [ 32 ]. The present study examined the perceptions and factors of northern Saudi medical students in choosing an ophthalmology residency program. A detailed evaluation among 384 students from two universities in the northern region revealed that internal medicine had the highest percentage of students who wished to pursue their residency program. Approximately11% of the students preferred the ophthalmology residency program as their first choice. Ophthalmology specialty is one of undergraduate medical students’ one of the highly preferred residency programs in the KSA and some other countries [ 12 , 13 , 24 , 33 , 34 ]. Nonetheless, our study found that it was a higher proportion than other studies [ 13 , 24 , 35 ]. A Nigerian study by Onyekwe and Nwosu reported that 16% of their participants responded that the ophthalmology specialty is their future career [ 36 ]. Notably, the high positive intention among Saudi medical students towards ophthalmology is particularly relevant in Vision 2030 contexts, indicating the alignments between the nation’s priorities and medical students’ aspirations [ 6 ]. Furthermore, it could be due to perceived professional fulfillment, the nature of ophthalmology practice, and personal choices [ 12 , 37 ]. Another contributing factor to the higher proportion of people who prefer ophthalmology is motivation from the college faculties. In the present study, the highest ratio of motivational factors was a faculty member (s) of their college, followed by financial aspects, family, and friends, and taking electives during the internship. The possible factor for the family and friends’ role in selecting a specialty could be, perhaps, a cultural factor in KSA, as the family seems to have a significant influence on the decision. This might be attributed to work-life balance and possible advancement found working within the health sector under Vision 2030’s transformation program. Therefore, educators and policymakers must take into consideration the effects that the family and friends’ roles have had before they set out to make policies that would compel students to choose a specialty as the nation demands. Our findings and other research findings indicate that the college’s faculties are not only teachers but also can be role models for medical students through their professionalism, work ethics, clinical skills, and patient care [ 38 – 40 ]. Similar to our study, Yu et al. from China reported that job opportunities, financial benefits, and passion for ophthalmology were the primary motivating factors influencing Chinese medical students to choose ophthalmology as a career [ 35 ]. According to Sud et al., personal and professional aspects and passion were the factors that influenced medical students in choosing a career [ 41 ]. While evaluating the motivating factors among the medical students influencing factors in choosing ophthalmology and non-ophthalmology career choice among Penn State College of Medicine, Cui et al. stated that professional satisfaction and executing different surgical procedures were the significant factors in choosing ophthalmology among the applicants [ 37 ]. On analyzing the motivational factors among Brazilian medical students of different age groups, Gameiro et al. reported that work hours and job satisfaction were the primary motivational factors in choosing ophthalmology. Furthermore, they revealed that these factors did not vary across different generations of participants [ 33 ]. Variation from the present study’s findings and other discussed studies indicate that the motivational factors significantly vary across different demographic locations. This emphasizes the significance of conducting a study in northern KSA due to the country’s wide sociocultural and demographic variations. Regarding medical students’ perception of an ophthalmology career, our findings are in alignment with the study done in Canada by Li et al. In their study, the mean scores of perceptions were high in lifestyle, personal, research, and academic competitiveness [ 12 ]. Both studies indicate that early exposure and positive perceptions of lifestyle and career opportunities contribute significantly to students’ interest in ophthalmology. The present study revealed the mean perception scores in the duration of residence training, competitiveness, diverse patient care, and available practice scopes than those who did not wish to select ophthalmology as their first choice. Similar to our study, some authors explored the association between the duration of training and career selection among medical students [ 42 ]. In contrast to the present study, a Canadian study reported that the mean score was significantly higher in participants’ perception of the prestige status of ophthalmology career [ 12 ]. A study conducted by Parija et al. in India stated that a rapidly advancing field was one of their participants’ major perceived factors [ 43 ]. Similar to our research, Gameiro et al. and Li et al. did not find age to be a significant factor in choosing ophthalmology [ 12 , 33 ]. In contrast, Parija et al. stated in their study that the lower age group was more inclined (positive perception) towards choosing ophthalmology as their career choice [ 43 ]. The differences observed across the studies may be attributed to variations in the study settings, such as demographic characteristics and inclusion and exclusion criteria. These variations across the studies once again emphasize the necessity of regional studies and ongoing monitoring of the medical students’ perceptions and factors in choosing a specialty. A high GPA and involvement in research related to ophthalmology again indicate the competitiveness of the residency program. However, evaluating the correlation between high GPA and other predictors can give insights into the medical students’ decision-making process when selecting ophthalmology as a career choice. This is particularly relevant as the available residency program in ophthalmology in KSA is limited. Finally, female gender preference in ophthalmology career choice is a crucial factor identified by the present study. This trend is a positive finding as there is always a need for diverse healthcare workers, especially in a culturally conservative Saudi society, and empowering women, according to the Vision 2030 initiative. Gender is one of the factors in choosing the specialty in some residency programs [ 44 , 45 ]. A study conducted in Madinah, KSA, reported no significant difference in choosing ophthalmology between males and females [ 14 ]. The present study was conducted with a standard and validated tool. We used appropriate statistical methods to obtain the results. However, the authors state the following limitations of the study: This study was conducted in the northern KSA. Therefore, we cannot generalize the results to other parts of KSA, as the perception and factors vary with the different geographical locations. There are limitations due to the nature of data collection (self-reported). This cross-sectional study cannot identify the temporal association between career choice and the influencing factors. Conclusion The present study demonstrated that northern Saudi medical students preferred ophthalmology as the second most preferred specialty. The highest proportion of the motivational factor was a faculty member (s) of their college, followed by financial aspects. Regarding preference to choose an ophthalmology career, we found that females and those with high GPAs had higher odds of preferring ophthalmology as their future career. The positive intention of the northern Saudi medical students is in alignment with the health sector transformation program of the Vision 2030 initiative. Hence, it is essential to recognize the students’ interests and nurture them by instituting appropriate medical education and healthcare policy. Therefore, individual aspiration can be achieved along with the required healthcare needs. Furthermore, we recommend prospective studies across all the regions of KSA to identify changes in trends and cross-cultural variations and for the generalization of findings. Acknowledgements None. Author contributions A.A., A.T, B.A, A.S.A, S.O.A, R.B.A, and L.A.Z.A: Made substantial contribution in concepts and design of the study; A.A, A.T, A.S.A, and S.0.A: Involved in validation. A.T, R.B.A, L.A.Z.A: Involved in acquisition of data; A.A, B.A, A.S.A, and S.O.A,: Involved in data entry; A.T, and R.B.A; Involved in analysis of data and its interpretation; A.A., A.T, B.A, and A.S.A: Involved in drafting the article and all other authors involved in critically revising the manuscript; All authors: Approved the final version of manuscript to be published; All authors: Agreed to be accountable for all aspects of the work. Funding “This work was funded by the Deanship of Graduate Studies and Scientific Research at Jouf University under grant No. (DGSSR-2024-01-01190)”. Data availability The data used for the current study are available from the corresponding author on reasonable request. Declarations Ethics approval This study was approved by the Local Committee of Bioethics, Jouf University (approval no: 2-04-45). The study complied with the Declaration of Helsinki. Informed consent was obtained from all subjects involved in the study. Consent for publication Non applicable. Competing interests The authors declare no competing interests. Footnotes Publisher’s note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. References 1. Khathlan AAB. Community ophthalmology clinic utilization and morbidities results from a private primary healthcare center in Saudi Arabia. Saudi J Ophthalmol. 2021;35(1):34–8. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. AAO. The Power of Ophthalmology in Community Health 2023 [cited 2024 5 December]. https://www.aao.org/eyenet/article/the-power-of-ophthalmology-in-community-health 3. AAO. Medical students - American Academy of Ophthalmology 2022 [cited 2023 15 October]. https://www.aao.org/medical-students 4. Alasiri AA, Mohammed V. Healthcare transformation in Saudi Arabia: an overview since the launch of vision 2030. Health Serv Insights. 2022;15:11786329221121214. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Chowdhury S, Mok D, Leenen L. Transformation of health care and the new model of care in Saudi Arabia: Kingdom’s vision 2030. J Med Life. 2021;14(3):347–54. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Vision2030. Health Sector Transformation Program. Riyadh, Saudi Arabia.2021 [cited 2023 15 October]. https://www.vision2030.gov.sa/en/vision-2030/vrp/health-sector-transformation-program/ 7. Alharbi AMD, Alhazmi AMS, Prevalence. Risk factors, and patient awareness of diabetic retinopathy in Saudi Arabia: a review of the literature. Cureus. 2020;12(12):e11991. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Alrabghi DA, Abudungor RL, Alsulaiman YS, Najjar A, Al-Manjoumi AM. Prevalence and Associated risk factors of dry eye disease among children and adults in Saudi Arabia: a cross-sectional study. Cureus. 2023;15(6):e40170. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Al-Ghamdi AS. Adults visual impairment and blindness - an overview of prevalence and causes in Saudi Arabia. Saudi J Ophthalmol. 2019;33(4):374–81. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Schmidt CH, Volpe NJ, Bryar PJ. Eye disease in medical practice: what you should know and why you should know it. Med Clin North Am. 2021;105(3):397–407. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google S