RS randomized trial. Ophthalmol Sci 2022;2:100168. 10.1016/j.xops.2022.100168 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 21. Wu D, Xiang Y, Wu X, et al. Artificial intelligence-tutoring problem-based learning in ophthalmology clerkship. Ann Transl Med 2020;8:700. 10.21037/atm.2019.12.15 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 22. Xu F, Jiang L, He W, et al. The clinical value of explainable deep learning for diagnosing fungal Keratitis using in vivo confocal microscopy images. Front Med 2021;8:797616. 10.3389/fmed.2021.797616 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 23. Lin H, Li R, Liu Z, et al. Diagnostic efficacy and therapeutic decision-making capacity of an artificial intelligence platform for childhood cataracts in eye clinics: a multicentre randomized controlled trial. EClinicalMedicine 2019;9:52–9. 10.1016/j.eclinm.2019.03.001 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 24. Siontis GCM, Sweda R, Noseworthy PA, et al. Development and validation pathways of artificial intelligence tools evaluated in randomised clinical trials. BMJ Health Care Inform 2021;28:e100466. 10.1136/bmjhci-2021-100466 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 25. Leslie D, Mazumder A, Peppin A, et al. Does "AI" stand for augmenting inequality in the era of COVID-19 healthcare. BMJ 2021;372:n304. 10.1136/bmj.n304 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 26. DECIDE-AI Steering Group . DECIDE-AI: new reporting guidelines to bridge the development-to-implementation gap in clinical artificial intelligence. Nat Med 2021;27:186–7. 10.1038/s41591-021-01229-5 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 27. Collins GS, Dhiman P, Andaur Navarro CL, et al. Protocol for development of a reporting guideline (TRIPOD-AI) and risk of bias tool (PROBAST-AI) for diagnostic and prognostic prediction model studies based on artificial intelligence. BMJ Open 2021;11:e048008. 10.1136/bmjopen-2020-048008 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 28. Sounderajah V, Ashrafian H, Rose S, et al. A quality assessment tool for artificial intelligence-centered diagnostic test accuracy studies: QUADAS-AI. Nat Med 2021;27:1663–5. 10.1038/s41591-021-01517-0 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 29. Sounderajah V, Ashrafian H, Golub RM, et al. Developing a reporting guideline for artificial intelligence-centred diagnostic test accuracy studies: the STARD-AI protocol. BMJ Open 2021;11:e047709. 10.1136/bmjopen-2020-047709 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 30. Page MJ, McKenzie JE, Bossuyt PM, et al. The PRISMA 2020 statement: an updated guideline for reporting systematic reviews. BMJ 2021;372:n71. 10.1136/bmj.n71 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials Supplementary data bmjhci-2023-100757supp003.pdf (190.2KB, pdf) Supplementary data bmjhci-2023-100757supp001.pdf (66.6KB, pdf) Supplementary data bmjhci-2023-100757supp002.pdf (76.2KB, pdf) Data Availability Statement Data sharing not applicable as no datasets generated and/or analysed for this study. All data relevant to the study are included in the article or uploaded as online supplemental information.',514,'2025-10-14 10:29:51','2025-10-14 10:29:51');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(2924,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6583047','Success in Attaining Independent Funding Among National Institutes of Health K Grant Awardees in Ophthalmology: An Extended Follow-up.','Protopsaltis NJ, Chen AJ, Hwang V, Gedde SJ, Chao DL.','2018','u10 emailing pasw requerying u10 bracket grantee u10 decimal u10 u10 u10 multiyear r21 nonrenewable r21 r21 nonrenewable requeried disincentives u10 k01 k11 k22 k25 u10 multiyear r21 r03 ambati cahoon rejuvenating culican weinreb schiffman gedde projectreporter brimr nih_awards nih_awards_2016 u10 r21 r21 k_awards_evaluation_finalreport_20110901 psw_report_acd_06042014 faupel badger motomura rangarajan ubel','Key Points Question How successful are ophthalmology clinician-scientists receiving National Eye Institute career development awards (K08 or K23) in obtaining R01 grant funding? Findings In this follow-up analysis of a cohort of National Eye Institute career development awardees, there was a 93% increase in the number of awardees who received an R01 grant. The mean time of receiving an R01 was nearly 3 years after completion of a K award. Meaning These findings suggest National Eye Institute career development awardees are more successful at obtaining independent funding than previously reported, but this can occur many years after the K award ended. Abstract Importance Understanding factors associated with attaining independent research funding by ophthalmology clinician-scientists receiving National Eye Institute career development awards (K08 or K23) in ophthalmology can be important to maintaining the pipeline of clinician-scientists. Objective To provide continued follow-up of a cohort of ophthalmology clinician-scientists who received National Institutes of Health (NIH) K career development grants. Design, Setting, and Participants Cohort study from an electronic database review of ophthalmologists who have received either a K08 or K23 career development grant from the NIH. Data were analyzed between December 30, 2015, and December 30, 2017. Main Outcomes and Measures Receipt of an NIH R01 grant. Results We previously characterized a group of more than 100 ophthalmologists who received K awards from 1996 to 2010, of whom 29 were awarded R01 grants. In follow-up of this cohort in 2017, 27 additional K awardees of this initial cohort were awarded an R01 from 2011 to 2017, leading to a total of 62 of 128 ophthalmologists receiving an R01. The mean time to receiving an R01 grant after the K award ended was 2.8 years. The data did not identify a definitive association with sex, having a PhD degree, or research tier of university in obtaining an R01 grant in this cohort. Conclusions and Relevance In comparison with our previous report of the same cohort, there was a 93% increase in the number of K awardees who have received an R01 award, with the mean time to award being nearly 3 years after completing their K grant. This suggests that most K awardees in ophthalmology are successful in obtaining R01 grants, but one should recognize this may be several years after their K grant has ended. This study follows up a cohort of ophthalmology clinician-scientists who received National Institutes of Health K career development grants. Introduction Clinician-scientists are highly trained physicians who use both their clinical and scientific expertise to advance research. These individuals play a vital role in ophthalmology, often serving as a bridge between basic scientists and clinicians to translate discoveries into new treatments for ophthalmic diseases. Much has been written about the dearth of clinician-scientists in ophthalmology and whether it is still a viable career pathway given the protracted training, financial pressure owing to educational debt, decreased financial remuneration compared with private practice, and intense competition for research funding. 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 The National Institutes of Health (NIH) K awards are a common pathway for development of clinician-scientists. This involves a 5-year career development award providing protected time and mentorship to develop an independent research program in either clinical or laboratory research. In 2013, 5 we characterized a cohort of ophthalmologists who have received NIH K awards and assessed their ability to get NIH R01 grants, a key benchmark for having an independent research program. We found a substantial decline in the proportion of K awardees who obtained R01 grants over time, with 63% of K awardees from 1996 to 2000 obtaining an R01 grant, while only 13% of K awardees from 2001 to 2005 obtained an R01 grant. 5 In this report, we provide continued follow-up on this cohort 5 years later and expanded the analysis to include ophthalmologists who received K awards since our previous report. Methods The methods for this study were described in detail previously. 5 K awardees were identified using the NIH’s online Research Portfolio Online Reporter Tools database (REPORTER). 6 Search terms used were K08 and K23 award recipients from the National Eye Institute (NEI) from 1996 to 2017. Data, including the recipient’s name, year beginning the K award, training institution, and project title, were exported into a Microsoft Excel spreadsheet. These individuals were then individually searched in the REPORTER database to identify whether they received R01 or U10 grants. Demographic data were collected including sex, educational degrees, training institution, year of start of their K award, and clinical specialty. Because this was a search of a public online database, no institutional review board approval was needed. K12 awardees were identified by emailing K12 directors at each of the respective programs with NEI K12 awards. A list of K12 awardees was provided by 4 of the 8 programs, which included the recipient’s name, sex, educational degrees, and award date. These names were then cross-referenced with the K08 and K23 awardees and searched in the NIH REPORTER database to determine whether they had received R01 funding. National Eye Institute budget and award data were also retrieved from the NIH REPORTER database. Descriptive statistics for the K08 and K23 awardees are presented. Comparisons between K awardees who did and did not receive R01 grants were performed with the χ 2 test for categorical variables. Independent predictive factors for success in obtaining an R01 grant were assessed with logistic regression. Statistical analysis was performed using SPSS PASW statistics, version 17.0 (IBM). Odds ratios with 95% confidence intervals were calculated. Results The demographic characteristics of ophthalmology K awardees are shown in Table 1 . Previously, we had identified 106 ophthalmologists who had received an NIH K award from 1996 to 2010. We identified 75 additional ophthalmologists who received K awards from 2011 to 2017 from the NEI. Among this cohort of new K08 or K23 grant recipients, 27 were women (36%). Thirty-nine (52%) had a PhD degree in addition to their MD degree, and 57 (76%) came from ophthalmology departments ranked in the top 20 for NIH funding based on the Blue Ridge Medical Institute. 7 We also identified 21 individuals who had received K12 grants during this period. Twelve had also received K08 or K23 awards and thus were already included within our analysis. Nine K12 awardees who did not receive an individual K08 or K23 award did not have a change in their R01 grant status. Table 1. Demographic Characteristics of Ophthalmology K Awardees. Characteristic No.(%) Total (N = 180) 1996-2000 (n = 36) 2001-2005 (n = 39) 2006-2010 (n = 30) 2011-2017 (n = 75) Sex Male 127 (71) 28 (78) 33 (85) 18 (60) 48 (64) Female 53 (29) 8 (22) 6 (15) 12 (40) 27 (36) Degree MD/PhD 81 (45) 17 (45) 14 (36) 11 (39) 39 (52) MD 99 (55) 21 (55) 25 (64) 17 (61) 36 (48) Specialty Comprehensive 5 (3) 2 (6) 1 (3) 1 (3) 1 (1) Cornea 33 (18) 7 (19) 9 (23) 7 (23) 10 (13) Glaucoma 39 (22) 5 (14) 4 (10) 7 (23) 23 (31) Neuro-ophthalmology 10 (6) 3 (8) 3 (8) 1 (3) 3 (4) Oculoplastics 8 (4) 1 (3) 3 (8) 1 (3) 3 (4) Pediatric ophthalmology 13 (7) 4 (11) 3 (8) 1 (3) 5 (7) Retina 64 (36) 10 (28) 14 (36) 12 (40) 28 (37) Uveitis 8 (4) 4 (11) 2 (5) 0 2 (3) NIH funding tier a 1 97 (54) 19 (53) 15 (39) 16 (53) 47 (63) 2 30 (17) 5 (14) 12 (31) 3 (10) 10 (13) 3 22 (12) 2 (6) 5 (13) 6 (20) 9 (12) 4 31 (17) 10 (28) 7 (18) 5 (17) 9 (12) Obtained R01 grant Yes 63 (35) 24 (67) 18 (46) 14 (47) 7 (9) No 117 (65) 12 (33) 21 (54) 16 (53) 68 (91) Open in a new tab Abbreviation: NIH, National Institutes of Health. a Tier 1 indicates institutions ranked 1 to 10; tier 2, institutions ranked 11 to 20; tier 3, institutions ranked 21 to 40; tier 4, institutions ranked higher than 40. We explored the NIH REPORTER database to determine whether previous K awardees had received new R01 grants since our last analysis in 2013. Previously, 29 of 105 K awardees from 1996 to 2010 had received R01 grants. In requerying the database, we identified 34 additional K awardees who received R01 grants since our last analysis (2 in the 1996-2000 cohort, 14 from the 2001-2005 cohort, 11 from the 2006-2010 cohort, and 7 in the 2011-2017 cohort). 6 All R01 grants were awarded from the National Eye Institute. The proportion of K awardees who obtained R01 funding was 67% from 1996 to 2000, 46% from 2001 to 2005, 47% from 2006 to 2010, and 9% from 2011 to 2017 ( Table 1 ). The Figure illustrates the time to obtain an R01 grant for K awardees. The mean (SD) time to get an R01 award after the K award ended was 2.8 (3.3) years. We also performed multivariate logistic regression analysis to identify factors associated with obtaining an R01 grant. Sex, having a PhD degree, research tier of university, or whether awardees had a K08 or K23 award were not significantly associated with attainment of an R01 grant in this cohort ( Table 2 ). Figure. Distribution of K Awardees Receiving an R01 or U10 Grant Over Time. Open in a new tab Total n = 128 encompassing K awardees from 1996 to 2012. Bracket indicates time when grantee is still receiving their K award (K award is 5 years in length). Table 2. Factors Associated With Conversion of K Awards to R01 or U10 Grants 1996-2012. Independent Variable No. (%) a Odds Ratio (95% CI) Received Grant (n = 66) Did Not Receive Grant (n = 62) Univariate b Multivariate c Sex Male 53 (80) 42 (68) 1.94 (0.86-4.3) 2.56 (0.96-6.82) Female 13 (20) 20 (32) 1 [Reference] 1 [Reference] Degree MD/PhD 30 (45) 25 (40) 1.23 (0.61-2.49) 1.25 (0.51-3.06) MD 36 (55) 37 (60) 1 [Reference] 1 [Reference] Specialty Comprehensive 2 (3) 2 (3) 1.75 (0.17-17.7) 1.03 (0.08-12.4) Cornea 15 (23) 12 (19) 2.19 (0.51-9.3) 3.13 (0.59-16.5) Glaucoma 11 (17) 14 (23) 1.38 (0.31-5.92) 2.04 (0.37-11.1) Neuro-ophthalmology 4 (6) 3 (5) 2.33 (0.33-16.2) 2.50 (0.30-20.6) Oculoplastics 4 (6) 1 (2) 7.00 (0.56-86.3) 10.8 (0.72-160) Pediatric ophthalmology 4 (6) 7 (11) 1 [Reference] 1 [Reference] Retina 23 (35) 20 (32) 2.01 (0.51-7.89) 2.87 (0.57-14.4) Uveitis 3 (5) 3 (5) 1.75 (0.23-13.2) 1.50 (0.15-14.5) NIH funding tier d 1 33 (50) 29 (47) 1 [Reference] 1 [Reference] 2 13 (20) 11 (18) 1.04 (0.40-2.67) 1.35 (0.46-3.96) 3 8 (12) 9 (15) 0.78 (0.26-2.29) 1.06 (0.32-3.49) 4 12 (18) 13 (21) 0.81 (0.32-2.06) 0.78 (0.26-2.27) Type of K award K08 47 (71) 44 (71) 1 [Reference] 1 [Reference] K23 19 (29) 18 (29) 0.99 (0.46-2.12) 1.86 (0.66-5.25) Year of K award e 1996-2000 25 (38) 12 (19) 5.56 (1.73-17.7) 6.85 (1.85-25.3) 2001-2005 19 (29) 20 (32) 2.53 (0.81-7.83) 1.91 (0.54-6.65) 2006-2010 16 (24) 14 (23) 3.05 (0.93-9.93) 3.21 (0.90-11.4) 2011-2012 6 (9) 16 (26) 1 [Reference] 1 [Reference] Open in a new tab Abbreviation: NIH, National Institutes of Health. a Column percentages may not add up to 100 because of decimal rounding. b Univariate logistic regression. c Multivariate logistic regression with all factors in model. d Tier 1 indicates institutions ranked 1 to 10 in 2010; tier 2, institutions ranked 11 to 20; tier 3, institutions ranked 21 to 40; tier 4, institutions ranked higher than 40. Determined by Blue Ridge Medical Institute. e Years 2013 to 2017 not included because K awardees are still receiving the training grant. In addition, we also explored the NIH REPORTER database to look at other NIH grants awarded to NEI K awardees other than the R01. U10 grants are large collaborative grants for investigator-initiated clinical vision research including clinical trials and large epidemiologic studies. 8 We identified 4 K awardees of 180 K awardees from 1996 to 2017 who received U10 awards but not R01 grants. Because U10 grants are large multiyear grants for clinical research, we have considered them an “R01 equivalent” in terms of statistical analysis for Table 2 . Additionally, we searched for NIH R21 awards, which are 2-year nonrenewable exploratory grants given to pilot projects to generate data for larger R01 grants. 9 We identified 4 individuals who were awarded R21 grants but not awarded R01 grants. Because R21 grants are pilot project grants and are nonrenewable, we did not include them in our statistical analysis. We used the NIH REPORTER to determine NEI budget expenditures from 1997 to 2017, and found that R01 award costs increased from $55.7 million in 1997 to $105.3 million in 2017 ( Table 3 ). 10 During the period for which K award data are available, the number of awards from the NEI increased from 43 in 1998 to 79 in 2017 ( Table 3 ). Table 3. NEI Budget and Award Data by Year a . Year No. Budget, $ million New R01 Applications New R01 Awards Renewal R01 Applications Renewal R01 Awards K Awards 1997 280 67 295 165 NA b 55.7 1998 300 88 251 125 43 54.1 1999 351 106 274 152 49 69.8 2000 378 122 274 157 50 82.3 2001 380 110 252 143 48 81.6 2002 351 101 273 157 50 88.9 2003 406 111 278 124 53 85.8 2004 486 109 306 142 51 91.6 2005 478 100 327 124 61 88.5 2006 524 91 322 121 55 83.2 2007 438 103 276 111 60 85.8 2008 405 99 294 124 65 89.4 2009 379 94 264 117 62 86.5 2010 426 95 242 113 60 83.8 2011 462 115 240 125 63 102.8 2012 483 129 269 130 61 110.7 2013 510 115 251 97 68 93.2 2014 527 108 251 122 69 98.6 2015 654 126 229 87 75 89.7 2016 666 166 233 89 77 108.5 2017 663 150 203 100 79 105.3 Open in a new tab Abbreviations: NA, not available; NEI, National Eye Institute; NIH, National Institutes of Health; REPORTER, Research Portfolio Online Reporter Tools database. a NEI data retrieved through the NIH REPORTER. b NEI K award data are not available for this year through the NIH REPORTER. Discussion We have extended our follow-up of ophthalmologists who received NIH K awards to better understand the outcomes of clinician-scientists pursuing independent research within ophthalmology. The newest cohort of ophthalmologists from 2011 to 2017 who received K awards had similar characteristics to those from previous years in terms of sex, whether they had a PhD degree, and the research tier of institution where they received the grant. Interestingly, in our follow-up analysis of K awardees from 1996 to 2010, a substantial number of K awardees who did not obtain R01 grants in our last analysis were found to have received NIH R01 grants when we requeried the NIH REPORTER database 5 years later. This might be surprising because most of these individuals had already finished their K award and would presumably no longer have salary support for protected research time. This suggests that they may be receiving bridge funding to continue their research programs from the department, private foundation awards, or Veterans Affairs awards. In support of this notion, we found the mean time to obtain an R01 grant after completion of the K award is 2.8 years, indicating that there is often a significant gap between completion of the K award and receiving an R01 grant. Therefore, many K awardees continued with their research projects even after their NIH salary support ended. In addition, 18% of the K awardees (11 of 62) received their R01 grants 5 years or more (the longest being 12 years) after their K awards had elapsed, indicating a persistent desire to obtain research funding. Expanding on this, the 1996 to 2000 cohort had a higher rate of obtaining an R01 than the later groups, which on initial approach seems intuitive given these individuals have simply had more time in which to obtain funding. However, in examining those who received K awards during 2001 to 2005, they do not have an increased rate of obtaining an R01 compared with those from 2011 to 2012. Thus, the mean length of time from the end of a K award to obtaining an R01 does not fully illustrate how large the gap is between these awards for clinician-scientists in ophthalmology because those who received a K award more than a decade ago have no higher likelihood of obtaining an R01 compared with those whose funding only recently ended. Although interesting, it would be difficult to ascertain exactly why this is the case, although the aforementioned disincentives likely play a role. One interesting finding from our study was that K awardees with combined MD/PhD degrees did not have an increased chance of obtaining an R01 grant compared with MD degrees alone. This was also found in our previous analysis. While our numbers are small, larger analysis by the NIH in general showed that MD/PhDs and MDs with prior K awards both had a 66% R01 award rate. 11 Comparison of R01 award rates for established vs new investigators lends additional context to these findings. A separate large NIH analysis in 2012 found the award rate for both MDs and MD/PhDs with a prior R01 was 23.3%, compared with an award rate of 14.9% for MD/PhDs and 14.7% for MDs without a prior R01. 12 While the overall award rate has generally decreased across all types of investigators, there has been a reduction in the gap between the award rates for new vs established R01 applicants, which the NIH notes is partly attributable NIH’s New Investigator policies implemented in 2007 and strengthened in 2009. 12 One consideration that determines, in part, the success of obtaining an R01 grant is the fluctuating NIH budget. The NIH budget has remained relatively flat in inflation-adjusted costs since 2003, despite rapid increase in the number of applications. Indeed, the rate of funding for new applications at the NEI is 20% vs 48.6% for renewal applications. 10 We examined NEI budget expenditures from 1997 to 2017 and found that R01 award costs increased from $55.7 million in 1997 to $105.3 million in 2017. During the period for which K award data are available, the number of awards increased from 43 in 1998 to 79 in 2017. In addition, the number of new K awards from the NEI each year from 2008 to 2017 has remained steady. Hence, while there are year-to-year fluctuations in budget and awards, the overall trend has been markedly positive. There is limited information assessing how K awardees perform in comparison with other scientists and how K awardees from the NEI perform in obtaining research funding compared with those of other NIH institutes. Data looking at the outcomes of K awards from the National Cancer Institute (NCI) indicate 56% of K awardees in the NCI received further NIH funding (including T32, R01, and U10 grants). 13 However, this comparison cannot be fully conducted because the NCI data include other K awards that do not apply to clinician-scientists, such as K01, K11, K22, and K25 mechanisms, although it appears the NEI and NCI have comparable rates of K awardees obtaining future independent NIH funding. Further research in comparing clinician-scientist outcomes between institutes would be interesting. Another study 14 looking at K08 and K23 awardees across the entire NIH from 1997 to 2003 showed a success ratio of 30% in obtaining R01 grants (n = 2784). 14 This suggests that K awardees from the NEI were at least as successful in obtaining R01 grants, and possibly more successful if followed up over a long enough period. Limitations There are multiple limitations of this study. We focused our outcomes on receipt of the NIH R01 or U10 grant, large multiyear grants that we defined as the benchmark of a substantial research program. We did not assess other major grants, such as Veterans Affairs grants and private foundation grants, because these are not easily accessible in a publicly available database. We also did not assess receipt of other NIH grants, such as R21 and R03 grants, because these are generally considered precursors to obtain R01 funding. Optometrists were not included in this study because they may have differing scope of clinical responsibilities and challenges compared with ophthalmologist clinician-scientists. Conclusions Beginning in 2013, numerous publications have discussed strategies to retain and nurture clinician-scientists in ophthalmology and whether the NIH K awards are a successful pathway to develop clinician-scientists in ophthalmology. 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 The data presented here suggest that NEI K awardees’ ability to obtain NIH R01 grants is better than we had previously reported, although it may take several years after the K award is finished for individuals to obtain independent research funding. Continued study of the development of clinician-scientists is needed to develop best policies to maintain this vital cohort of ophthalmologists. References 1. Ambati BK, Cahoon J. Rejuvenating clinician-scientist training. Invest Ophthalmol Vis Sci. 2014;55(3):1853-1855. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Culican SM, Rupp JD, Margolis TP. Retaining clinician-scientists: nature versus nurture. Invest Ophthalmol Vis Sci. 2014;55(5):3219-3222. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Dana R, Miller JW. On the edge: the clinician-scientist in ophthalmology. JAMA Ophthalmol. 2013;131(11):1401-1402. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Weinreb RN, Goldberg JL. Clinician-scientists in ophthalmology revisited. Ophthalmology. 2013;120(10):1949-1950. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Chao DL, Schiffman JC, Gedde SJ. Characterization of a clinician-scientist cohort in ophthalmology: a demographic analysis of K grant awardees in ophthalmology. Ophthalmology. 2013;120(10):2146-2150. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. National Institutes of Health Research Portfolio Online Reporter Tools database. http://projectreporter.nih.gov/reporter.cfm . Accessed December 30, 2017. 7. Blue Ridge Medical Institute http://www.brimr.org/NIH_Awards/2016/NIH_Awards_2016.htm . Accessed December 30, 2017. 8. National Institutes of Health U10 grants. https://grants.nih.gov/grants/guide/pa-files/PAR-10-207.html . Accessed December 30, 2017. 9. National Institutes of Health NIH exploratory/developmental research grant award (R21). https://grants.nih.gov/grants/funding/r21.htm . Accessed December 30, 2017. 10. National Institutes of Health Research Portfolio Online Reporting Tools (RePORT). http://report.nih.gov/ . Accessed May 25, 2018. 11. National Institutes of Health Individual Mentored Career Development Awards Program 2011. http://grants.nih.gov/training/K_Awards_Evaluation_FinalReport_20110901.pdf . Accessed May 25, 2018. 12. The Physician-Scientist Workforce Working Group National Institute of Health Physician-Scientist Workforce Working Group Report. 2014. http://acd.od.nih.gov/reports/PSW_Report_ACD_06042014.pdf . Accessed May 25, 2018. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] 13. Mason JL, Lei M, Faupel-Badger JM, et al. Outcome evaluation of the National Cancer Institute career development awards program. J Cancer Educ. 2013;28(1):9-17. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. Jagsi R, Motomura AR, Griffith KA, Rangarajan S, Ubel PA. Sex differences in attainment of independent funding by career development awardees. Ann Intern Med. 2009;151(11):804-811. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',5,'2025-10-14 10:30:03','2025-10-14 10:30:03');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(2925,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11047060','Ophthalmic care may not align with patient need: An analysis on state-wide patient needs and provider density between 2008 and 2022.','Gilson A, Chen Q, Adelman RA.','2024','approximating mavragani seifter programmer unitless buckle chorioretinopathy epiretinal trafficked trafficked unitless 100k 100k ols 100k 100k 100k 100k 100k 22e ubiquity populace incongruity gravitate locales juxtaposed electing t35dk104689 leir qingyu 1k99lm014024 gershon kirch petelle peril curti javitt gudbranson glickman reassessing mellon elections scharkow vogelgesang mavragani ochoa tsagarakis e270 willard dreher ghiraldi pelat turbelin hen flahault valleron seifter schwarzwalder geis aucott e55205 ahrf asrs newsfeed aupo fcc aupofcc oes oes291241 e106 e116 s2214109x yau jwy dc11 haidet','Abstract Purpose: This study aims to assess the extent to which the demand for ophthalmologic care among patients at the state level is reflected in Google Trends data, serving as an indicator of patient desire in ophthalmology. Methods: For each state, patient interest in ophthalmologic care was estimated using the Google Trends resource measuring web search and YouTube search rates for multiple ophthalmologic terms. We compared the change in search for ophthalmologic terms over time and used ordinary least squares regression to evaluate whether search interest for ophthalmologic terms was able to predict the rate of practicing ophthalmologists in each state. We also compare the changing rates of searches across the web and YouTube to evaluate the resources patients are most likely to utilize. Results: From 2008 to 2022, web search rates for general ophthalmology related terms increased by 43.98%, while search interest for retinal specific terms increased by 19.51%. YouTube specific results for general ophthalmology terms increased by 55.83% while search for retinal terms fell by 58.48%. Ophthalmologic and retinal specific search interest was not significantly associated with either outcome. Conclusions: Our findings suggest that patient information needs, demographic elements, and the educational backgrounds of residents and fellows – those important factors – are surprisingly poorly correlated with ophthalmology provider density. Furthermore, we observed no noteworthy correlation between the search interest in ophthalmology and the overall density of ophthalmologists or retinal specialists. This implies that there is a pressing need to explore and implement strategies aimed at better aligning these influencing factors the choices made by ophthalmologists in selecting their practice locations to bridge the gap between healthcare availability and public interest. Keywords: Google Trends, Ophthalmology, Patient Need, Access to Care, YouTube 1. Introduction The national shortage of physicians is a well-known and worsening problem. 1 , 2 Specifically, ophthalmology is predicted to have the largest physician shortage of all surgical specialties by 2025. 3 This is driven not only by a lack of physicians, but also by where physicians choose to practice, as physicians are not uniformly distributed throughout the United States. The physician shortage has been linked to higher rates of mortality in areas with fewer physicians. 4 And in the case of ophthalmology, increased availability of care is linked to improved outcomes and increased screening frequency. 5 , 6 The estimation of physician shortage is often performed by evaluating the number of physicians per capita. 7 However, the need for physicians in an area is not exclusively proportional to the number of people, it is also dependent on the demographic of the area, public health, and the rate at which those patients desire and will utilize care. One potential method of measuring patient desire for care independent of population is through internet search frequency. 8 Google is often the first online tool for patients to turn to in researching their own conditions. And in general, the aggregated searches from all patients has been validated as a useful, although not perfect, tool in approximating the public’s interest in a given topic. 9 Google Trends is a publicly available resource that provides a means of quantifying this public interest and has been proposed by Mavragani et al. as “a reliable tool in predicting changes in human behaviour.” 10 Studies have shown that geographic and temporal variability in disease prevalence is reflected in search data. 11 Similarly, search for treatment modalities for these conditions correlated with these conditions indicating that the increased search was related to information and treatment seeking behaviour. 12 Therefore, by using Google Trends and querying specific terms related to ophthalmological conditions, it is possible to estimate a populations’ desire for increased ophthalmologic care. This desire represents the intersection of both disease prevalence and disease severity, as conditions with greater morbidity or mortality burden likely confer an increased rate of desire for treatment. Previous studies have demonstrated the concept of tracking disease rates using Google Trends search results. 13 – 15 Seifter et al. showed that rates of Lyme disease search interest peaked in spring and summer months when incidence of the disease is highest. They also demonstrated the geographic correlation of the search interest by proving a positive correlation between areas with the greatest disease burden and greatest search frequency. Additionally, Kang et al. discussed the usefulness of more general search terms in tracking rates of influenza in southern China, by showing that terms such as “fever”, and “cough” correlated with influenza infectivity rates. 15 We studied the use of Google Trends for a variety of ophthalmologic conditions and applied this information to evaluate the ophthalmologist provider shortage. We examined the search interest through both basic web searches, and through YouTube video searches. Additionally, we examine the search interest for retina specific conditions and how they correlate with vitreoretinal provider density to analyze the potential differences between comprehensive ophthalmology and subspecialty specific needs. Specifically, we will 1) show how patient interest in ophthalmology-related terms has changed over time; 2) how web searches vs YouTube searches can demonstrate the difference in quality of information that patients are seeking; and 3) to use search interest to demonstrate where there is an increased need for ophthalmologic care. In summary, this research offers valuable insights into understanding patient demand for ophthalmologic care and provides a novel approach for addressing the physician shortage in this specialty by utilizing advanced methods such as Google Trends analysis and exploring the nuanced aspects of patient information-seeking behavior through web and YouTube searches. 2. Material and methods 2.1. Datasets: This is a cross sectional study using data from multiple sources for analysis. The 2021 Area Health Resources File, a product of the US Department of Health and Human services, provided information on practicing ophthalmologists locations as well as demographic information in each state. 16 The American Society of Retinal Specialists member database provided information on the total number of retinal specialists in each state. 17 SF Match was used to find up to date information on locations and number of positions available for ophthalmology residency positions in 2021. 18 We found information on ophthalmology fellowship locations, specialties, and positions through the Association of University Professors of Ophthalmology. 19 Finally, we found information on the hourly wages of ophthalmologists in each state through the US Bureau of Labor Statistics. 20 We requested summary statistics on national and state specific search results for ophthalmologic terminology and medications from the Google Trends application programmer interface (API). 21 This method offers nuanced insights into geographic distribution, which are often overlooked in national-level analyses. Moreover, utilizing Google Trends enables analysis of patients’ desired care, rather than focusing solely on rates of delivered healthcare, potentially providing a more accurate assessment of the actual need. We first queried search result rates for a variety of ophthalmologic terms related to general ophthalmology practice, retinal specific conditions, and Anti-VEGF medications through the Google Trends API. Per request parameters provided, these results were formatted on a week-by-week basis from 2004– 2022 for each state. The results are normalized to a unitless scale of 0–100. The same information was obtained for all search terms for YouTube search frequency. We converted number of all practicing ophthalmologists, practicing retinal surgeons, total residents, total fellows, and retinal fellows to a rate per 100,000 for each state for use in further analysis. Only states with a population greater than one million were included. This was done to reduce the confounding that will occur with smaller states, since because of the small denominator of population, the rate of providers per 100,000 has a large amount of variance with a small change in the total number of providers. 2.2. Statistical Analysis: Simple linear regression was used to identify correlations between ophthalmologist provider number and potential influencing variables. We performed both univariate and multivariate ordinary least square regressions to identify variables associated with both dependent variables. Variables of interest included statewide demographic characteristics, including age, gender, and racial breakdown of each state, as well as provider salary, and interest in ophthalmology procedures as measured by Google Trends searches. For all analysis, we calculated a two-sided p-value where <0.05 was considered significant after Bonferroni correction. 2.3. Terminology: Search terms used to approximate general ophthalmologic interest included “glaucoma”, “cataract”, “uveitis”, “macular degeneration”, and “diabetic retinopathy”. To evaluate retinal specific interest the search terms “retinal tear”, “macula”, “macular edema”, “vitrectomy”, “scleral buckle”, “retinal laser”, “endophthalmitis”, “vitreous hemorrhage”, “retinopathy of prematurity”, “central serous chorioretinopathy”, “myopic degeneration”, “retinal detachment”, “epiretinal membrane”, “macular hole”, “retinitis pigmentosa”, “macular degeneration”, and “diabetic retinopathy” were used. All analysis was performed in Python version 3.10.5. This study adhered to the guidelines of the Declaration of Helsinki under the Yale University Institutional Review Board. 3. Results 3.1. Google Trends and Patient Interest: The Google Trends search results between March 1, 2018, and March 1, 2022 for a collection of ophthalmologic conditions are shown in Figure 1 . Average search interest fell from 75.8 in the three months prior to the widespread COVID pandemic in the United States (12–01-2019 through 02–31-2020) to 52.53 during the first three months of the pandemic (03–01-2020 through 05–31-2020). This marked a fall of 30.69% in total search interest. Figure 1: Open in a new tab Search interest in the United States between Feb 2018 and Feb 2022 for Glaucoma (blue), Cataract (orange), Uveitis (green), Macular Degeneration (red), Diabetic Retinopathy (purple). Average of all 5 terms is shown in black 3.2. Retinal Interest over Time: Average retina interest, the mean of search interest for all retinal terms mentioned previously, is shown in Figure 2 . Interest is shown for the USA as a whole and for each of 5 regions. In the USA, average search interest for retina related terms has increased by 19.51% from 2008 to 2022. Across regions the Western US has increased by 24.61%, Southeast by 22.41%, Southwest by 19.96%, Northeast by 17.52%, and Midwest by 12.91%. The difference in search results rates between the two most trafficked regions (Northeast and West) vs the three least trafficked regions (Southwest, Southeast, and Midwest) has increased 2.91% from 18.55% to 21.46%. Figure 2: Open in a new tab Average search interest for retinal specific terms in the United States between 2009 and 2022. Results are broken down by region. 3.3. YouTube vs Google Search for Retinal Conditions: YouTube search frequency for selected general ophthalmologic search terms and retinal search terms, as well as the average for both groups is shown in Figure 3 . For both general ophthalmologic and retinal search terms, YouTube search frequency peaked in 2016. Retinal terms have consistently been searched less frequently than general ophthalmologic terms. In 2008 retinal terms were searched 65.93% as often as general terms falling to 38.57% in 2016, and most recently 17.56% in 2022. Retinal search terms are searched relatively less frequently in 2022 than 2008, falling from 12.73 to 5.28, marking a fall of 58.48%. In contrast ophthalmologic terms were searched 55.83% more frequently in the same time frame. Figure 3: Open in a new tab YouTube search interest between 2008 and 2022 in the USA. Retinal Average (blue) is an average of all retinal search terms, not just the three represented in the figure. Similarly, General Average (red) is an average of all search terms for ophthalmology described in the methods . 3.4. Regression Analysis of Provider Locations: Figure 4 shows three linear regressions with retinal surgeons per state as the independent variable. Results show a significant positive linear correlation with retinal specific search interest, the number of surgical retinal fellows per state, and the number of ophthalmologic residents per state, with r 2 values of 0.22, 0.51, and 0.73 respectively. Figure 4: Open in a new tab (Top) Left, a map of the retinal interest per state for the USA in a unitless scale. Right, linear regression analysis predicting the total number of retinal surgeons per state using retinal interest. (Middle) Left, a map of the total number of surgical retinal fellowship positions available in each state. Right, linear regression analysis predicting the total number of retinal surgeons per state using total retinal fellowship positions. (Bottom) Left, a map of the total number of ophthalmology resident positions available in each state. Right, linear regression analysis predicting the total number of retinal surgeons per state using total ophthalmology resident positions. Table 1 shows results for ordinary least square regression predicting both the total number of ophthalmologists per 100k people in each state, and the retinal specialists per 100k people in each state. Upon multivariate analysis, a smaller state population (OR: 0.98 95% CI 0.97–0.99), an increase in the number of ophthalmology fellows in the state (OR: 1.18 95% CI 1.09–1.27), and fewer surgical retinal fellows in each state (OR: 0.71 95% CI 0.56–0.89) were found to significantly predict an increase in the rate of ophthalmologists per 100,000 individuals. Similar analysis performed to predict the rate of retinal surgeons only identified an increase in the number of ophthalmology fellows in the state (OR: 0.96 95% CI 0.93–0.99) which identified a smaller state population (OR: 1.00 95% CI 0.97–0.99) as associated with an increase in the rate of retinal specialists. OLS regression identified significant p-values associated with the state population, the number of vitreoretinal fellows, and ophthalmologic search interest, however the 95% CI contained 1 upon adjustment for significant figures. Table 1: Multivariate ordinary least squares regression predicting the rate of ophthalmologists per 100k people and the rate of retinal specialists per 100k people in each state. (1 million pop cut-off, 44 states) Ophthalmologists Per 100k Retinal Specialists per 100k OR P-Value OR P-Value State Population (100k people) 0.98 (0.97-0.99) 1.4E-4* 1.00 (1.00-1.00) 9.5E-3* Total Number of Ophthalmology Fellows 1.18 (1.09-1.27) 2.4E-4* 1.01 (1.00-1.02) 0.031* Number of Surgical Retinal Fellows 0.71 (0.56-0.89) 4.22E-4* 0.96 (0.93-0.99) 9.7E-3* Ophthalmologic Search Interest 1.06 (1.00-1.12) 0.053 1.01 (1.00-1.02) 0.036* Number of Ophthalmology Residents 1.05 (1.00-1.10) 0.054 1.01 (1.00-1.01) 0.13 African American (Percentage of Pop) 1.04 (0.95-1.14) 0.38 1.00 (0.99-1.02) 0.44 Per Hour Payment ($) 1.00 (0.99-1.00) 0.45 1.00 (1.00-1.00) 0.34 Retinal Specific Search Interest 1.04 (0.90-1.20) 0.58 1.00 (0.98-1.02) 0.93 Caucasian (Percentage of Pop) 1.02 (0.93-1.12) 0.66 1.00 (0.99-1.02) 0.5 Male (Percentage of Pop) 1.04 (0.86-1.25) 0.71 1.00 (0.97-1.02) 0.91 Hispanic (Percentage of Pop) 1.01 (0.96-1.06) 0.72 1.00 (1.00-1.01) 0.27 Open in a new tab 4. Discussion Our study concludes that the relationship between patient information needs, demographic factors, and the educational background of residents and fellows, while informative, offers limited predictive power for ophthalmology provider density. Notably, our findings reveal no significant correlation between search interest in ophthalmology and the overall density of ophthalmologists or retinal specialists. This indicates that the decision-making process of ophthalmologists regarding practice locations is influenced by factors beyond regional search interest levels. In line with our results, we initially observed an increase in absolute search interest for retinal terms through Google over a span of 15 years. However, the correlation between this search interest and the presence of retinal surgeons is not as straightforward as anticipated. Interestingly, we noted that while there was heightened search interest in retinal conditions, YouTube search data did not exhibit a corresponding increase in patient engagement. Specifically, retinal terms showed a decrease of almost 60% in search frequency on YouTube compared to other ophthalmologic conditions, suggesting that patients may be less likely to seek information on retinal conditions through video platforms. The reasons for this decrease are unknown, however causes include a lack of material on YouTube about retinal conditions that is understandable by patients, retinal conditions being less amenable to video explanations, or a relative lack of patient desire to learn about retinal conditions compared to other conditions. Given the ubiquity of certain retinal conditions, namely macular degeneration and diabetic retinopathy, and the increasing number of patients with these conditions, the decrease in search rates is unlikely due to a decrease in patient need for education. 22 , 23 YouTube is also an inherently visual education tool, and its modest use as a means of patient education may be explained by the fact that it is not well suited to educate the visually impaired. However, this fact would be expected to translate to all ophthalmologic search terms, not just those for retinal related terms. Finally we examined certain factors which may contribute to where providers practice. The demographic composition of states across gender, age and race may influence provider location as provider demographics often diverge from the broader demographics of the United States populace. This incongruity may influence provider practice location preferences, as patient and physician satisfaction increases when they share demographic characteristics, there may be a propensity for practitioners to gravitate towards areas reflective of their own demographic profiles. 24 Higher salary prospects also serve as a significant draw for ophthalmic professionals, as with all working individuals, impacting their decisions regarding practice placement. The geographical proximity to training institutions significantly influences provider density, as recent graduates, including residents and fellows, exhibit a propensity to establish their practices in proximity to their training locales. 25 Finally, the analysis of search interest via Google Trends represents the localized demand for ophthalmic care, thereby potentially shaping provider distribution to where they are most needed. Although univariate linear regression demonstrated some association between search interest and retina provider density, there was an even stronger association between ophthalmology resident and fellow locations with retina provider density. Upon employing multivariate regression models, we found that no single factor was significantly linked to retinal surgeon provider density. A similar analysis for overall ophthalmologist provider density highlighted that only the number of retinal fellows had meaningful predictive potential. Other factors failed to show substantial odds ratios, indicating limited value in predicting provider density. It is likely that other factors not included in this study have a measurable influence on where providers choose to practice. However, many of these factors are difficult to obtain and analyze, including the location of a provider’s family, the requirements of their spouse’s job location, and research opportunities and the timing of job openings which influence academic positions. Incorporating these models into a regression analysis is nearly impossible however as they are fluid and unique to each provider on an individual level. These observations underscore a crucial finding: ophthalmologists’ practice locations are not strictly determined by patient search interest. When juxtaposed with the reduced engagement with retinal conditions on YouTube, it becomes evident that patients’ informational and healthcare needs are not being fully met. Regions with heightened search interest for ophthalmologic terms do not necessarily correspond to a proportional increase in practicing physicians. Furthermore, the utilization of YouTube as an educational resource appears to be deficient, potentially due to inadequate material or accessibility concerns. There are a few notable limitations to the present study. Due to the nature and variety of information used, data was collected from multiple sources which may have different levels of reliability. For instance, we used the American Society of Retinal Specialists member database to determine the number of retinal surgeons in each state. However, not all surgeons are listed in this database, and not all listed surgeons have an associated location. Therefore, this study relies on the assumption that the available group is a representative sample of all retinal specialists. In this study, we identified a mechanism for measuring patient interest in ophthalmologic care using Google Trends data, and outlined two potential mechanisms of ameliorating that interest, YouTube and direct ophthalmologic care. In the case of YouTube, we showed that patients are not seeking out information for retinal conditions at a rate similar to other ophthalmologic conditions. Finally, we showed that ophthalmologists are not electing to practice mainly in relation to this interest. Our study fills a significant gap in the literature by utilizing Google Trends data to measure patient interest in ophthalmologic care, thereby providing a novel approach to understanding healthcare demand. This innovative method allows us to capture real-time trends in patient information-seeking behavior, offering valuable insights into the dynamics of patient preferences and healthcare utilization. Further efforts should examine ways to increase the availability of YouTube resources and identify whether the problem lies in the quality of information, or its accessibility. Additional efforts should be made to identify methods to coordinate availability of care with the interest. Highlights. The shortage of ophthalmologists is a nationally recognized issue. Geographic distribution of diseases can be correlated with Google Trends search results. Patient search for ophthalmic terms does not correlate with ophthalmologists distribution. Patient demographic characteristics do not correlate with ophthalmologist density. Summary Table: What is known What this study adds • The shortage of ophthalmologists is a nationally recognized issue. • Disease rates can be tracked with Google Trends search results and correlation to geographic distribution. • Patient need for ophthalmologists, measured in proxy by Google search interest for ophthalmic conditions, poorly correlates with where ophthalmologists elect to practice. • Population level demographic characteristics due not strongly correlate with ophthalmologist density. Open in a new tab Funding: This work was supported by the National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases of the National Institutes of Health under Award Number T35DK104689 and a grant from the Leir Foundation. Dr. Qingyu Chen was also supported by the National Library of Medicine of the National Institutes of Health under award number 1K99LM014024. The content is solely the responsibility of the authors and does not necessarily represent the official views of the National Institutes of Health. This publication was made possible by the Richard K. Gershon, M.D., Endowed Medical Student Research Fellowship. Footnotes Declaration of interests The authors declare that they have no known competing financial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to influence the work reported in this paper. Conflict of Interest: Declarations of interest: none Publisher''s Disclaimer: This is a PDF file of an unedited manuscript that has been accepted for publication. As a service to our customers we are providing this early version of the manuscript. The manuscript will undergo copyediting, typesetting, and review of the resulting proof before it is published in its final form. Please note that during the production process errors may be discovered which could affect the content, and all legal disclaimers that apply to the journal pertain. References: 1. Kirch DG, Petelle K. Addressing the physician shortage: the peril of ignoring demography. Jama. 2017;317(19):1947–1948. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Watson CJ. The relationship between physician practice location and medical school area: an empirical model. Social Science & Medicine Part D: Medical Geography. 1980;14(1):63–69. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. National and Regional Projections of Supply and Demand for Surgical Specialty Practitioners: 2013–2025. 4. Gong G, Phillips SG, Hudson C, Curti D, Philips BU. Higher US rural mortality rates linked to socioeconomic status, physician shortages, and lack of health insurance. Health Affairs. 2019;38(12):2003–2010. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Gibson DM. Eye care availability and access among individuals with diabetes, diabetic retinopathy, or age-related macular degeneration. JAMA ophthalmology. 2014;132(4):471–477. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Wang F, Javitt JC. Eye care for elderly Americans with diabetes mellitus: failure to meet current guidelines. Ophthalmology. 1996;103(11):1744–1750. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Gudbranson E, Glickman A, Emanuel EJ. Reassessing the data on whether a physician shortage exists. Jama. 2017;317(19):1945–1946. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Mellon J. Internet search data and issue salience: The properties of Google Trends as a measure of issue salience. Journal of Elections, Public Opinion & Parties. 2014;24(1):45–72. [ Google Scholar ] 9. Scharkow M, Vogelgesang J. Google insight for search: A methodological innovation in the study of public agenda. Retrieved September. 2009;12:2010. [ Google Scholar ] 10. Mavragani A, Ochoa G, Tsagarakis KP. Assessing the methods, tools, and statistical approaches in Google Trends research: systematic review. Journal of Medical Internet Research. 2018;20(11):e270. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Willard SD, Nguyen MM. Internet Search Trends Analysis Tools Can Provide Real-time Data on Kidney Stone Disease in the United States. Urology. 2013;81(1):37–42. doi: 10.1016/j.urology.2011.04.024 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. Dreher PC, Tong C, Ghiraldi E, Friedlander JI. Use of Google Trends to Track Online Behavior and Interest in Kidney Stone Surgery. Urology. 2018;121:74–78. doi: 10.1016/j.urology.2018.05.040 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. Pelat C, Turbelin C, Bar-Hen A, Flahault A, Valleron AJ. More diseases tracked by using Google Trends. Emerging infectious diseases. 2009;15(8):1327. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. Seifter A, Schwarzwalder A, Geis K, Aucott J. The utility of Google Trends for epidemiological research: Lyme disease as an example. Geospatial health. 2010;4(2):135–137. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 15. Kang M, Zhong H, He J, Rutherford S, Yang F. Using google trends for influenza surveillance in South China. PloS one. 2013;8(1):e55205. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 16. Area Health Resources Files. Accessed March 14, 2023. https://data.hrsa.gov/topics/health-workforce/ahrf 17. Home - The American Society of Retina Specialists. Accessed January 26, 2024. https://www.asrs.org/ 18. Doximity. Doximity. Accessed January 26, 2024. https://www.doximity.com/newsfeed 19. The AUPO FCC, a Limited Liability Corporation, is a non-profit professional medical organization committed to the establishment of ophthalmology training standards in fellowship programs. | Association of University Professors of Ophthalmology Fellowship Compliance Committee. Accessed January 26, 2024. https://aupofcc.org/ 20. Ophthalmologists, Except Pediatric. Accessed January 26, 2024. https://www.bls.gov/oes/current/oes291241.htm 21. Google Trends. Accessed January 26, 2024. https://trends.google.com/trends/?geo=US 22. Wong WL, Su X, Li X, et al. Global prevalence of age-related macular degeneration and disease burden projection for 2020 and 2040: a systematic review and meta-analysis. The Lancet Global Health. 2014;2(2):e106–e116. doi: 10.1016/S2214109X(13)70145-1 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 23. Yau JWY, Rogers SL, Kawasaki R, et al. Global Prevalence and Major Risk Factors of Diabetic Retinopathy. Diabetes Care. 2012;35(3):556–564. doi: 10.2337/dc11-1909 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 24. Street RL, O’Malley KJ, Cooper LA, Haidet P. Understanding Concordance in Patient-Physician Relationships: Personal and Ethnic Dimensions of Shared Identity. The Annals of Family Medicine. 2008;6(3):198–205. doi: 10.1370/afm.821 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 25. Table C6: Physician Retention in State of Residency Training, by State. AAMC. Accessed January 26, 2024. https://www.aamc.org/data-reports/students-residents/data/table-c6-physician-retention-state-residency-training-state',47,'2025-10-14 10:30:16','2025-10-14 10:30:16');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(2926,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10502694','Gender Bias and Ophthalmology Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education Milestones Evaluations.','Huh DD, Yamazaki K, Holmboe E, Bartley GB, Schnabel SD, Levine RB, Srikumaran D.','2023','midyear midyear wqe midyear midyear midyear pbli semiannual cccs wqe wqe midyear wqe pbli midyear subcompetency aspirational wqe examinee psychometrically equating wqe wqe wqe wqe regressed wqe exchangeable exchangeable wqe disentangle wqe qic penalizing qic wqe wqe midyear wqe midyear midyear wqe midyear midyear wqe wqe midyear wqe midyear qic midyear wqe wqe wqe midyear midyear wqe midyear midyear wqe discernable cccs cccs wqe wqe wqe wqe wqe wqe reassuringly midyear midyear midyear midyear cccs wqe midyear xierali nivet lazzaro lidder bounds bakri bounds bakri ahluwalia adelman kalavar watane balaji svider aguillo winn beal meyerson sternbach zwischenberger e111 e118 elrashidi oxentenko gerull seiler norcini bf02597403 feinsilber siripala mears e5518 01s1 01s1 syvyk wirtalla dayal qadri yarris hamstra dayal ufere e2010888 jurich vandergrift thackeray ficalora engstler oxentenko bienstock shivraj praeger mcneish stapleton ubiquity met0000078 341x oetting blomquist kloosterboer yannuzzi gedde sridhar abbuhl pietrobon teherani lucey','This cross-sectional study investigates if there are gender biases in the Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education (ACGME) Milestones evaluations for ophthalmology trainees. Key Points Question Are there gender biases in the Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education Milestones evaluations for ophthalmology trainees? Findings This cross-sectional study of 452 postgraduate year 4 residents found that although there were no gender differences in Milestones ratings by graduation, women were rated lower in medical knowledge at the midyear point compared with men. Meaning As unfair evaluations may adversely affect career opportunities for trainees, it is important to consider potential sources of gender bias throughout training and ensure that women trainees receive comparable surgical and educational experiences as men. Abstract Importance Women remain underrepresented in ophthalmology and gender-based disparities exist in salary, grant receipt, publication rates, and surgical volume throughout training and in practice. Although studies in emergency medicine and general surgery showed mixed findings regarding gender differences in Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education (ACGME) Milestones ratings, limited data exist examining such differences within ophthalmology. Objective To examine gender differences in ophthalmology ACGME Milestones. Design, Setting, and Participants This was a retrospective cross-sectional study of postgraduate year 4 (PGY-4) residents from 120 ophthalmology programs graduating in 2019. Main Outcomes and Measures PGY-4 midyear and year-end medical knowledge (MK) and patient care (PC) ratings and Written Qualifying Examination (WQE) scaled scores for residents graduating in 2019 were included. Differential prediction techniques using Generalized Estimating Equations models were performed to identify differences by gender. Results Of 452 residents (median [IQR] age, 30.0 [29.0-32.0] years), 275 (61%) identified as men and 177 (39%) as women. There were no differences in PC domain average between women and men for both midyear (−0.07; 95% CI, −0.11 to 0; P =.06) and year-end (−0.04; 95% CI, −0.07 to 0.03; P =.51) assessment periods. For the MK domain average in the midyear assessment period, women (mean [SD], 3.76 [0.50]) were rated lower than men (mean [SD], 3.88 [0.47]; P = .006) with a difference in mean of −0.12 (95% CI, −0.18 to −0.03). For the year-end assessment, however, the average MK ratings were not different for women (mean [SD], 4.10 [0.47]) compared with men (mean [SD], 4.18 [0.47]; P = .20) with a difference in mean of −0.08 (95% CI, −0.13 to 0.03). Conclusions and Relevance Results suggest that ACGME ophthalmology Milestones in 2 general competencies did not demonstrate major gender bias on a national level at the time of graduation. There were, however, differences in MK ratings at the midyear mark, and as low ratings on evaluations and examinations may adversely affect career opportunities for trainees, it is important to continue further work examining other competencies or performance measures for potential biases. Introduction Although gender diversity in the ophthalmology workforce has improved over the past several decades, women remain significantly underrepresented, and a recent study suggested that the number of women applicants in ophthalmology may be trending downward. 1 , 2 Gender gaps are well documented in many different areas in ophthalmology. Studies show significant gender differences in salary, 3 compensation for clinical activities and industry roles, 4 , 5 cataract surgery volume and number of billed clinic visits, 6 authorship and publication rates in academia, 7 and amount of funding awarded in National Institute of Health grants even after controlling for publication experience. 8 Gender disparities begin early in ophthalmology training and affect resident medical education. Compared with men, women residents in various fields graduate with lower surgical volume during training, 9 have significantly less operative autonomy in the operating room, 10 receive lower peer evaluation scores, 11 and receive more negative feedback from faculty based on gender stereotypes. 12 , 13 Faculty evaluations of residents are especially important in this context of gender disparities, as such, ratings can be a subjective assessment of performance if faculty fail to use evidence-based criteria. Resident evaluations are potentially subject to explicit and implicit biases of the evaluating individuals or committees. 14 If present, such biases may explain some of the differences that women and men experience in training previously described, have significant consequences for the educational and career opportunities available to trainees, and contribute to gender disparities seen among faculty and practicing ophthalmologists. The Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education (ACGME) Milestones evaluate resident performance in 6 core competencies: patient care and procedural skills (PC), medical knowledge (MK), systems-based practice (SBP), practice-based learning and improvement (PBLI), professionalism (PROF), and interpersonal communication skills (ICS). 15 The Milestones were designed as a standardized and structured framework for assessing resident competence at various stages of training. The ACGME mandates semiannual assessment of all residents by a clinical competency committee (CCC) using rubrics with behavioral anchors. 16 Gender disparities in Milestone scores have previously been examined in general surgery, 17 emergency medicine, 18 , 19 , 20 internal medicine, 21 , 22 and gastroenterology 23 and have demonstrated mixed findings regarding whether gender differences are associated with biased rating processes. To our knowledge, there have been no studies examining gender differences in Milestone scores in ophthalmology. In this study, we aimed to examine gender differences in ACGME Milestone scores among a national sample of ophthalmology residents. Methods Study Population The study protocol was deemed exempt by the Johns Hopkins University School of Medicine institutional review board. In this national retrospective cross-sectional study, we compared the Milestone scores of ophthalmology residents in 2 of the 6 core competencies (MK and PC). To further verify that the CCCs have applied fair assessments for residents based on MK and skills regardless of gender, we included the American Board of Ophthalmology’s (ABO’s) Written Qualifying Examination (WQE) scaled scores as a comparative, benchmark measure using deidentified data merged across 2 organizations: the ACGME and the ABO. These 2 core competencies were selected for this analysis, as the WQE measures similar constructs, and prior studies in other specialties have shown that qualifying examination scores have the strongest associations with these ACGME Milestones competencies compared with other core competencies. 24 Resident gender used in the data analysis originated from gender information provided by the programs, either based on a resident’s declaration of their gender, based on the discretion of the program director or coordinator, or a combination of both. The categories for gender were men, women, nonbinary, or prefer not to report, although no residents were identified as nonbinary or prefer not to report. Participant race and ethnicity were not included in this study as these data were not available. As a cross-sectional study, the relevant Strengthening the Reporting of Observational Studies in Epidemiology ( STROBE ) reporting guidelines were followed. Measures Postgraduate year 4 (PGY-4) midyear and year-end ACGME ophthalmology Milestones ratings were matched with the WQE scaled score by the ABO. The ophthalmology Milestones reporting form consists of 6 competencies, further divided into 24 subcompetencies: 8 for PC, 2 for MK, 3 for SBP, 3 for PBLI, 4 for PROF, and 4 for ICS. This study analyzed the PGY-4 midyear and year-end MK and PC Milestone ratings. Each subcompetency is rated on a 5-point scale with progressive behavioral anchors to demonstrate levels of competency, ranging from level 0.0 (not yet achieved level 1) and level 1.0 (level expected of novice resident) to level 5.0 (aspirational level beyond what is expected at time of graduation). Ratings were averaged for each general competency domain (MK and PC) for the analysis. The WQE is a 250-multiple choice question examination that is administered to ophthalmology residency graduates to assess ophthalmic medical knowledge that is relevant to patient care. Each examinee’s raw score (number of questions answered correctly) is transformed into a reporting scale ranging from 200 to 1000, with a cut score (score needed to pass) of 700. The cut score is established through a psychometrically validated standard setting study and then an equating procedure is used on subsequently published forms to ensure that all examinees are held to the same standard. Statistical Analysis Descriptive statistics were reported for Milestones domain ratings and WQE scores. Differential prediction techniques 25 were used to determine whether women and men with similar MK or PC Milestones ratings differ in the WQE score to detect gender differences in the Milestones rating process. To assess the conceptual relevance between Milestones ratings and WQE score, we first compared bivariate Pearson correlations between each competency domain average and the examination score to see if MK and PC domain averages are more closely related to the WQE score than the other competency domains. To investigate gender rating bias, we regressed the WQE score on Milestones domain average and gender using a generalized estimating equation (GEE) model with an exchangeable working matrix for each residency training program as a unique clustering unit. 26 We specified an exchangeable working matrix for each program having an equal size of correlations among outcome observations. We modeled matrices as independent between programs to obtain population-average effects of focal exposure variables. A series of GEE models tested how Milestones domain average and gender were associated with the WQE score. Specifically, to disentangle potential gender bias in the Milestones rating process from substantive performance differences captured by Milestones ratings, we tested whether gender was associated with the WQE score over and above the effect of Milestones domain average (main-effects model). Furthermore, the interaction between Milestones domain average and gender was tested to see if gender bias was prevalent over all Milestones ratings or observed for a particular range (interaction model). The interaction models were compared with the main-effects models using the quasi-likelihood information criterion (QIC) to assess the relative quality of statistical models while penalizing model complexity. 27 A lower QIC value indicates better model fit. Statistical analyses were performed using SAS enterprise guide software, version 7.15 (SAS Institute). All statistical tests were 2-tailed, a P value <.05 was considered statistically significant, and there were no adjustments to P values for multiple analyses. Results Of the 481 PGY-4 residents from 120 ophthalmology programs graduating in 2019 based on ACGME data, 452 trainees (median [IQR] age, 30.0 [29.0-32.0] years) were successfully matched to WQE scores from the ABO (94% matching rate). Among the 452 residents included in the analysis, 177 (39%) identified as women and 275 (61%) as men. Table 1 shows the bivariate correlations between each of the 6 competency domain averages and the WQE score. For both midyear (Pearson r = 0.29) and year-end (Pearson r = 0.31) Milestones reporting periods, MK ratings had the strongest correlation with WQE performance follow by PC ratings (Pearson r = 0.12 and 0.10 for midyear and year-end assessments, respectively). The remaining domains had a similar range of weaker correlations. Table 1. Bivariate Correlations for Milestones Competency Domain Average and Written Qualifying Examination Score for Postgraduate Year 4 Ophthalmology Residents. Competency domain Midyear assessment period ( r value) Year-end assessment period (Pearson r value) Medical knowledge 0.29 0.31 Patient care 0.12 0.10 Systems-based practice 0.01 0.02 Practice-based learning and improvement 0.09 0.07 Professionalism 0.04 0.04 Interpersonal and communication skills 0.03 0.03 Open in a new tab Table 2 shows the average MK and PC Milestones domain scores and WQE scores, stratified by gender based on GEE models. There were no differences in PC domain average between women and men for both midyear (−0.07; 95% CI, −0.11 to 0; P =.06) and year-end (−0.04; 95% CI, −0.07 to 0.03; P =.51) assessment periods. For the MK domain average in the midyear assessment period, women (mean [SD], 3.76 [0.50]) were rated lower than men (mean [SD], 3.88 [0.47]; P =.006) with a difference in mean of −0.12 (95% CI, −0.18 to −0.03). For the year-end assessment, however, the average MK ratings were not different for women (mean [SD], 4.10 [0.47]) compared with men (mean [SD], 4.18 [0.47]; P =.20), showing a difference in mean of −0.08 (95% CI, −0.13 to 0.03). No differences were found in the WQE scores between women (mean [SD], 786.39 [57.10]) and men (mean [SD], 793.59 [59.39]; P = .24), with a difference in mean of −7.20 (95% CI, −20.51 to 5.08). Table 2. Descriptive Statistics for Average Milestones Ratings in Patient Care (PC) and Medical Knowledge (MK) Domains and Written Qualifying Examination (WQE) Scores. Rating Midyear Year-end Women (n = 188) Men (n = 292) Difference P value Women (n = 188) Men (n = 291) Difference P value PC ratings, mean (SD) 3.82 (0.40) 3.89 (0.38) −0.07 (−0.11 to 0) .06 4.19 (0.36) 4.23 (0.36) −0.04 (−0.07 to 0.03) .51 MK ratings, mean (SD) 3.76 (0.50) 3.88 (0.47) −0.12 (−0.18 to −0.03) .006 4.10 (0.47) 4.18 (0.47) −0.08 (−0.13 to 0.03) .20 WQE, mean (SD) NA NA NA NA 786.39 (57.10) a 793.59 (59.39) b −7.20 (−20.51 to 5.08) .24 Open in a new tab Abbreviation: NA, not applicable. a Total number, 177. b Total number, 275. Table 3 shows regression coefficients related to MK or PC competency domain averages and gender for PGY-4 trainees for the midyear and year-end assessment period. The main-effects model includes either MK or PC competency domain average and gender, and the interaction model consists of either the MK or PC competency domain average, gender, and the interaction of the 2 variables. The slightly lower QIC values indicate that main-effects models fit better than the interaction model for both Milestones assessment periods. The main-effects model indicates that both the midyear and year-end MK and PC domain averages were positively related with the WQE score, whereas gender was not a predictor. These results indicate that residents with higher MK or PC domain average ratings tend to score higher on the WQE, and this association holds for both genders ( Figure ). Conversely, residents with lower average MK and PC Milestones ratings obtained lower scores on the WQE regardless of gender. Table 3. Generalized Estimating Equation Regression Results for Average Milestones Ratings in Medical Knowledge (MK) and Patient Care (PC) Domains and Gender Regression Coefficients for Main- and Interaction-Effects Models. Domain Midyear reporting period Year-end reporting period Main-effect model P value Interaction model P value Main-effect model P value Interaction model P value MK domain Intercept (SE) [95% CI] 625.945 (21.204) [584.386 to 667.504] <.001 618.230 (30.974) [557.522 to 678.937] <.001 583.475 (23.968) [536.500 to 630.451] <.001 554.591 (31.827) [492.213 to 616.971] <.001 MK domain average (SE) [95% CI] 43.018 (5.413) [32.409 to 53.628] <.001 45.002 (7.949) [29.423 to 60.581] <.001 50.094 (5.577) [39.163 to 61.025] <.001 57.000 (7.531) [42.240 to 71.760] <.001 Female (SE) [95% CI] −3.295 (6.370) [−15.780 to 9.190] .61 14.257 (42.035) [−68.131 to 96.645] .73 −5.117 (6.402) [−17.666 to 7.431] .42 62.101 (45.377) [−26.836 to 151.037] .17 Domain by female interaction (SE) [95% CI] NA NA −4.604 (10.919) [−26.005 to 16.797] .67 NA NA −16.258 (10.929) [−37.680 to 5.163] .14 Quasi-like information criterion 456.218 NA 457.085 NA 455.387 NA 455.998 NA PC domain Intercept (SE) [95% CI] 692.454 (31.077) [631.546 to 753.363] <.001 724.411 (41.743) [642.597 to 806.225] <.001 669.844 (40.488) [590.490 to 749.199] <.001 666.148 (52.651) [562.953 to 769.343] <.001 PC domain average (SE) [95% CI] 25.844 (7.882) [10.396 to 41.292] .001 17.646 (10.610) [−3.148 to 38.440] .10 29.089 (9.530) [10.411 to 47.767] .002 29.962 (12.378) [5.701 to 54.223] .02 Female (SE) [95% CI] −6.528 (6.555) [−19.375 to 6.320] .32 −80.029 (50.988) [−179.964 to 19.905] .12 −7.315 (6.634) [−20.316 to 5.686] .27 1.673 (74.634) [−144.607 to 147.953] .98 Domain by female interaction (SE) [95% CI] NA NA 19.084 (13.071) [−6.534 to 44.702] .14 NA NA −2.133 (17.715) [−36.854 to 32.587] .90 Quasi-like information criterion 456.298 NA 456.693 NA 456.046 NA 457.551 NA Open in a new tab Abbreviation: NA, not applicable. Figure. Midyear and Year-End Assessment Period for Postgraduate Year 4 (PGY-4) Trainees in the Academic Year (AY) of 2018 to 2019. Open in a new tab Medical knowledge and patient care domain average and Written Qualifying Examination (WQE) score for PGY-4 trainees during the midyear (A and B, respectively) and year-end (C and D, respectively) assessment periods in AY 2018-19. Discussion Results of this cross-sectional study of 452 ophthalmology residents graduating in 2019 suggest that although women received lower MK Milestones ratings at the midyear assessment period, there were no differences in MK and PC Milestones ratings between men and women by the time of graduation. Our findings showing that men and women without differences in Milestones ratings performed without differences on the WQE suggest that there are no discernable gender differences in Milestones evaluations on a national level in this study cohort by the end of training. Although there are no studies to date, to our knowledge, examining gender disparities in Milestones evaluations within ophthalmology on a large scale, studies in other specialties have demonstrated mixed findings regarding gender differences associated with biased rating processes. Some studies examining general surgery, emergency medicine, and internal medicine residents have shown that, despite starting at similar levels in the beginning of residency, men have higher rates of Milestone attainment throughout training, resulting in higher Milestone scores for men than women at graduation. 17 , 18 , 21 In another study examining a national data set of emergency medicine residents, however, men and women were rated similarly at the end of their training for the majority of subcompetencies, with the exception of statistically significant but small absolute differences noted in 4 PC subcompetencies. 19 Similar to our findings, a study examining a national data set of internal medicine residents demonstrated that Milestones ratings were largely similar for women and men and that women and men with similar MK or PC Milestone ratings performed similarly on future written examinations. 22 Although Milestones scores are a useful tool in facilitating competency-based certification of trainees and accreditation of programs, there may be limitations to this form of evaluation. Even if residents may be rated similarly among different genders, it is important to examine whether the CCCs made gender-fair ratings for residents and to consider the possibility of implicit social desirability bias—that CCCs may have made biased ratings against 1 gender group so that both gender groups appear equal in ratings. Although this hypothesis is difficult to assess or confirm, our analysis included the WQE scores as an unbiased measure of MK and PC skills. Subtle differences in performance between trainees may be difficult to address via Milestones, and these 6 core competencies may not be as granular or sufficiently nuanced to account for individual differences. This study’s results also suggest a correlation between MK Milestones ratings (and, to a lesser degree, PC ratings) made by the CCC and WQE scores. Lee et al 28 previously found that higher Ophthalmic Knowledge Assessment Program (OKAP) examination scores, administered during residency, were associated with a higher likelihood of passing the WQE in a large multicenter cohort. However, the association between the ophthalmology MK Milestones rating and the WQE score has not been previously documented and may be helpful to programs in identifying trainees at risk for not passing the certification examinations. Because the CCC may consider a trainee’s OKAP examination score in its determination of Milestones ratings, and the examinations are testing similar constructs, this finding is not unexpected. Furthermore, that the correlation between PC ratings and the WQE score was weaker is not surprising as PC Milestones incorporate several subcompetencies and skills that are not assessed through the WQE. Nonetheless, the absence of measurable gender bias in this competency domain is reassuring. Our findings shed light on possible biases that may impede the advancement of women in ophthalmology. Women still remain significantly underrepresented as ophthalmology residents, practicing ophthalmologists, and as faculty and leaders in academic departments. 1 , 29 , 30 Although our study, reassuringly, did not find gender difference in ACGME Milestones by the time of graduation, the differences seen in MK ratings at the midyear assessment period were small (0.12 on a 5-point scale) but may still have significant implications on trainee outcomes through the cascade effect. 31 Similar differences were observed in the PC ratings at the midyear assessment, although these differences did not reach statistical significance in our study. Midyear periods before graduation are often critical time points for trainees in terms of fellowship applications or job placements, and lower Milestones ratings at the midyear assessment may potentially influence letter writers and evaluators and, in turn, these placements to a greater extent than year-end evaluations. Of note, the OKAP examination results, which are presumably an objective measure of MK, are available in the spring before the year-end evaluations. It is not known to what extent true improvement in resident MK vs availability of OKAP scores played a role in reducing potential gender bias for year-end ratings. Thus, it is important to consider and eliminate potential sources of biases that may impact lower Milestones ratings for women trainees at any time points throughout training. Limitations This study has several limitations. First, we could not assess for potential biases in the other core competencies for which the assessment methods may be more subjective. Thus, the results indicating an absence of bias need to be interpreted with caution. It is still critical for evaluators and institutions to remain vigilant in assessing for possible biases and to mitigate bias when identified. Neuropsychological testing of faculty or Cognitive Bias Modification Therapy for faculty tasked with resident assessments may help to minimize bias and promote equitable and fair resident assessments. 14 Second, although we did not study the composition of CCCs and do not have data on whether programs engage in unconscious bias training, the findings of this study may also reflect the potential benefit of bias mitigation that can come from group decision-making (from a diverse CCC) rather than an individual faculty rater. Third, although the analytical models account for clustering within a program, the study is evaluating gender bias on a national level, and it is possible that some programs may have biases that could not be assessed due to the gender composition of the program’s trainees, as we did not consider the gender composition of individual programs in the creation of GEE models. Fourth, although our study assumes that the WQE is gender-neutral because it is a highly reliable standardized examination and women and men perform comparably, the potential for gender bias cannot be excluded. Fifth, the gender data used in our analysis originated from program-reported gender, which could reflect a resident’s declaration of their own gender in some programs, a program director or coordinator’s discretion in other programs, or a combination of both methods in others. In addition, although “nonbinary” and “prefer not to report” were options provided for programs to select, none of the residents were reported as such. Therefore, this study is limited to examining the possible differences in evaluations between men and women, and we could not study other forms of gender identity. Finally, we were unable to investigate for race and ethnicity bias as reliable demographic data on race and ethnicity was not available at the time of this study. This should be a focus of future work, including examining the intersectionality of race and ethnicity and sex on Milestone ratings. Conclusions Results suggest that ACGME ophthalmology Milestones in 2 general competencies did not demonstrate major gender bias on a national level at the time of graduation. There were, however, differences in MK ratings at the midyear mark. It is critical that we consider other potential contributors to gender disparities and continue to support gender equity and retention of women surgeons in ophthalmology, especially ensuring that women trainees receive comparable surgical exposure and educational experiences as men trainees. For instance, parental leave during residency may be important to consider in the efforts to support gender equity. Program policies that support residents in maintaining surgical case volume and educational experiences and allow flexibility in forming rotation schedules that minimize disruption to resident education can be critical for residents taking parental leave during training. Finally, as low ratings on evaluations and examinations may adversely affect career opportunities for trainees, it is important to continue further work examining other competencies and relevant performance measures for potential biases. Supplement. Data Sharing Statement Click here for additional data file. (13.7KB, pdf) References 1. Xierali IM, Nivet MA, Wilson MR. Current and future status of diversity in ophthalmolo