INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(2961,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11947863','EMYWAY Workplace-Based Entrustable Professional Activities Assessments in Otolaryngology Residency Training: A Nationwide Experience.','Guo FC, Chen YT, Hsu WC, Wang PC, Chen M, Chen JW.','2024','emyway emyway emyway theaters emyway emyway encapsulate pbli emyway emyway emyway jct jct dalin tzu emyway emyway backend jct emyway emyway submits deidentifies sends jct emyway tien emyway signify epa1 epa2 epa3 epa4 epa5 epa6 epa7 epa8 epa9 epa10 sdb epa11 plasty sdb awarding epa1 epa2 epa3 epa4 epa5 epa6 epa7 epa8 epa9 epa10 epa11 sdb plasty pc3 pc5 pc6 pc7 pc8 pca1 uadt mk1 mk2 mk3 inhalant mk4 mka1 sbp1 sbp2 pbli1 pblin2 ics1 icsn1 icsn2 pbli emyway parenthesis theaters emyway emyway emyway detracting detract emyway depersonalizing constructiveness postobservation formulaic emyway emyway emyway emyway mingchih jeng nstc my2 nstc my2 nstc my3 emyway emyway seniorities ohn 20mb hwei uan shr hnsf sajid telder lindeman sarosi zma001146 scheele 0b013e31805559c7 wolfsthal ferentz flexner galaiya campisi lio2 encandela shaull jayas mink shumway dacus lathrop karnad teherani tsue 01s1 muffly cusumano postpandemic balmer caretta weyer hatala hennus s96 s104 ruangvoravat vandermeer semiannual e220 e228 gilhooly krumm fahim sonnadara fairbridge krouse karthikeyan pulimoottil s12070 lozada ferrandino colley hobday borman shoap s50 s55 lewandrowski elfar jpm13050852 rosser otolaryngology一head jtohns 202206_57 emyway bok bolts balmer borman shoap s95 s102 victores schuwirth lwt cpm vande walle quamme srp eliasz nick zabar binoculars stadler cooney redett dorafshar zarrabi lifchez faucett mccrary erman ishman kozin bohnen bowe laury kepchar lospinoso bowe cwd schmalbach foradori favreau tewksbury lupi cutrer jokela yarris lupi ownby jokela gurgel prager myer myer kashat falcone parham hendren kumagai laeeq fahey barie wijnen booij uhtrust chagnon monarque lavigne ayad gurgel mccrary riedy grundfast duijn ccma mandoki bok hgj otj jvme 123r sanfey markwell mellinger dunnington e132 e138 otles solano solano e72 e77 bok favier schuwirth lwt cpm cpm schuwirth lwt driessen emyway emyway seniorities ohn 20mb','Abstract Objective To present workplace‐based entrustable professional activities (EPAs) assessment data from the first 2 years of the EMYWAY platform in otolaryngology residency training in Taiwan. Study Design Two‐year cross‐sectional study. Setting Otolaryngology training programs. Methods In 2020, the Taiwan Society of Otorhinolaryngology–Head and Neck Surgery (TSO‐HNS) developed a workplace‐based assessment (WBA) framework with 11 EPAs, integrating milestones to evaluate resident competency. In 2021, TSO‐HNS piloted the EMYWAY platform for WBAs, which includes an EPA‐based assessment workflow, coaching feedback, and a dashboard displaying residents'' entrustment‐supervision levels. Data are analyzed annually for accreditation and curriculum enhancement. This study reports on the pilot year and the first full‐scale year of implementation. Results Eleven programs participated in the pilot year. Subsequently, 362 faculty members and 274 resident physicians from 34 programs nationwide engaged with EMYWAY. In the full‐scale year from August 2022 to July 2023, 9805 responses were recorded, primarily from surgical theaters (45.9%; 4502/9805) and third‐year residents (23.8%; 2331/9805). The most frequently evaluated EPAs were “head and neck” (17.5%; 1716/9805), “sinonasal” (13.5%; 1324/9805), and “ear” (12.2%; 1193/9805), with task complexity increasing with resident seniority ( P < .0001). A positive correlation was found between residents'' self‐assessments and faculty members'' ratings ( r = 0.531; P < .001). Over 98.2% of residents and 88.4% of faculty members provided substantial feedback (>10 words). Analysis of WBAs reported by training programs identified faculty development targets and teaching‐intensive tasks. Conclusion EMYWAY effectively documents workplace learning and tracks resident competency progression. Continuous improvement of WBA quality is essential for advancing the competency‐based medical education ecosystem. Keywords: competency‐based medical education, EMYWAY platform, entrustable professional activities, milestones, otolaryngology residency training, workplace‐based assessment The landscape of medical education is evolving, with an increasing emphasis on competency‐based medical education (CBME) that prioritizes the assessment of residents'' clinical skills in real‐world settings. 1 , 2 , 3 CBME aims to cultivate core competencies among healthcare professions that align with the expectations of local communities and meet the diverse needs of the population. 4 , 5 , 6 In the field of otolaryngology–head and neck surgery, the demand for effective training methodologies is especially critical due to the complexity of procedures and the vital importance of patient care. 7 , 8 Entrustable Professional Activities (EPAs) have emerged as a key framework within CBME, 5 offering a structured approach to evaluate residents’ readiness for unsupervised practice. 9 , 10 EPAs encapsulate the essential tasks that residents must perform independently, ensuring educational outcomes are directly linked to clinical responsibilities. 11 In CBME, both Milestones and EPAs frameworks serve complementary roles. 12 , 13 , 14 , 15 Milestones, as exemplified by the Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education (ACGME) Milestones Project, track incremental progress across specific competency domains—such as patient care (PC), medical knowledge (MK), system‐based practice (SBP), practice‐based learning and improvement (PBLI), professionalism, and interpersonal communication skills (ICS). 16 These domains guide learners from novice to expert through formative feedback and continuous development. 17 EPAs, in contrast, assess a learner''s readiness to perform clinical tasks independently, integrating multiple competencies for summative assessment. 18 , 19 Milestones facilitate the development of competencies necessary for EPA achievement, while EPAs focus on task‐specific performance and entrustment in clinical practice. 20 Together, they ensure comprehensive learner development in CBME. 21 Workplace‐based assessments (WBAs) are crucial in the EPA framework, fostering continuous feedback and learning in clinical environments. 22 They enhance residents'' education and ensure competency for high‐quality patient care. 23 However, their implementation in otolaryngology residency programs remains inconsistent despite clear benefits. 24 , 25 , 26 , 27 This study evaluates the EMYWAY platform, a digital tool designed to streamline assessments for otolaryngology residents. By using real‐time data and comprehensive evaluation methods, EMYWAY facilitates multiple assessments, provides instant feedback, and optimizes training. We hypothesized that EMYWAY would improve the assessment process and enhance resident learning outcomes. This study focuses on the platform''s impact on otolaryngology training and its potential to promote data‐driven evaluations. 28 , 29 , 30 Analyzing the performance of residents and educators on the platform provided insights into the practical applications of WBAs and their future role in residency training. Methods In August 2020, the Taiwan Society of Otorhinolaryngology–Head and Neck Surgery (TSO‐HNS) formed a work group comprising members of the residency review committee (RRC), program directors, specialty experts, and physician educators to develop an EPA framework for otolaryngology, aligned with the core competencies of resident training. Employing an iterative process based on Glaser''s modified state‐of‐the‐art approach, 31 the group reached consensus on the definitions of the 5 entrustment‐supervision levels, resulting in an initial framework comprising 11 EPA titles. The draft was subsequently subjected to both internal and external review through quantitative and qualitative methodologies. After further refinement, the milestone‐based EPA framework was finalized in June 2021. 32 Established in 1999, the Joint Commission of Taiwan (JCT) is a well‐regarded nongovernmental organization dedicated to continuous improvement of healthcare quality in Taiwan. In 2021, the JCT collaborated with the information technology team at Dalin Tzu Chi Hospital to develop EMYWAY, a CBME‐based platform designed to enhance workplace teaching and learning for healthcare professionals through an EPA‐based assessment workflow. The platform facilitates timely feedback and provides ongoing background analysis, fostering continuous improvement in outcome‐based education. 33 Supplemental Figure S1, available online depicts the architecture of the EMYWAY platform. The platform''s front end comprises various modules, including a study calendar, learning records, surgical log, ad hoc EPA assessments, and a summative entrustment decision module for extended practice observations. The backend supports data analysis, report generation, module configuration, system updates, and administrative functions for both individual training programs and the professional society. Program directors can leverage the platform to track, organize, and analyze data, enabling continuous feedback, validation, and improvement, thereby optimizing their residency training programs. In July 2021, the TSO‐HNS CBME workgroup collaborated with the JCT to integrate 11 otolaryngology EPAs into the EMYWAY. Each WBA included both a resident self‐rating and a faculty member rating, using a 1 to 10 Likert scale. To evaluate autonomy, the platform employs a 5‐level entrustment‐supervision scale for workplace assessments: level 1 (allowed to observe without active participation), level 2 (permitted to perform the EPA under direct and proactive supervision), level 3 (permitted to perform the EPA with indirect and reactive supervision), level 4 (authorized to work independently), and level 5 (qualified to supervise junior learners). 5 , 34 , 35 Both registered residents and faculty members on the EMYWAY can initiate an EPA assessment through a streamlined process. To begin an assessment, a resident logs into the platform (Supplemental Figure S2, available online ), follows the steps ( Figure 1 ), and submits the assessment. The platform''s back end automatically deidentifies the patient''s information and sends an email to the designated faculty member. The faculty member can then click the email link to review the resident''s submission, assess task complexity, provide overall ratings, and deliver feedback via text or voice input ( Figure 1 ). This process allows faculty members to complete an EPA workplace assessment in under 2 minutes, enabling the collection of multiple observations for comprehensive evaluation. These formative assessments serve as reference points for summative entrustment decisions during Clinical Competency Committee (CCC) reviews. 36 , 37 Similarly, faculty members can initiate an EPA, follow the same steps, and submit the form, requesting the resident to complete a self‐evaluation and reflection. Figure 1. Open in a new tab (A) Steps of the resident''s workplace‐based entrustable professional activities entries. (B) Steps of the faculty member''s response. In August 2021, the TSO‐HNS, in collaboration with the JCT, invited training programs to voluntarily participate in the pilot phase of the EMYWAY platform. During this test phase, residents of varying seniority levels and faculty members from the participating programs were involved. Participants utilized the platform''s comprehensive features, including the study calendar for scheduling academic activities, EPA‐based short practice observation assessments, the surgical log, and a real‐time achievement dashboard. The dashboard enabled the comparison of each resident''s progress with the average progress of peers at the same seniority level. By August 2022, TSO‐HNS announced the full‐scale implementation of the platform. Data have been collected and analyzed annually to evaluate residents’ learning progress, training program performance, and the accreditation process. Data analysis was approved by the Institutional Review Board at Cardinal Tien Hospital (CTH‐112‐2‐1‐002). All participants provided electronic informed consent prior to their first access to the platform. Statistical analyses were performed using SPSS Statistics version 26 (IBM). Descriptive statistics were generated, and the distributions of categorical and continuous variables were evaluated using the chi‐square or Fisher''s exact test, and Student''s t ‐test, respectively. For multiple comparisons, one‐way analysis of variance (ANOVA) was applied. Spearman''s correlation coefficient was calculated to assess relationships between specific variables. A significance level of α = 0.05 was established for all analyses. Results Table 1 outlines the TSO‐HNS EPA framework implemented on the EMYWAY platform, which consists of 11 EPA titles. These titles are mapped to specific clinical settings. Contexts marked with 2 stars indicate that faculty members must observe learners in these settings before granting them autonomy in performing the EPA task. Table 2 presents the integrated blueprint for residency training, aligning the 11 EPA titles with the 21 subcompetencies of the TSO‐HNS Milestones. Subcompetencies marked with 2 stars signify that these core competencies and subcompetencies are essential for workplace‐based EPA assessments. Table 1. Framework of the Taiwan Society of Otorhinolaryngology–Head and Neck Surgery Entrustable Professional Activities Assessment for Resident Training EPA acronym EPA title Key observation contexts or workplaces Outpatient clinic Ward Emergency department Operating room Consult EPA1 (Airway) Assessing and managing patients with airway presentations ✪ ✪ ✪✪ ✪ ✪✪ EPA2 (FB) Assessing and managing patients with suspicious foreign body presentations ✪✪ ✪ ✪✪ ✪ ✪ EPA3 (Bleeding) Assessing and managing patients with upper aerodigestive tract bleeding presentations ✪ ✪ ✪✪ ✪ ✪✪ EPA4 (Vertigo) Assessing and managing patients with vertigo ✪✪ ✪ ✪ ✪ ✪ EPA5 (Infection) Assessing and managing patients with head and neck infections ✪✪ ✪✪ ✪✪ ✪ ✪ EPA6 (H&N) Assessing and managing patients with head and neck masses ✪✪ ✪✪ ✪ ✪✪ ✪ EPA7 (Ear) Assessing and managing patients with ear and hearing diseases ✪✪ ✪ ✪ ✪✪ ✪ EPA8 (Sinonasal) Assessing and managing patients with sinonasal diseases ✪✪ ✪ ✪ ✪✪ ✪ EPA9 (Larynx) Assessing and managing patients with laryngopharyngeal diseases (voice/speech/language/dysphagia) ✪✪ ✪ ✪ ✪✪ ✪ EPA10 (SDB) Assessing and managing patients with sleep‐disordered breathing ✪✪ ✪ ✪ ✪✪ ✪ EPA11 (Plasty) Assessing and managing patients with facial plastic and reconstructive surgery ✪✪ ✪ ✪ ✪✪ ✪ Open in a new tab Abbreviations: FB, foreign body; H&N, head and neck; SDB, sleep‐disordered breathing. ✪✪ Faculty members MUST observe learners in these workplaces or contexts before awarding them independence in the EPA task (mandatory). ✪ Faculty members MAY observe learners in these workplaces or contexts for entrustment with independence in the EPA task (optional). Table 2. Blueprint of Taiwan Society of Otorhinolaryngology–Head and Neck Surgery Integrated Milestones and Entrustment Professional Activities for Resident Training Milestones 21 sub‐competencies Code Entrustable professional activities (EPAs) EPA1 EPA2 EPA3 EPA4 EPA5 EPA6 EPA7 EPA8 EPA9 EPA10 EPA11 Airway FB Bleeding Vertigo Infection H&N Ear Sinonasal Larynx SDB Plasty Salivary Disease PC1 ✪✪ ✪✪ Aerodigestive Tract Lesions PC2 ✪✪ ✪ ✪✪ ✪✪ ✪ ✪ ✪✪ Sleep‐Disordered Breathing PC3 ✪ ✪✪ Rhinosinusitis PC5 ✪ ✪ ✪✪ ✪ ✪✪ ✪ Nasal Deformity PC6 ✪ ✪✪ ✪✪ Chronic Ear Disorders PC7 ✪ ✪✪ Pediatric Otitis Media PC8 ✪ ✪✪ Neck Mass PCA1 ✪✪ ✪✪ Upper Aerodigestive Tract (UADT) Malignancies MK1 ✪✪ ✪ ✪✪ ✪✪ ✪✪ ✪ ✪✪ Hearing Loss MK2 ✪ ✪✪ Dysphagia and Dysphonia MK3 ✪✪ ✪✪ ✪✪ ✪ ✪✪ Inhalant Allergy MK4 ✪ ✪✪ ✪✪ Dizziness MKA1 ✪✪ ✪ Patient Safety SBP1 ✪✪ ✪✪ ✪✪ ✪✪ ✪✪ ✪✪ ✪✪ ✪✪ ✪✪ ✪✪ ✪✪ Resource Utilization SBP2 ✪ ✪ ✪ ✪✪ ✪ ✪✪ ✪ ✪ ✪ ✪✪ ✪ Life‐Long Learning and Constant Self‐evaluation PBLI1 ✪ ✪ ✪ ✪ ✪ ✪ ✪ ✪ ✪ ✪ ✪ Self‐Directed Learning PBLIN2 ✪ ✪ ✪ ✪ ✪ ✪ ✪ ✪ ✪ ✪ ✪ Professionalism PROF ✪✪ ✪✪ ✪✪ ✪✪ ✪✪ ✪✪ ✪✪ ✪✪ ✪✪ ✪✪ ✪✪ Interpersonal Communication Skills ICS1 ✪✪ ✪✪ ✪✪ ✪✪ ✪✪ ✪✪ ✪✪ ✪✪ ✪✪ ✪✪ ✪✪ Health Care Team Communications ICSN1 ✪✪ ✪ ✪ ✪✪ ✪ ✪✪ ✪ ✪ ✪ ✪✪ ✪ Patient‐ and Family‐Centered Care ICSN2 ✪✪ ✪✪ ✪✪ ✪✪ ✪✪ ✪✪ ✪✪ ✪✪ ✪✪ ✪✪ ✪✪ Open in a new tab Abbreviations: ICS, interpersonal communication skills; MK, medical knowledge; PBLI, practice‐based learning and improvement; PC, patient care; PROF, professionalism; SBP, system‐based practice. ✪✪ These core competencies and subcompetencies are IMPORTANT for this entrustable professional activity. ✪ These core competencies and subcompetencies are USEFUL for this entrustable professional activity. This study reports the results from the first 2 years of the platform''s implementation. Table 3 presents the characteristics of the study population. During the pilot phase, from August 2021 to July 2022, 11 programs participated, with 84.1% (90/107) of residents and 70.9% (105/148) of faculty members from these programs submitting at least one EPA assessment, resulting in a total of 1210 submissions. In the subsequent period, from August 2022 to July 2023, all 34 programs nationwide adopted EMYWAY. During this time, 100% of the 274 residents and 87.9% (362/412) of faculty members submitted at least one EPA, generating a total of 9805 assessments. Table 3. Characteristics of the TSO‐HNS Training Programs and the Participants Pilot year (2021‐2022) P value Full‐scale year (2022‐2023) P value No. of training program 11 34 Study population Residents 90 274 Faculty members 105 362 Grading of training program Medical center 8 (772) a .606 b 19 (7398) a .115 b Quasi‐medical center 1 (104) a 3 (442) a Regional teaching hospital 2 (334) a 12 (1965) a Location of training program Northern region 4 (746) a .785 b 16 (5389) a .985 b Central region 3 (124) a 7 (1857) a Southern region 2 (124) a 9 (2131) a Eastern region 2 (216) a 2 (428) a No. of submitted EPAs 1210 9805 Open in a new tab Abbreviations: EPAs, Entrustable Professional Activities; TSO‐HNS, Taiwan Society of Otorhinolaryngology–Head and Neck Surgery. a The number of completed EPAs is shown in the parenthesis. b Kruskal‐Wallis test. Figure 2 displays the distribution of EPA titles over the first 2 years. During both the pilot and full‐scale phases, the 3 most frequently evaluated EPAs were “head and neck” (19.2%; 231/1210 and 17.5%; 1716/9805, respectively), “sinonasal” (13.7%; 165/1210 and 13.5%; 1324/9805, respectively), and “ear” (12.8%; 154/1210 and 12.2%; 1193/9805, respectively). The majority of assessments were conducted in surgical theaters (62.5%; 756/1210 and 45.9%; 4502/9805) and wards (13.1%; 158/1210 and 17.8%; 1750/9805) ( Figure 3 ). Third‐year residents submitted the highest number of EPAs (31.4%; 380/1210 and 23.8%; 2331/9805), followed by fourth‐year residents (30.8%; 373/1210 and 22.1%; 2158/9805) (Supplemental Figure S3 , available online). A trend of increasing task complexity, reflected by a higher proportion of advanced EPA tasks, was observed with increased resident seniority during both the pilot and full‐scale phases (Chi‐square test, P < .0001) ( Figure 4 ). Additionally, a positive correlation was identified between resident self‐assessments and faculty member ratings in both phases ( r = 0.578, P < .001 and r = 0.531, P < .001, respectively) (Supplemental Figure S4 , available online). Figure 2. Open in a new tab Percentage of entrustable professional activities (EPAs) submitted in the pilot year and full‐scale year by EPA titles. Figure 3. Open in a new tab Percentage of entrustable professional activities (EPAs) submitted in the pilot year and full‐scale year by workplace. Figure 4. Open in a new tab Proportions of advanced EPA tasks by resident physician''s seniority during (A) the pilot year and (B) the full‐scale year. In the pilot year, 17.8% (215/1210) of EPAs received a faculty member''s response on the same day, and 34.1% (413/1210) within 1 to 7 days. Compared to the pilot year, the faculty members'' response time decreased significantly during the full‐scale year (Chi‐square test, P < .0001) ( Figure 5 ). Figure 6 illustrates the percentage of EPAs containing resident reflections or faculty member feedback with a word count of 10 or more. The proportion of EPAs with resident reflections of ≥10 words increased from 93.2% (1128/1210) to 98.2% (9626/9805) (Fisher''s exact test, P < .0001) between the pilot and full‐scale years. Similarly, the percentage of faculty member feedback with ≥10 words rose significantly from 68.7% (831/1210) to 92.7% (9090/9805) (Fisher''s exact test, P < .0001). Figure 7 shows the correlation between entrustment‐supervision levels and seniority of the residents in the pilot year and the full‐scale year. Supplemental Figure S5, available online presents a representative dashboard of a resident, showing multiple data points related to entrustment‐supervision levels. This dashboard is used by the CCC to facilitate summative entrustment decisions and identify areas for remediation. Supplemental Figure S6, available online depicts ad hoc entrustment‐supervision levels distribution in EPA assessments during the pilot year and the full‐scale year. Additionally, analysis of entrustment‐supervision levels by faculty members, program directors, society, and EPA titles assists in identifying priorities for faculty development, program enhancement, and teaching‐intensive tasks. Figure 5. Open in a new tab Percentage of entrustable professional activities (EPAs) submitted in the pilot year and full‐scale year by faculty member response time. Figure 6. Open in a new tab Percentage of entrustable professional activities (EPAs) with a resident reflection or faculty member feedback with a word count of 10 or more words. Figure 7. Open in a new tab Distribution of ad hoc entrustment‐supervision levels in the submitted EPA assessments by seniority of resident physicians during (A) the pilot year and (B) the full‐scale year. Discussion The 2‐year implementation of the EMYWAY platform in otolaryngology residency training provided valuable insights into WBA effectiveness. Nationwide participation from 34 programs led to 9805 EPAs submitted. A positive correlation between resident self‐ratings and faculty member evaluations indicated alignment in perceived competencies. Faculty feedback response time also improved significantly in the full‐scale implementation year. Additionally, the data showed increasing task complexity with resident seniority, demonstrating the platform''s ability to support structured, competency‐based training. Since 2017, our society has used Milestones as the foundational framework for residency training. 38 The transition from Milestones to EPAs marks a significant shift in the programmatic assessment approach within CBME. 32 The introduction of EPAs shifts the focus from abstract competencies to specific, observable tasks that residents must be entrusted to perform independently. 25 This change emphasizes practical skills in real‐world settings and enables more direct assessment of readiness for unsupervised practice. 39 By integrating EPAs into the assessment process, training programs can promote continuous feedback and targeted evaluations, aligning educational outcomes with clinical responsibilities. 40 , 41 Additionally, mapping subcompetencies to EPAs in the training blueprint allows frontline clinical instructors to provide formative feedback during direct observation. 42 , 43 This structured and transparent assessment framework ensures that residents are adequately prepared to meet patient care demands. 44 In this study, WBAs adopting EPAs framework provide assessments in actual clinical settings, allowing for the evaluation of residents'' competencies in real‐time situations. With the help of technology, 24 , 45 , 46 immediate and structured feedback helps residents identify areas for improvement and fosters a culture of learning, ultimately leading to better educational outcomes. 47 , 48 The platform''s capacity to collect and analyze data allows for comprehensive evaluations of resident performance over time. This data‐driven approach can inform curriculum enhancements 2 , 7 , 44 , 49 and faculty development, 50 , 51 ensuring that training programs remain responsive to the needs of both residents and the healthcare system. 23 The high participation rates among residents (84.1% in the pilot year and 100% in the full‐scale year) and faculty members (70.9% and 87.9%, respectively) suggest that EMYWAY‐assisted WBAs can increase engagement among both learners and faculty members. 52 This engagement can lead to a more invested learning environment. 52 , 53 Although WBAs align well with the principles of CBME, several challenges warrant attention. One concern is the subjectivity and halo effect that can influence how faculty members assess resident''s performance, potentially leading to inconsistent ratings that may not accurately reflect residents’ true capabilities. 54 Moreover, while platforms like EMYWAY are designed to streamline the evaluation process, the requirement for frequent assessments and feedback could increase the workload for faculty members, detracting from their primary responsibilities of teaching and mentoring. 55 , 56 , 57 , 58 Additionally, WBAs may create undue pressure on residents to perform consistently at high levels, potentially contributing to anxiety and burnout. 59 This pressure might detract from the learning experience and hinder the cultivation of a growth mindset among trainees. 60 The increasing use of digital platforms for assessment, such as EMYWAY, also raises concerns about over‐reliance on technology, which could risk depersonalizing the educational experience and diminishing the importance of mentor‐mentee interactions that are essential for effective learning. 61 , 62 Furthermore, the effectiveness of WBAs can vary significantly across different training programs and specialties. 63 Standardizing assessments and ensuring consistent quality across diverse clinical environments can pose significant challenges for training programs. 64 As a result, while WBAs offer considerable benefits in enhancing postgraduate training, these challenges must be carefully managed to ensure that the full potential of WBAs is realized without compromising the educational experience. 65 During both the pilot and full‐scale implementation phases, the top 3 assessed EPAs were “head and neck,” “sinonasal,” and “ear.” The high volume of assessments in these areas can be attributed to several factors: the critical nature of head and neck procedures, the prevalence of sinonasal disorders in local clinical practice, and the complexity of ear‐related conditions. These frequently evaluated professional activities not only reflect the most common clinical scenarios residents encounter, but also underscore their significance in otolaryngology training. 66 , 67 By prioritizing these EPAs, residency programs can enhance the overall quality of training, ensuring that residents are well‐prepared for independent practice. 67 The observation that 3rd and 4th‐year residents submitted the greatest number of EPAs, with the majority recorded in the OR, can be explained by several factors. These include their increased participation in complex procedures and patient care, a greater emphasis on surgical training during the later years of residency, and heightened responsibilities. Additionally, residents may feel more motivated to submit EPAs in order to receive feedback on their performance, particularly in high‐stakes environments like the OR, where their skills are directly observed and closely linked to patient outcomes. 68 The overlapping entrustment‐supervision level plots for the first 3 years of training suggest that learners are achieving similar foundational competencies in otolaryngology. This may reflect a well‐structured curriculum focused on essential skills necessary for more complex tasks. However, it is important to recognize the variability in individual learner competencies. The overlap does not imply uniformity in performance but highlights the need for tailored educational strategies to support each resident''s unique learning trajectory. High‐quality feedback is paramount in WBAs. The belief that a word count of 10 or more signifies high‐quality feedback is a common myth in educational and professional settings. While a longer feedback response may suggest that the evaluator has invested time in providing a detailed assessment, it does not inherently guarantee the quality or effectiveness of the feedback. High‐quality feedback is characterized by its relevance, specificity, constructiveness, and the inclusion of an actionable plan, rather than its length. 47 , 69 A concise response that directly addresses the strengths and areas for improvement can be more valuable than a lengthy comment that lacks focus or clarity. Additionally, timely feedback is essential for WBAs. While the proportion of same‐day feedback from faculty members increased significantly during the full‐scale year compared to the pilot, delayed feedback remains substantial. Studies indicate that feedback given more than 3 days postobservation lacks clarity and detail, particularly for those operative performance ratings, underscoring the need to discourage such delays. 70 Additional studies are warranted to explore the factors associated with faculty feedback delay. Furthermore, the context in which feedback is delivered plays a crucial role in its quality. Feedback should be tailored to the individual''s performance and the specific situation, rather than being generic or formulaic. Given the substantial amount of narrative feedback collected through the platform, future investigations should incorporate artificial intelligence or natural language processing techniques to assist in evaluating the quality of feedback. 71 , 72 While WBAs with the EPAs framework are a valuable tool for assessing resident competencies through short practice observation, they represent only one aspect of a comprehensive assessment strategy. To fully evaluate and support the development of resident competencies, it is essential to incorporate long practice assessments, such as multi‐source feedback, entrustment‐based discussions, product collection—such as portfolios, case reports, and procedural logs—and self‐reflection. 40 , 49 , 73 , 74 , 75 This multifaceted approach ensures that all dimensions of a resident''s training are addressed, ultimately leading to the cultivation of well‐rounded, competent healthcare professionals. 73 , 74 , 75 The study''s strengths include comprehensive data from a nationwide cohort of residents and faculty members across multiple otolaryngology programs. Its focus on the EMYWAY platform, integrating WBAs with the EPA framework, allows for detailed insights into effective real‐world implementation. The positive correlation between resident self‐assessments and faculty member ratings reinforces the assessment''s validity and highlights the platform''s role in fostering feedback and continuous improvement. However, this study has several limitations that warrant consideration. A key limitation is its emphasis on short‐term practice observations through the EPA framework, which may overlook essential components of a comprehensive assessment strategy, such as extended practice assessments and entrustment‐based discussions. This narrow focus could restrict the generalizability of the findings to broader contexts in medical education. Additionally, utilizing these general EPAs instead of more discrete procedural ones had advantages and disadvantages. General EPAs aim to streamline the number of EPAs within the framework, focusing on their relevance to the broad skill set needed in otolaryngology residency training. However, it''s essential to consider potential limitations in specificity when interpreting the data. Furthermore, the study''s reliance on quantitative metrics, such as the word count of feedback provided, risks neglecting the qualitative aspects vital for fostering effective learning. The uncertainty surrounding the quality of feedback may impede the creation of a supportive learning environment. To mitigate this issue, future research should incorporate qualitative evaluations of feedback to ensure a more nuanced understanding of its impact. Additionally, the effectiveness of the EMYWAY platform might vary across different training programs, and the study does not address potential variations in platform usage across institutions. Future research should extend the study duration, include subgroup and factor analyses, and gather feedback from both residents and instructors regarding time and resource constraints. These measures would provide a more comprehensive evaluation of the EMYWAY platform''s impact on resident training and competency development. Conclusions This study demonstrates the potential of the EMYWAY platform to enhance WBA in otolaryngology residency training. While promising, further research is needed to explore feedback quality, implementation challenges, and its impact on healthcare outcomes. As medical education shifts to an outcome‐based model, assessing the platform''s effectiveness in promoting resident growth and patient care is crucial. Future studies should focus on longitudinal evaluations and integrating robust feedback mechanisms. Author Contributions Fang‐Cen Guo : Acquisition analysis, and interpretation of data; drafting of the manuscript. Yu‐Ting Chen : Acquisition, analysis, and interpretation of data; drafting of the manuscript. Wei‐Chung Hsu :